internal val videoCatalogPart1: List<String> = listOf(
    ",{\"vid\":\"AJ-auWfJTts\",\"title\":\"Classic Game Postmortem: Sid Meier's Civilization\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"A 2017 GDC postmortem in which Sid Meier and Bruce Shelley tell how Civilization came together from board game design and sim game experience. They cover its development history and design decisions, including fairness and luck.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a great account of how Civilization was made and as a source on perceived fairness in game design.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"game design history\",\"categories\":[\"Game design\",\"Game history & retro gaming\"],\"tags\":[\"civilization\",\"sid meier\",\"gdc\",\"postmortem\",\"strategy games\"],\"quote\":\"Check out this great GDC talk by Sid and Bruce on the history of the development of the game.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"Agu6jipKfYw\",\"title\":\"\\\"Controlling Time and Space: understanding the many formulations of FRP\\\" by Evan Czaplicki\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Evan Czaplicki's Strange Loop 2014 talk surveys the many formulations of functional reactive programming and how they relate historically and theoretically. It then shows how Elm's variant enables hot-swapping and its time-traveling debugger.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people point to it as the clearest explanation of what FRP actually means and how its flavors differ.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"functional reactive programming\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Web development\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"frp\",\"elm\",\"time-traveling debugger\",\"reactive programming\",\"strange loop\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend Evan's Strange Loop talk\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"BxQw4CdxLr8\",\"title\":\"Stanford Lecture: Don Knuth—\\\"A Conjecture That Had To Be True\\\" (2017)\",\"channel\":\"Stanford Online\",\"summary\":\"Donald Knuth's 2017 Christmas Tree Lecture at Stanford about a combinatorial geometry problem whose computed numbers matched a striking formula. He tells the story of the conjecture and its sequel.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN fans love it for Knuth's humor and for how he makes open math problems feel approachable, and it is often cited for details like dancing links and counting rectangle subdivisions.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"combinatorial geometry\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Algorithms & data structures\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"donald knuth\",\"combinatorics\",\"christmas lecture\",\"dancing links\",\"conjecture\"],\"quote\":\"it's always a pleasure for me to see Knuth's talks or read his work\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"C2RO34b_oPM\",\"title\":\"Taking Vector Transposes Seriously | Jiahao Chen | JuliaCon 2017\",\"channel\":\"The Julia Programming Language\",\"summary\":\"Jiahao Chen's JuliaCon 2017 talk argues that vector transposes should be taken seriously in type systems and linear algebra libraries. It explains why treating vectors as matrices causes trouble in languages.\",\"why_hn\":\"Strongly recommended for anyone working with vectors for its clear take on how they should be treated in types.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"vector transposes in type systems\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"julia\",\"linear algebra\",\"type systems\",\"vectors\",\"juliacon\"],\"quote\":\"but seriously take the time to watch this, it's fantastic\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"DgVS-zXgMTk\",\"title\":\"Pete Hunt: React - Rethinking Best Practices (updated) - JSConf.Asia 2013\",\"channel\":\"JSConf\",\"summary\":\"Pete Hunt of Facebook and Instagram presents React at JSConf.Asia 2013, explaining its design principles and why they seemed shocking at first. He argues that React separates concerns rather than technologies, and the talk also announces the React Chrome developer tools.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters credit it with persuading them to adopt React, and cite it for the argument that HTML and JS cannot truly be separated.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"react design philosophy\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"react\",\"pete hunt\",\"jsx\",\"separation of concerns\",\"javascript\"],\"quote\":\"Pete Hunt did a great job then, explaining how the false separation of concerns between HTML and JS really is unavoidable.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"DmT80OseAGs\",\"title\":\"A Sudoku Solver in APL\",\"channel\":\"Dyalog\",\"summary\":\"A Dyalog demonstration of developing a Sudoku solver in APL, using an algorithm by Arthur Whitney with performance work by John Scholes. The narration steps through the array-oriented solution.\",\"why_hn\":\"Strongly recommended as a beautiful demonstration that APL can be read and reasoned about step by step.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"array programming\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Algorithms & data structures\"],\"tags\":[\"apl\",\"sudoku\",\"dyalog\",\"john scholes\",\"functional programming\"],\"quote\":\"I've always liked this video, where one of the Dyalog developers effortlessly writes a Sudoku solver in APL\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"ELeZAKCN4tY\",\"title\":\"Can C++ be 10x Simpler & Safer?  - Herb Sutter - CppCon 2022\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Herb Sutter's CppCon 2022 talk on whether C++ can become ten times simpler and safer. He introduces his experimental Cpp2 syntax and the cppfront transpiler, explaining the limits on incremental improvement.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the main introduction to Cpp2 and cppfront, and as a credible effort to evolve C++.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"c++ language evolution\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"c++\",\"cpp2\",\"cppfront\",\"herb sutter\",\"memory safety\",\"cppcon\"],\"quote\":\"One of those few talks that left me genuinely enthused about the topic.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"Gatn5ameRr8\",\"title\":\"\\\"Two incompatible sacred values in American universities\\\" Jon Haidt, Hayek Lecture Series\",\"channel\":\"Duke University Department of Political Science\",\"summary\":\"Jonathan Haidt's Hayek Lecture at Duke argues American universities pursue two potentially incompatible sacred values, truth and social justice. He discusses campus conflict, honor, dignity and victimhood cultures, and viewpoint diversity.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it heavily in diversity and academia debates as an intellectual analysis of the truth versus social justice conflict.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"universities truth vs social justice\",\"categories\":[\"Social issues & culture\",\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\"],\"tags\":[\"jonathan haidt\",\"campus politics\",\"moral psychology\",\"viewpoint diversity\",\"academic freedom\"],\"quote\":\"This is the best intellectual analysis of the situation I've come across so far\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"HJ-719EGIts\",\"title\":\"Advanced Topics in Programming Languages: A Lock-Free Hash Table\",\"channel\":\"GoogleTalksArchive\",\"summary\":\"A 2007 Google Tech Talk in which Cliff Click of Azul Systems presents a lock-free concurrent hash table that scales to 768 CPUs. He explains why it is correct by reasoning in a different way than usual happens-before and memory-order arguments.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN regards it as one of the best technical talks, a deep and clear walkthrough of scalable lock-free hash tables built on compare-and-swap.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"lock-free data structures\",\"categories\":[\"Algorithms & data structures\",\"Software performance & optimization\",\"Distributed systems\"],\"tags\":[\"lock-free\",\"hash table\",\"concurrency\",\"cliff click\",\"compare-and-swap\",\"java\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend this talk by Cliff Click about building highly scalable hash tables via Compare And Swap\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"Hhy7JUinlu0\",\"title\":\"Margin Call (2011) - Senior Partners Emergency Meeting [HD 1080p] (Re-Upload / Audio Fixed)\",\"channel\":\"Extractor\",\"summary\":\"A clip from the 2011 film Margin Call in which senior partners hold an emergency meeting and decide to sell off toxic assets immediately. It is set at the start of the 2008 financial crisis.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites the scene as a model of clear, decisive communication and of leaders acting fast to survive, and recommends the film as the best take on 2008.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"margin call movie scene\",\"categories\":[\"Film & TV clips\",\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\",\"Investing & personal finance\"],\"tags\":[\"margin call\",\"financial crisis\",\"film clip\",\"leadership\",\"communication\"],\"quote\":\"There is a very fine example of this in cinema\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"IAF8DjrQSSk\",\"title\":\"Interactive notebooks ~ Pluto.jl | Fons van der Plas | JuliaCon 2020\",\"channel\":\"The Julia Programming Language\",\"summary\":\"Fons van der Plas introduces Pluto.jl, a reactive notebook for Julia that tracks references between cells and re-runs affected cells when code changes. The talk was given at JuliaCon 2020.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an enthusiastic demo of spreadsheet-like reactive notebooks, which commenters think new notebook projects should copy.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"reactive notebooks\",\"categories\":[\"Data science & statistics\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"pluto.jl\",\"julia\",\"notebooks\",\"reactive programming\",\"dependency graph\"],\"quote\":\"I think every new notebook project should take ideas from Julia's Pluto\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"IRTfhkiAqPw\",\"title\":\"Object-Oriented Programming is Embarrassing: 4 Short Examples\",\"channel\":\"Brian Will\",\"summary\":\"Brian Will follows up his earlier anti-OOP video by walking through four short code examples, including Java code in the style of Uncle Bob, to show how object-oriented design complicates simple programs. It argues that OOP hides and scatters state and adds needless indirection.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as a sharp, example-driven critique of OOP and Clean Code style, often alongside Brian Will's other anti-OO videos.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"object-oriented programming critique\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"oop\",\"code examples\",\"state\",\"clean code\",\"software design\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend \\\"Object Oriented Programming is Bad\\\" and \\\"Object Oriented Programming is Embarrassing\\\" on Youtube.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"JLb5hZLw44s\",\"title\":\"Bret Weinstein on \\\"The Portal\\\" (w/ host Eric Weinstein), Ep. #019 - The Prediction and the DISC.\",\"channel\":\"Eric Weinstein\",\"summary\":\"A long Portal podcast episode in which Bret Weinstein tells Eric Weinstein the story of his lab mice telomere work, the prediction behind it, and what it means for science and aging.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the full account of the lab mice issue and evolutionary tradeoffs between cancer and longevity, despite its length.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"lab mice telomeres\",\"categories\":[\"Evolution & genetics\",\"Biology & biotechnology\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"telomeres\",\"lab mice\",\"evolutionary biology\",\"scientific institutions\",\"aging\"],\"quote\":\"an evolutionary biologist goes into depth about telomeres, evolution and how cancer and longevity are two sides of the same coin\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"JxBg4sMusIg\",\"title\":\"You Suck at Excel - Joel Spolsky (Reupload)\",\"channel\":\"Cool Bug Facts\",\"summary\":\"A reupload of Joel Spolsky's talk teaching spreadsheet users the Excel features they are probably missing, such as keyboard navigation, named ranges, tables and lookups. The original disappeared and this copy comes from the Wayback Machine.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call it an internet classic that makes you a more proficient Excel user, and note it is still relevant despite predating newer functions like XLOOKUP.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"excel productivity\",\"categories\":[\"Productivity & self-improvement\",\"Data science & statistics\",\"Software (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"excel\",\"spreadsheets\",\"joel spolsky\",\"data tables\",\"keyboard shortcuts\"],\"quote\":\"will turn you into a more proficient user of Excel.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"KRcecxdGxvQ\",\"title\":\"Michael Stonebraker | Big Data is (at least) Four Different Problems\",\"channel\":\"Michigan Engineering\",\"summary\":\"Database pioneer Michael Stonebraker argues that 'big data' is really at least four different problems: big volumes with small analytics, big analytics, velocity, and variety. He comments on where each market is heading.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a thoughtful, first-principles take from a Turing award winner that cuts through NoSQL and big data hype.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"databases and big data\",\"categories\":[\"Databases & storage\",\"Data science & statistics\"],\"tags\":[\"michael stonebraker\",\"big data\",\"databases\",\"sql\",\"nosql\"],\"quote\":\"a rather interesting talk by a very thoughtful Turing award winning engineer named Michael Stonebraker\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"LxgMdjyw8uw\",\"title\":\"We WILL Fix Climate Change!\",\"channel\":\"Kurzgesagt – In a Nutshell\",\"summary\":\"Kurzgesagt's animated take on climate change argues that, despite alarming headlines, there are real reasons for hope as technology, policy and public attitudes shift. It surveys trends and solutions while acknowledging the scale of the problem.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as an articulate, uplifting counterweight to climate doomerism, though one critic calls it a form of denialism and another notes it omits nuclear.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"climate change outlook\",\"categories\":[\"Climate science & climate change\",\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\"],\"tags\":[\"climate change\",\"emissions\",\"tipping points\",\"kurzgesagt\",\"optimism\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend this video from the wonderful Kurzgesagt science video series\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"NH1Tta7purM\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2017: Carl Cook “When a Microsecond Is an Eternity: High Performance Trading Systems in C++”\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Optiver's Carl Cook explains how to build high-frequency trading systems in C++, where the critical path runs rarely but must be extremely fast. He covers techniques for low and predictable latency, including avoiding main memory and cache misses.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly points people to it as the best overview of low-latency trading engineering.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"low-latency trading systems\",\"categories\":[\"Software performance & optimization\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\"],\"tags\":[\"c++\",\"hft\",\"performance\",\"cache optimization\",\"cppcon\"],\"quote\":\"Here are some talks that will give you a small overview into that world\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"O133ppiVnWY\",\"title\":\"Arithmetic, Population and Energy -  a talk by Al Bartlett\",\"channel\":\"lXlSalemlXl\",\"summary\":\"Physicist Al Bartlett's well-known lecture on the arithmetic of exponential growth, applied to population, energy consumption and resource depletion. He argues that steady percentage growth cannot be sustained on a finite planet and that most public discussion ignores this.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it as a mind-changing introduction to exponential growth, though some note his peak oil predictions did not pan out.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"exponential growth and sustainability\",\"categories\":[\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\",\"Earth & energy (other)\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"exponential growth\",\"population\",\"energy\",\"peak oil\",\"sustainability\"],\"quote\":\"have totally changed the way I look at all political discourse\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"OjMPJVmXxV8\",\"title\":\"Canceling | ContraPoints\",\"channel\":\"ContraPoints\",\"summary\":\"A long video essay by Natalie Wynn of ContraPoints on cancel culture, drawing on her own experience of being pile-on targeted by other leftists. It is theatrical and discursive, mixing argument with performance.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a more thoughtful, balanced and personal take on cancel culture than usual discussion.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cancel culture\",\"categories\":[\"Social issues & culture\",\"Internet culture & memes\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"cancel culture\",\"social media\",\"twitter\",\"left politics\",\"video essay\"],\"quote\":\"I think that it’s useful to hear a balanced take on cancel culture\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"PQmSUHhP3ug\",\"title\":\"The 919 Tribute Tour: On-board record lap, Nordschleife.\",\"channel\":\"Porsche\",\"summary\":\"Onboard footage of Timo Bernhard driving the Porsche 919 Hybrid Evo to an all-time Nurburgring Nordschleife record lap of 5:19.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as the benchmark lap record and recommends it even to non-car fans for its sheer speed and precision.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"nurburgring lap record\",\"categories\":[\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\",\"Sports & athletics\"],\"tags\":[\"porsche 919\",\"nordschleife\",\"onboard\",\"motorsport\",\"timo bernhard\"],\"quote\":\"If anyone hasn't seen this, I highly recommend it, even if you're not a car fan.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"PfYYraMgiBA\",\"title\":\"Cracking Ancient Codes: Cuneiform Writing - with Irving Finkel\",\"channel\":\"The Royal Institution\",\"summary\":\"Irving Finkel of the British Museum lectures at the Royal Institution on the invention and decipherment of cuneiform writing in Mesopotamia, and why it is so hard to translate.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN users recommend it as a delightful, well-told account of how cuneiform developed and was decoded.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cuneiform decipherment\",\"categories\":[\"Language & linguistics\",\"World history\"],\"tags\":[\"cuneiform\",\"irving finkel\",\"mesopotamia\",\"writing systems\",\"ancient languages\"],\"quote\":\"Highly recommend Prof Irving Fink's talk to Royal Institution on the 3,000 year development of cuneiform\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"Q7jcBGLIpus\",\"title\":\"Explained: New Navy UFO Videos\",\"channel\":\"Mick West\",\"summary\":\"Mick West explains the three Navy UFO videos (FLIR, GIMBAL and GOFAST) released in 2020, giving mundane explanations such as a distant plane and camera artifacts. He notes the videos were not new and had been circulating since 2017.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a calm, evidence-based debunking whenever the Navy UFO videos come up.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"navy ufo video analysis\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"ufo\",\"uap\",\"debunking\",\"gimbal\",\"flir\"],\"quote\":\"Mick West has some great videos on it\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"R2Aa4PivG0g\",\"title\":\"\\\"I See What You Mean\\\" by Peter Alvaro\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Peter Alvaro's Strange Loop 2015 talk argues that query languages let you state what you mean, and applies that to distributed programming. It covers Datalog, Dedalus and the Bloom lineage for reasoning about distributed systems.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a fantastic introduction to declarative and Datalog-based distributed systems and failure modeling.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"declarative distributed programming\",\"categories\":[\"Distributed systems\",\"Databases & storage\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"datalog\",\"dedalus\",\"bloom\",\"query languages\",\"distributed systems\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend watching Peter Alvaro’s Strangeloop talk about creating Dedalus\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"RaI_TuISSJE\",\"title\":\"ORCA Sequencer Intro (Experimental Livecoding!)\",\"channel\":\"Allieway Audio\",\"summary\":\"Allieway Audio gives an informal introduction and live-coding demo of ORCA, a free esoteric programming environment for building procedural sequencers from Hundred Rabbits. It walks through the interface and how to make music with it.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call it the best video overview of Orca, the explanation that makes the unusual interface click.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"live coding music\",\"categories\":[\"Creative coding & generative art\",\"Music theory, synthesis & production\"],\"tags\":[\"orca\",\"livecoding\",\"sequencer\",\"generative music\",\"hundred rabbits\"],\"quote\":\"It is by far the best video overview of the system that I am aware of.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"SrDEtSlqJC4\",\"title\":\"The REAL Reason McDonalds Ice Cream Machines Are Always Broken\",\"channel\":\"Johnny Harris\",\"summary\":\"Johnny Harris investigates why McDonald's ice cream machines are so often broken, covering the Taylor machines, the repair restrictions on franchisees and the Kytch workaround device.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it in right-to-repair and McFlurry-machine threads as the go-to in-depth explainer of the franchisee and Taylor arrangement.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"right to repair and mcdonalds ice cream machines\",\"categories\":[\"Repair, teardowns & right to repair\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"right to repair\",\"mcdonalds\",\"taylor\",\"kytch\",\"franchisees\",\"mcbroken\"],\"quote\":\"This video did a good indepth investigation.\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"T0CpOYZZZW4\",\"title\":\"The Spectacular Failure of the Star Wars Hotel\",\"channel\":\"Jenny Nicholson\",\"summary\":\"Jenny Nicholson's roughly four-hour review of her two-night stay on Disney's Star Wars Galactic Starcruiser immersive hotel. She goes through the experience, its story and production, and why it failed.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people strongly recommend it as the best overview of the Galactic Starcruiser and a case study in misaligned incentives and poor guest experience, engaging despite its length.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"immersive theme park experiences\",\"categories\":[\"Tech company stories & documentaries\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\",\"Film analysis & criticism\"],\"tags\":[\"star wars\",\"disney\",\"galactic starcruiser\",\"immersive experience\",\"larp\",\"theme parks\"],\"quote\":\"here's as an incredible documentary covering the misaligned incentives and poor guest experience at the now-shuttered Disney Star Wars hotel.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"UEAMfLPZZhE\",\"title\":\"Distributed Systems 1.1: Introduction\",\"channel\":\"Martin Kleppmann\",\"summary\":\"The first lecture in Martin Kleppmann's 8-lecture distributed systems series from the University of Cambridge undergraduate course. It introduces the subject, and accompanying lecture notes are available.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN regularly recommends the series as a free, accessible, theory-oriented course on distributed computing that complements his book Designing Data-Intensive Applications.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"distributed systems\",\"categories\":[\"Distributed systems\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"distributed systems\",\"consensus\",\"causality\",\"university course\",\"kleppmann\"],\"quote\":\"the distributed system audio course on youtube is extremely accessible\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"WZskjLq040I\",\"title\":\"Tom Scott vs Irving Finkel: The Royal Game of Ur | PLAYTHROUGH | International Tabletop Day 2017\",\"channel\":\"The British Museum\",\"summary\":\"Tom Scott plays the Royal Game of Ur against British Museum curator Irving Finkel, who deciphered its rules from a cuneiform tablet. Finkel teaches the game and explains its history as the oldest playable board game.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in threads on ancient games and cuneiform as a charming match with an enthusiastic expert.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ancient board games\",\"categories\":[\"Chess, board games & puzzles\",\"World history\"],\"tags\":[\"royal game of ur\",\"irving finkel\",\"tom scott\",\"british museum\",\"mesopotamia\"],\"quote\":\"he also did a fantastic video on with Tom Scott\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"XEVlyP4_11M\",\"title\":\"Government Surveillance: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO)\",\"channel\":\"LastWeekTonight\",\"summary\":\"John Oliver interviews Edward Snowden about NSA surveillance, including street interviews showing people have not thought about what the government can see. The segment famously frames the issue through private photos.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the best way to explain to ordinary people why privacy and mass surveillance matter.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"government surveillance\",\"categories\":[\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"edward snowden\",\"nsa\",\"privacy\",\"john oliver\",\"mass surveillance\"],\"quote\":\"I believe John Oliver provided the best example when he interviewed Edward Snowden.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"XFDM1ip5HdU\",\"title\":\"What does it feel like to invent math?\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"3Blue1Brown explores infinite sums from convergent to divergent, with an introduction to the 2-adic metric, themed on the cycle between discovering and inventing mathematics.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a favorite Grant Sanderson video and a visual introduction to p-adic numbers and sums like 1+2+4+...\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"infinite sums and p-adic numbers\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"2-adic\",\"infinite series\",\"3blue1brown\",\"number theory\",\"math invention\"],\"quote\":\"Great intro\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"XGo4PJd1lng\",\"title\":\"How I Designed a Free Music Font for 5 Million Musicians (MuseScore 3.6)\",\"channel\":\"Tantacrul\",\"summary\":\"Tantacrul explains how he and engraver Simon Smith designed the free music notation font for MuseScore 3.6. It draws on the history of the SCORE notation application by Leland Smith.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an entertaining look at how hard notation fonts are to make, and it comes up in threads on music engraving and open source.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"music notation fonts\",\"categories\":[\"Graphic design & typography\",\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\"],\"tags\":[\"musescore\",\"music engraving\",\"font design\",\"score software\",\"open source\"],\"quote\":\"He does a good job at making the topic of music notation come to life\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"XfQUOHlAocY\",\"title\":\"How Bankrupt American Cities Stay Alive - Debt [ST04]\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"Not Just Bikes explains how many American suburbs, built all at once rather than incrementally, cannot cover their infrastructure costs and survive on growing debt. It draws on Strong Towns' work on the growth Ponzi scheme.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an introduction to the finances of suburban sprawl and its long-term consequences.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"urban sprawl finance\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\"],\"tags\":[\"strong towns\",\"municipal debt\",\"suburbs\",\"urban planning\",\"growth ponzi scheme\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend this video on the finances around urban sprawl and their long term consequences\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"YThelfB2fvg\",\"title\":\"Mouse Cursor History (and why I made my own)\",\"channel\":\"Posy\",\"summary\":\"Posy gives a short history of the computer mouse cursor, from early straight pointers to the tilted arrow, comparing Apple and Windows designs. He then explains why he designed his own cursor set and offers it as a free download.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the definitive, short, well-made video on mouse cursors and pulls it out whenever cursor design, pi