:[\"cathedrals\",\"rules of thumb\",\"history of engineering\",\"medieval\",\"bill hammack\"],\"quote\":\"I encourage everyone to watch this series of videos.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"aFQFB5YpDZE\",\"title\":\"Jon Stewart on Crossfire\",\"channel\":\"Alex Felker\",\"summary\":\"Jon Stewart appears as a guest on CNN's Crossfire in 2004 and criticizes hosts Tucker Carlson and Paul Begala for turning political debate into partisan theater that hurts America.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it constantly as the definitive critique of adversarial TV punditry, still seen as relevant to polarized media and online discourse.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"political media criticism\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"US politics & elections\"],\"tags\":[\"jon stewart\",\"crossfire\",\"punditry\",\"tucker carlson\",\"polarization\"],\"quote\":\"It's so very worth watching again even if you've seen it multiple times.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"bSfe5M_zG2s\",\"title\":\"K Lars Lohn - Keynote - PyCon 2016\",\"channel\":\"PyCon 2016\",\"summary\":\"K Lars Lohn's PyCon 2016 keynote weaves together music theory, programming, biking road trips and fractals. It is a personal, wide-ranging talk rather than a Python how-to.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters call it a mind-blowing, beautiful talk and cite it among their favorite tech talks.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"music and programming\",\"categories\":[\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Creative coding & generative art\"],\"tags\":[\"keynote\",\"music theory\",\"fractals\",\"pycon\",\"creativity\"],\"quote\":\"It's a beautiful talk about music, programming, biking, and life\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"bpQFCcSI0pU\",\"title\":\"I Found The Source of the Coronavirus\",\"channel\":\"laowhy86\",\"summary\":\"A 2020 video by laowhy86 arguing that the coronavirus did not originate outside China, pointing to evidence about the Wuhan institute and a possible patient zero. It cites sources in the description.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently posted in early COVID origin and lab-leak threads as a compelling early case, though other commenters cite rebuttals and note it is speculative.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"covid-19 origins\",\"categories\":[\"Medicine & infectious disease\",\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\"],\"tags\":[\"lab leak\",\"wuhan\",\"coronavirus\",\"china\",\"patient zero\"],\"quote\":\"I wish more people would watch this [0], might not be true but damn does it seem convincing.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"cQP8WApzIQQ\",\"title\":\"Lecture 1: Introduction\",\"channel\":\"MIT 6.824: Distributed Systems\",\"summary\":\"The opening lecture of MIT 6.824 Distributed Systems (Spring 2020), introducing the course and core challenges of distributed systems. It begins a playlist that goes on to cover Raft and other consensus papers in Go.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the standard free course for learning distributed systems from real papers.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"distributed systems\",\"categories\":[\"Distributed systems\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"mit 6.824\",\"raft\",\"consensus\",\"go\",\"course\"],\"quote\":\"Martin Kleppmann (author of DDIA)\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"cfyWvJdsDRI\",\"title\":\"Mock Interview with Shawn McGrath\",\"channel\":\"Molly Rocket\",\"summary\":\"A live mock programmer interview in which Casey Muratori interviews game developer Shawn McGrath using a less confrontational format devised by Chris Hecker. It gets deep into technical details while showing how the candidate actually thinks and works.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as the example of a better alternative to whiteboard and fizzbuzz-style interviews.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"programmer interviewing\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"hiring\",\"technical interviews\",\"game development\",\"interview format\",\"casey muratori\"],\"quote\":\"please watch this alternative method of interviewing\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"cidchWg74Y4\",\"title\":\"Simplicity Ain't Easy - Stuart Halloway\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"Stuart Halloway argues that simplicity is objective, with a definition and etymology useful to programmers, rather than a subjective buzzword. He shows how this view informs Clojure's design and how to make programs simpler.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it, alongside Rich Hickey's talk, for its precise definition of simple versus easy and its implications for code design.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software simplicity\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"clojure\",\"simplicity\",\"software design\",\"rich hickey\",\"functional programming\"],\"quote\":\"He talks about his definition of \\\"simple\\\" (by digging into what the original English definition was) and what that means for code.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"dGVqrGmwOAw\",\"title\":\"Clojure Concurrency - Rich Hickey\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"Rich Hickey's long presentation on Clojure and concurrency covers locking and immutability, then goes in depth on refs, transactions and agents. It ends with a multithreaded ant colony simulation demo.\",\"why_hn\":\"Seen as the definitive explanation of why traditional concurrency is hard and how Clojure's model addresses it, with some commenters crediting it with changing their careers.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"clojure concurrency\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"clojure\",\"rich hickey\",\"stm\",\"immutability\",\"agents\"],\"quote\":\"one my favorite talks I ever went to.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"da1vvigy5tQ\",\"title\":\"Type 2 diabetes | Sarah Hallberg | TEDxPurdueU\",\"channel\":\"TEDx Talks\",\"summary\":\"Dr. Sarah Hallberg argues in a TEDx talk that type 2 diabetes can be treated and potentially reversed through diet, especially by restricting carbohydrates. TED flagged the talk as outside its guidelines and warned against taking it as medical advice.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters recommend it as a clear explanation of carbs, insulin and hunger, and several say it led them to try low-carb eating.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"type 2 diabetes and diet\",\"categories\":[\"Nutrition & diet science\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"low carb\",\"diabetes\",\"insulin\",\"nutrition\",\"ketosis\"],\"quote\":\"Dr. Sarah Hallberg provides compelling evidence that it can, and the solution is simpler than you might think.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"doaQC-S8de8\",\"title\":\"Akira Kurosawa - Composing Movement\",\"channel\":\"Every Frame a Painting\",\"summary\":\"An Every Frame a Painting video essay on how Akira Kurosawa used movement, weather and composition to tell stories. It draws on clips and interviews with other filmmakers.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a deeply researched example of film analysis and one of the most educational videos on YouTube.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"film composition\",\"categories\":[\"Film analysis & criticism\",\"Filmmaking & video production\"],\"tags\":[\"kurosawa\",\"film analysis\",\"cinematography\",\"movement\",\"video essay\"],\"quote\":\"check out the wonderful commentary by 'Every Frame a Painting'\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"fE2KDzZaxvE\",\"title\":\"Zebras All the Way Down - Bryan Cantrill, Uptime 2017\",\"channel\":\"Code & Supply\",\"summary\":\"Bryan Cantrill's Uptime 2017 talk on the engineering challenges of the stateful data path, including storage, firmware and hardware bugs. It uses the 'zebras vs. horses' diagnostic heuristic to explain why rare failures keep appearing.\",\"why_hn\":\"A perennial HN favorite whenever firmware, disks, ECC or opaque hardware come up, valued as both entertaining and sobering.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"storage and firmware reliability\",\"categories\":[\"Databases & storage\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\"],\"tags\":[\"bryan cantrill\",\"firmware\",\"storage\",\"data path\",\"debugging\"],\"quote\":\"As with all his talks, you can expect it to be quite entertaining as well as informative\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"f_0QlhYlS8g\",\"title\":\"Factor: an extensible interactive language\",\"channel\":\"Google TechTalks\",\"summary\":\"A 2008 Google Tech Talk in which Slava Pestov introduces Factor, a concatenative language influenced by Forth, Lisp and Smalltalk. It covers the interactive environment, metaprogramming, and the language's modern high-level features and libraries.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the best introduction to Factor and concatenative programming, with commenters saying it impressed them with the engineering.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"concatenative programming languages\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"factor\",\"forth\",\"stack language\",\"metaprogramming\",\"slava pestov\"],\"quote\":\"It absolutely blew my mind.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"fbhPIK-oBvA\",\"title\":\"Happy People | Spring (part 1) [Happy People Documentary | Spring in Siberia Part 1\",\"channel\":\"Дмитрий Васюков\",\"summary\":\"The first part of Dmitry Vasyukov's four-part Russian documentary series about trappers and villagers living in the Siberian taiga. This episode follows spring life along the Yenisei, with hunting, fishing and subsistence in harsh surroundings.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the authentic original that many prefer over Werner Herzog's shortened re-cut.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"siberian taiga life\",\"categories\":[\"Documentaries & storytelling\",\"Animals & nature\",\"World history\"],\"tags\":[\"siberia\",\"trappers\",\"russia\",\"vasyukov\",\"subsistence living\"],\"quote\":\"I like the original much better, I think of it as the authentic view\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"gTAghAJcO1o\",\"title\":\"Founder School Session: The Future Doesn't Have to Be Incremental\",\"channel\":\"DEMO\",\"summary\":\"Alan Kay speaks at the DEMO Founder School session on why real progress takes invention rather than incremental innovation. He argues for problem-oriented thinking and long-term research funding.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters treat it as a thought-provoking talk on invention versus innovation, and cite it in discussions of software complexity and startups.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"invention versus innovation\",\"categories\":[\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Startups & entrepreneurship\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"alan kay\",\"innovation\",\"research funding\",\"software engineering\",\"startups\"],\"quote\":\"probably the most insightful and though-provoking technology talk I have ever seen\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"gxAaO2rsdIs\",\"title\":\"Simulating an epidemic\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"3Blue1Brown builds toy SIR models to simulate how an epidemic spreads and tests scenarios like social distancing, travel restrictions, central gathering places and quarantine. It was made during the early COVID-19 outbreak.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN pointed 