m comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"shen lisp\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"shen\",\"lisp\",\"macros\",\"type systems\",\"prolog\",\"strange loop\"],\"quote\":\"@deech's talks, though older now, are fantastic and relevant[1,2].\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"l_aFHrzSBrM\",\"title\":\"Is olive oil safe at high heat? Does it taste bad?\",\"channel\":\"Adam Ragusea\",\"summary\":\"Adam Ragusea examines whether olive oil is unsafe for high-heat cooking and whether it tastes bad when heated. He cites studies showing it is among the most stable cooking oils and that smoke-point worries are overblown.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it to debunk the stubborn olive-oil-at-high-heat myth, valued for its cited sources and food scientist interviews.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cooking oils\",\"categories\":[\"Cooking & food\",\"Nutrition & diet science\"],\"tags\":[\"olive oil\",\"smoke point\",\"food science\",\"cooking\",\"myth busting\"],\"quote\":\"Adam Ragusea did a great video on the topic, with cited sources. I highly recommend watching it\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"lhYGXYeMq_E\",\"title\":\"Why Greatness Cannot Be Planned — Kenneth Stanley\",\"channel\":\"Machine Learning Street Talk\",\"summary\":\"A long Machine Learning Street Talk interview with Kenneth Stanley, creator of NEAT and novelty search, about his book on why greatness cannot be planned. They discuss open-endedness, the tyranny of objectives, serendipity and the evolution of ideas.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended to people who find it inspiring or perspective-changing on pursuing goals, open-ended search and AI beyond optimization.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"open-endedness\",\"categories\":[\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"AI & data (other)\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"kenneth stanley\",\"novelty search\",\"objectives\",\"neuroevolution\",\"serendipity\"],\"quote\":\"It really changed my perspective on achieving my long term goals\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"mD4L7xDNCmA\",\"title\":\"Bitcoin 2012 London: Mike Hearn\",\"channel\":\"Independent POV\",\"summary\":\"Mike Hearn's talk at the 2012 London Bitcoin Conference on the future of Bitcoin, new applications and rebuilding the banking system. He covers lesser-known protocol features such as contracts, escrow and scripting.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a great overview of what Bitcoin's protocol can do beyond simple payments, particularly contracts and smart property.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"bitcoin contracts\",\"categories\":[\"Cryptography\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"mike hearn\",\"bitcoin\",\"smart contracts\",\"scripting\",\"2012\"],\"quote\":\"Mike Hearn's talk at Bitcoin 2012 is a great overview of all that\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"mII9NZ8MMVM\",\"title\":\"The Collapse of The American Dream Explained in Animation\",\"channel\":\"doctordude\",\"summary\":\"A 30-minute animated film explaining how money, banking and the Federal Reserve work and arguing that the American economy is heading for collapse. It is opinionated and has a strong point of view.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an accessible animated primer on banking and the Fed, usually with the caveat that it is biased and dated.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"banking and the federal reserve\",\"categories\":[\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\",\"Investing & personal finance\"],\"tags\":[\"federal reserve\",\"banking\",\"money creation\",\"debt\",\"animation\"],\"quote\":\"There's this amazing animated movie from 2011 that explains how banking works and the history of banks\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"mbEfzuCLoAQ\",\"title\":\"Why Trains Suck in America\",\"channel\":\"Wendover Productions\",\"summary\":\"Wendover Productions explains why US passenger rail lags behind other countries. It covers freight companies owning most of the track, geography and population density, and the economics of competing with flying.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a short, solid walkthrough of why American trains are slow and limited, most notably because freight gets priority.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"us passenger rail\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Business & economics (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"amtrak\",\"freight rail\",\"transportation economics\",\"high-speed rail\",\"wendover\"],\"quote\":\"This is worth your 8 minutes of time.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"mbyG85GZ0PI\",\"title\":\"Lecture 01 - The Learning Problem\",\"channel\":\"caltech\",\"summary\":\"The first lecture of Caltech's CS 156 Machine Learning course by Yaser Abu-Mostafa, introducing the learning problem and supervised, unsupervised and reinforcement learning. It is one of 18 lectures in the Learning from Data course.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended repeatedly in 'where do I start with ML' threads as a clear, math-aware introduction.\",\"content_type\":\"course\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"machine learning fundamentals\",\"categories\":[\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"machine learning\",\"learning from data\",\"vc dimension\",\"supervised learning\",\"caltech\"],\"quote\":\"I have found the Caltech machine learning intro course to be the most elucidating of any resource so far\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"nLApFANtkHs\",\"title\":\"ElixirConf 2018 - Docker and OTP Friends or Foes - Daniel Azuma\",\"channel\":\"ElixirConf\",\"summary\":\"Daniel Azuma's ElixirConf 2018 talk on whether Docker and OTP conflict. He shows they can coexist and uses Horde and DeltaCrdt, with a live tank-game demo, to keep state alive across container kills.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended for its practical strategy for preserving state on ephemeral containers, and for the live demo.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"elixir in containers\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\",\"Distributed systems\"],\"tags\":[\"elixir\",\"otp\",\"docker\",\"horde\",\"crdt\",\"clustering\"],\"quote\":\"I would recommend watching Dan Azuma's fantastic video (stick around for the live demo)\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"nfDoml-Db64\",\"title\":\"The man who almost faked his way to a Nobel Prize\",\"channel\":\"BobbyBroccoli\",\"summary\":\"BobbyBroccoli's first of three parts on Jan Hendrik Schon, the Bell Labs postdoc whose fabricated organic semiconductor results nearly won a Nobel Prize. It covers his rise and the context around Bell Labs.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends the series as a gripping account of scientific fraud, and cites it whenever research misconduct comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"scientific fraud\",\"categories\":[\"Scientific method & science communication\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\",\"Semiconductors & chip manufacturing\"],\"tags\":[\"jan hendrik schon\",\"bell labs\",\"research misconduct\",\"nobel prize\",\"physics\"],\"quote\":\"amazing piece of documentary\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"oFeoS41xe7w\",\"title\":\"James Baldwin Debates William F. Buckley (1965)\",\"channel\":\"The Riverbends Channel\",\"summary\":\"The 1965 Cambridge Union debate between James Baldwin and William F. Buckley Jr. on whether the American Dream comes at the expense of the American Negro. It is a historic recording of the two speaking.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended for Baldwin's riveting delivery and as an example of serious debate between opposing worldviews.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"race in america debate\",\"categories\":[\"Social issues & culture\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\",\"US politics & elections\"],\"tags\":[\"james baldwin\",\"william f. buckley\",\"cambridge union\",\"civil rights\",\"oratory\"],\"quote\":\"I was watching James Baldwin's speech at Cambridge University the other day and was riveted the entire time\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"oJTwQvgfgMM\",\"title\":\"Emacs Org-mode - a system for note-taking and project planning\",\"channel\":\"Google TechTalks\",\"summary\":\"A 2008 Google Tech Talk by the creator of Org-mode, introducing the Emacs subsystem for plain-text note-taking and project planning. It covers outlines, TODO states, tags, dates and priorities, and later shows using Org-mode as a spreadsheet.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN regards it as the best introduction and advertisement for Org-mode, and many say it was enough to get them started with it.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"emacs org-mode\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"org-mode\",\"emacs\",\"note-taking\",\"task management\",\"plain text\"],\"quote\":\"The best advertisement for org-mode I've seen is this long Google Tech Talk\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"oKg1hTOQXoY\",\"title\":\"Alan Kay at OOPSLA 1997 - The computer revolution hasnt happened yet\",\"channel\":\"Jeff Gonis\",\"summary\":\"Alan Kay's 1997 OOPSLA keynote, preserved on YouTube after being lost from Google Video. He discusses what object orientation was meant to be, messaging, system design, and the state of computing practice.\",\"why_hn\":\"Treated as a foundational talk, cited constantly for Kay's views on object-oriented programming and for rethinking software practice.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"object-oriented programming philosophy\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"GUI, HCI & interface history\"],\"tags\":[\"alan kay\",\"smalltalk\",\"oop\",\"oopsla\",\"system design\"],\"quote\":\"I encourage all developers to watch that talk and do some thinking about the current state of our practice\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"oP3c1h8v2ZQ\",\"title\":\"Kurt Vonnegut on the Shapes of Stories\",\"channel\":\"David Comberg\",\"summary\":\"A short lecture in which Kurt Vonnegut graphs the simple shapes of stories, such as Man in Hole and Boy Gets Girl. He sketches how fortune rises and falls over a narrative.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as a charming, funny way to see story structure and a source for the idea that plots have shapes.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"story structure\",\"categories\":[\"Literature & writing craft\"],\"tags\":[\"kurt vonnegut\",\"storytelling\",\"narrative arcs\",\"writing\",\"plot shapes\"],\"quote\":\"Vonnegut is really special. I always like coming back to his Shape of Stories talks\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"oTugjssqOT0\",\"title\":\"Randy Pausch Lecture: Time Management\",\"channel\":\"Carnegie Mellon University\",\"summary\":\"Carnegie Mellon professor Randy Pausch gives a lecture on time management at the University of Virginia in 2007. He covers practical habits such as tackling important tasks first and distinguishing important from urgent work.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a practical, memorable guide to prioritization, and some rewatch it every year.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"time management\",\"categories\":[\"Productivity & self-improvement\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"productivity\",\"prioritization\",\"procrastination\",\"randy pausch\",\"self-improvement\"],\"quote\":\"So much so that I rewatch it about once a year\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"ob_GX50Za6c\",\"title\":\"Steve Jobs, \\\"Computers are like  a bicycle for our minds.\\\" - Michael Lawrence Films\",\"channel\":\"Michael Lawrence\",\"summary\":\"A short clip of Steve Jobs describing the computer as a bicycle for the mind. He compares human efficiency of travel to what computers do for mental work.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as the source of the 'bicycle for the mind' quote, which commenters cite in discussions of computing, programming and LLMs.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"computers as tools for the mind\",\"categories\":[\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"GUI, HCI & interface history\"],\"tags\":[\"steve jobs\",\"bicycle for the mind\",\"computing philosophy\",\"apple\"],\"quote\":\"Immediately made me think of Steve Jobs and this great clip\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"pLPpl2ISKTg\",\"title\":\"Pig Butchering Scams: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO)\",\"channel\":\"LastWeekTonight\",\"summary\":\"A Last Week Tonight segment on pig butchering scams, in which long-con romance and investment fraud is run, often by trafficked workers. It explains how victims are groomed and interviews people who lost large sums.\",\"why_hn\":\"Offered as an accessible, well-made explainer to share in threads on scam texts, crypto fraud and scam compounds.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"pig butchering scams\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Law, policing & justice\"],\"tags\":[\"scams\",\"crypto fraud\",\"romance scams\",\"john oliver\",\"human trafficking\"],\"quote\":\"John Oliver did an excellent explainer about how pig butchering works.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"pQ0CvjAJXz4\",\"title\":\"GigCityElixir23 - Bryan Hunter\",\"channel\":\"Groxio\",\"summary\":\"Bryan Hunter's GigCityElixir 2023 talk on HCA Healthcare's Waterpark system, built on Elixir/Erlang. It describes how the architecture is distributed and fault-tolerant, with a multi-datacenter cluster and runtime code swapping.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a rare real-world look at a large fault-tolerant Erlang/Elixir system with no downtime in healthcare.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"fault-tolerant distributed systems\",\"categories\":[\"Distributed systems\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"elixir\",\"erlang\",\"hot code loading\",\"healthcare\",\"distributed systems\",\"beam\"],\"quote\":\"an excellent presentation of design/architecture of a Real-World fault-tolerant and distributed System in Erlang/Elixir\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"pXOK-ZVJMaU\",\"title\":\"Patrick Stewart interview - Parkinson - BBC\",\"channel\":\"BBC Studios\",\"summary\":\"Patrick Stewart on the BBC's Parkinson talk show talks humorously about going bald, how it affected his career and relationships, and how he came to accept it.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it in hair-loss threads as a funny, inspiring example of accepting baldness.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"accepting baldness\",\"categories\":[\"Arts & entertainment (other)\",\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\"],\"tags\":[\"patrick stewart\",\"baldness\",\"talk show\",\"hair loss\",\"bbc\"],\"quote\":\"Patrick Stewart has this awesome interview about his baldness.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"plVk4NVIUh8\",\"title\":\"The Evolution of Bacteria on a “Mega-Plate” Petri Dish (Kishony Lab)\",\"channel\":\"Harvard Medical School\",\"summary\":\"A two-minute time-lapse from the Kishony Lab showing bacteria spreading across a giant petri dish with increasing antibiotic doses, evolving resistance as they go.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it as a visually striking demonstration of evolution and antibiotic resistance happening in days.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"bacterial evolution\",\"categories\":[\"Evolution & genetics\",\"Biology & biotechnology\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"antibiotic resistance\",\"evolution\",\"bacteria\",\"time-lapse\",\"natural selection\"],\"quote\":\"the mega-plate video is an unbelievably good demonstration of bacterial evolution\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"q5SRrsr-Iug\",\"title\":\"Bret Weinstein and Yuri Deigin: Did Covid-19 leak From a Lab?\",\"channel\":\"Bret Weinstein\",\"summary\":\"Bret Weinstein and Yuri Deigin discuss the evidence for the hypothesis that SARS-CoV-2 leaked from a lab, including the furin cleavage site and gain-of-function research. The conversation draws on Deigin's Medium article.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters shared it in 2020 and 2021 as an early, in-depth presentation of the lab-leak case, though it is a contested viewpoint.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"covid lab leak hypothesis\",\"categories\":[\"Medicine & infectious disease\",\"Biology & biotechnology\"],\"tags\":[\"lab leak\",\"sars-cov-2\",\"gain of function\",\"furin site\",\"covid origins\"],\"quote\":\"he had a very interesting conversation with a russian virologist who also thinks this is the case\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"qEV9qoup2mQ\",\"title\":\"Alexander Pope Wrote a 35-Page Takedown of a Historian\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey tells the story of Alexander Pope's long takedown of a historian, Thomas Hearne, framed around the quirks of library books and archival research. It is a funny, digressive video essay about literary feuds and tracking down original sources.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as a masterpiece of humorous archival detective work and a great watch about books and libraries.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"literary history\",\"categories\":[\"Literature & writing craft\",\"World history\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"alexander pope\",\"archival research\",\"library books\",\"cgp grey\",\"history\"],\"quote\":\"Linking the full video because it's a masterpiece\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"sD0NjbwqlYw\",\"title\":\"But what is the Riemann zeta function? Visualizing analytic continuation\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"3Blue1Brown visualizes the Riemann zeta function and the analytic continuation that extends it beyond where its series converges, building toward the Riemann hypothesis.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN praises its visualizations, its handling of the -1/12 result, and its ending, and often prefers it to other explanations of the topic.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"riemann zeta function\",\"categories\":[\"Pure mathematics\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"riemann hypothesis\",\"analytic continuation\",\"complex analysis\",\"visualization\",\"3blue1brown\"],\"quote\":\"I don't think it comes close to 3blue1brown's video on the same topic -\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"u404SLJj7ig\",\"title\":\"Netscape Mozilla Documentary 1998 - 2000 ProJect Code Rush  - creative common licence\",\"channel\":\"znoopy2k\",\"summary\":\"Code Rush is a 2000 PBS documentary following Netscape engineers during the year they open-sourced the browser as Mozilla. It shows the pressure and long hours of work in Silicon Valley at the height of the dot-com era.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a great tech documentary on Mozilla's open-sourcing and the hard work of Netscape engineers.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"netscape and mozilla history\",\"categories\":[\"Tech company stories & documentaries\",\"Hacker culture & free software\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"netscape\",\"mozilla\",\"open source\",\"dot-com\",\"silicon valley\"],\"quote\":\"Code Rush is a great SV tech documentary about the initial release of Mozilla\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"vvZA9n3e5pc\",\"title\":\"Stanford Seminar - The Soul of a New Machine: Rethinking the Computer\",\"channel\":\"Stanford Online\",\"summary\":\"Bryan Cantrill of Oxide Computer Company gives a Stanford EE380 talk on rethinking the server. He argues that hyperscaler-style rack-scale machines with open firmware, RISC-V and Rust should be available to everyone, not just the giants.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the founders' own explanation of what Oxide is building and why, a frequent link whenever Oxide comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"rack-scale server design\",\"categories\":[\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\"],\"tags\":[\"oxide computer\",\"bryan cantrill\",\"servers\",\"open firmware\",\"rust\",\"datacenter\"],\"quote\":\"The talk lays out a history of servers, describes the problems with the servers that you can buy from vendors today\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":6.0},{\"vid\":\"vzfy4EKwG_Y\",\"title\":\"\\\"Outperforming Imperative with Pure Functional Languages\\\" by Richard Feldman\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Richard Feldman explains how pure functional languages can match or beat imperative ones on performance. Compilers can turn immutable code into in-place mutation, avoiding naive copying. The talk draws on his work on the Roc language.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the clearest explanation of why immutable data need not be slow, and as the introduction to Roc.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"functional language performance\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Software performance & optimization\",\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\"],\"tags\":[\"roc\",\"functional programming\",\"immutability\",\"compilers\",\"in-place mutation\"],\"quote\":\"As a starting point for those nitty grittys, this talk is probably as good as