d's most popular programming language and shows how spreadsheets work as a pure functional language. She implements a few mathematical problems in spreadsheet formulas to illustrate why they are so powerful and approachable.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it whenever Excel, spreadsheets or functional programming come up, as a fun and serious take on spreadsheets as real programming.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"spreadsheets as programming\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Software (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"excel\",\"functional programming\",\"spreadsheets\",\"end-user programming\",\"felienne hermans\"],\"quote\":\"Felienne has a great talk on functional programming in Excel\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"1bNOO3xxMc0\",\"title\":\"Zach Tellman - Everything Will Flow\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"Zach Tellman's Clojure West talk covers the basics of queueing theory and its practical use in systems, including backpressure, how queues break down under load, and pitfalls of naive core.async use.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a fascinating explanation of queues, flow control and overload that applies well beyond Clojure.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"queueing theory and backpressure\",\"categories\":[\"Distributed systems\",\"Functional programming\",\"Software performance & optimization\"],\"tags\":[\"queues\",\"backpressure\",\"clojure\",\"latency\",\"distributed systems\"],\"quote\":\"He talks about queues and how they break down under load.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"2wZ1pCpJUIM\",\"title\":\"Scale By The Bay 2018: Bryan Cantrill, Rust and Other Interesting Things\",\"channel\":\"FunctionalTV\",\"summary\":\"Bryan Cantrill argues that programming languages and platforms reflect core values, and compares languages through that lens, with Rust as a main example. He draws on his experience with Solaris, DTrace and ZFS.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited again and again as the framework for discussing languages in terms of values, and how organizational values shape software quality.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming language values\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\"],\"tags\":[\"bryan cantrill\",\"rust\",\"platform values\",\"language design\",\"software culture\"],\"quote\":\"Bryan Cantrill has an excellent talk on this\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"43XKfuptnik\",\"title\":\"Heat Pumps are Not Hard: Here's what it will take to start pumping\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connections\",\"summary\":\"Technology Connections' long closing video in his heat pump series, arguing that adopting heat pumps is well within reach. It explains how they work and what it takes to start using them.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends the series as an enthusiastic, entertaining deep dive into residential heat pumps whenever heating or climate threads arise.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"heat pumps\",\"categories\":[\"Power grid, heating & buildings\",\"Renewables, batteries & storage\"],\"tags\":[\"heating\",\"hvac\",\"energy efficiency\",\"home electrification\",\"residential\"],\"quote\":\"If you want an (entertaining) deep dive into heat pumps, I can recommend Technology Connection's\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"5pdPrQFjo2o\",\"title\":\"Opioids: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO)\",\"channel\":\"LastWeekTonight\",\"summary\":\"A Last Week Tonight segment on the US opioid epidemic, tracing how prescription painkillers fed addiction and heroin use. It is the first of three episodes the show devoted to the crisis.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as an informative, fact-heavy overview of the opioid crisis and Purdue Pharma.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"opioid epidemic\",\"categories\":[\"Medicine & infectious disease\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Law, policing & justice\"],\"tags\":[\"opioids\",\"purdue pharma\",\"john oliver\",\"addiction\",\"public health\"],\"quote\":\"he managed to compile an impressive amount of testimonials and facts.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"5wAo54DHDY0\",\"title\":\"MyHouse.WAD - Inside Doom's Most Terrifying Mod\",\"channel\":\"Power Pak\",\"summary\":\"A long video essay on MyHouse.wad, a Doom II mod uploaded by an anonymous user that hides unsettling secrets in its walls. It tells the story of the map, its mystery and its liminal horror.\",\"why_hn\":\"Strongly recommended as a gripping story and art piece worth watching even if you do not care about games.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"doom horror mod\",\"categories\":[\"Gaming culture & analysis\",\"Speedrunning, glitches & game hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"myhouse.wad\",\"doom\",\"liminal spaces\",\"horror games\",\"game mods\"],\"quote\":\"Just click the link and watch. One of my favorite YouTube rides of all time.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"6h3RJhoqgK8\",\"title\":\"Mike Monteiro - \\\"Fuck you pay me\\\" (PT Br)\",\"channel\":\"Montalvo Machado\",\"summary\":\"Mike Monteiro's Creative Mornings talk \\\"Fuck You, Pay Me\\\" with his lawyer Keith Levine, on contracts, scope creep and making sure designers and freelancers get paid. This copy has Portuguese subtitles.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended to freelancers and consultants as practical advice on contracts and getting paid.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"freelance contracts\",\"categories\":[\"Startups & entrepreneurship\",\"UX & UI design\"],\"tags\":[\"freelancing\",\"contracts\",\"getting paid\",\"consulting\",\"mike monteiro\"],\"quote\":\"1 of my favorite videos on the internet about this is [1].\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"72y2EC5fkcE\",\"title\":\"Tomorrow Corporation Tech Demo\",\"channel\":\"retrogameinternals\",\"summary\":\"Allan Blomquist demonstrates the internal programming and debugging tools Tomorrow Corporation uses to make games, including live modification of a running game and time-travel debugging.\",\"why_hn\":\"Held up in debugger and tooling threads as an amazing ideal of a tight feedback loop and what debugging could be.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"debugging tools\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"time-travel debugging\",\"hot reload\",\"game development\",\"tooling\",\"live coding\"],\"quote\":\"this demo has got to be the closest thing to the perfect debugger I've ever seen\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"7G4ipM2qjfw\",\"title\":\"Connecting Solar to the Grid is Harder Than You Think\",\"channel\":\"Practical Engineering\",\"summary\":\"Practical Engineering explains why connecting solar and other inverter-based generation to the grid is hard. It covers grid stability, load balancing and the behind-the-scenes engineering challenges of renewables.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people link it as the go-to explainer for grid and inverter stability, especially in threads about blackouts and distributed solar.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"solar grid integration\",\"categories\":[\"Power grid, heating & buildings\",\"Renewables, batteries & storage\"],\"tags\":[\"solar\",\"power grid\",\"inverters\",\"grid stability\",\"renewables\"],\"quote\":\"Just watch this Practical Engineering video [1], if you are not familiar with the topic.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"8An2SxNFvmU\",\"title\":\"Do Nothing- a message of motivation from Self-help Singh- (un) motivational speaker and life coach\",\"channel\":\"Masood Boomgaard\",\"summary\":\"A short satirical 'motivational' talk by the character Self-help Singh, a sham life coach who urges viewers to do nothing. It parodies self-help gurus and hustle culture.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as funny anti-motivational comedy that also touches on real truths about productivity and the rat race.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"anti-motivational satire\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"satire\",\"self-help parody\",\"productivity\",\"doing nothing\",\"comedy\"],\"quote\":\"While it's meant to be funny, it actually touches some deeper philosophical truths.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"8JOD1AQGqEg\",\"title\":\"The Web That Never Was - Dylan Beattie\",\"channel\":\"NDC Conferences\",\"summary\":\"Dylan Beattie's entertaining NDC talk imagines an alternate history of the web, asking what if different decisions had been made on operating systems, browsers and standards. It blends real history with humor.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters strongly recommend it as a fun alt-history of the web and bring it up whenever people muse about how the web could have been different.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"alternate history of the web\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"History of computing\",\"Software (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"web history\",\"alternate history\",\"microsoft\",\"javascript\",\"dylan beattie\"],\"quote\":\"Edit 2: finished, it is definitely worth watching.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"8Ko3TdPy0TU\",\"title\":\"How lucky is too lucky?: The Minecraft Speedrunning Dream Controversy Explained\",\"channel\":\"Stand-up Maths\",\"summary\":\"Matt Parker analyzes the Minecraft speedrunning controversy in which Dream was accused of cheating via improbably lucky drops. He walks through the probability and the statistical arguments in the original accusation and the reply.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an engaging lesson in probability and in how statistics can expose cheating.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"probability and cheating detection\",\"categories\":[\"Probability & game theory\",\"Gaming culture & analysis\",\"Speedrunning, glitches & game hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"minecraft\",\"speedrunning\",\"statistics\",\"matt parker\",\"dream\"],\"quote\":\"It not only exposes the cheater, it also gives some intuition into negligible probabilities.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"98LdFA-_zfA\",\"title\":\"Christopher Alexander - Patterns in Architecture\",\"channel\":\"Peter Petrash\",\"summary\":\"Christopher Alexander's keynote at OOPSLA 1996 on patterns in architecture, addressed to the software community. He moves beyond patterns toward deeper ideas of wholeness and living structure.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as the primary source for the claim that Alexander deprecated patterns, and as a prescient talk for programmers.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"pattern languages in architecture\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Visual arts, photography & architecture\"],\"tags\":[\"christopher alexander\",\"design patterns\",\"oopsla\",\"living structure\",\"architecture\"],\"quote\":\"Just finished watching a great 1996 lecture by Christopher Alexander\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"9eyFDBPk4Yw\",\"title\":\"Admiral Grace Hopper Explains the Nanosecond\",\"channel\":\"funbury\",\"summary\":\"Admiral Grace Hopper holds up a length of wire to show how far light travels in a nanosecond, about 11.8 inches. She explains how she made the idea concrete to people who could not picture a billionth of a second.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it to illustrate physical latency limits and why performance and distance matter in computing.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"latency and the speed of light\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"grace hopper\",\"nanosecond\",\"latency\",\"speed of light\",\"performance\"],\"quote\":\"Admiral Grace Hopper Explains the Nanosecond\\\" is a good illustration of this (segment is only 2m)\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"9yjZpBq1XBE\",\"title\":\"Disney's FastPass: A Complicated History\",\"channel\":\"Defunctland\",\"summary\":\"A feature-length Defunctland documentary on the history of Disney's FastPass and FastPass+ expedited queue systems. It covers how the systems evolved and how they were designed, with simulation of their effects on park lines.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an accessible, engrossing lesson in queueing theory and systems design, even for people with no interest in Disney.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"queueing theory in theme parks\",\"categories\":[\"Documentaries & storytelling\",\"Tech company stories & documentaries\",\"Algorithms & data structures\"],\"tags\":[\"disney fastpass\",\"queueing theory\",\"theme parks\",\"systems design\",\"defunctland\"],\"quote\":\"If you ever wanted a feature-length explanation of queueing theory and how ignoring it makes everything worse, well... here you go\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"APZCfmgzoS0\",\"title\":\"Fasting vs. Eating Less: What's the Difference? (Science of Fasting)\",\"channel\":\"Joseph Everett - WIL\",\"summary\":\"A short explainer on the physiological differences between fasting and simply eating less. It covers ketosis, ghrelin and hunger waves, and the hormonal effects of fasting.\",\"why_hn\":\"Shared as an accessible, convincing case for intermittent fasting and its physiology.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"fasting physiology\",\"categories\":[\"Nutrition & diet science\",\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\"],\"tags\":[\"fasting\",\"ketosis\",\"ghrelin\",\"intermittent fasting\",\"metabolism\"],\"quote\":\"provide some compelling arguments for switching to intermittent fasting for many major systemic health benefits\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"AW3gaelBypY\",\"title\":\"The Carbon Offset Problem\",\"channel\":\"Wendover Productions\",\"summary\":\"Wendover Productions examines why carbon offsets so often fail to reduce emissions, covering specific examples and the perverse incentives in offset schemes.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in greenwashing and offset threads as a well-sourced, detailed explanation of how offsets underdeliver.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"carbon offsets\",\"categories\":[\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\",\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\",\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\"],\"tags\":[\"carbon credits\",\"greenwashing\",\"climate policy\",\"perverse incentives\",\"wendover\"],\"quote\":\"does a much better job than the article of both highlighting specific examples and laying out the perverse incentive structures\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"AmgkSdhK4K8\",\"title\":\"Who cares about topology?   (Old version)\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"An older 3Blue1Brown video on why topology matters, built around the inscribed rectangle problem. It walks through how tori and Mobius strips appear in the solution. The channel later replaced it with an updated version.\",\"why_hn\":\"Fans name it as a favorite 3Blue1Brown video, praising its detective-story treatment of the inscribed square and rectangle problem.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"topology\",\"categories\":[\"Pure mathematics\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"inscribed rectangle\",\"mobius strip\",\"torus\",\"3blue1brown\",\"geometry\"],\"quote\":\"A particular favourite is the one on the Inscribed Square Problem\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"AyOAjFNPAbA\",\"title\":\"AWS re:Invent 2016: Tuesday Night Live with James Hamilton\",\"channel\":\"Amazon Web Services\",\"summary\":\"James Hamilton's Tuesday Night Live keynote at AWS re:Invent 2016, touring AWS infrastructure at scale including networking, power and custom silicon. It also features NASA JPL's Tom Soderstrom.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats Hamilton's annual talks as the best window into how AWS regions and hardware are actually built.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"cloud infrastructure\",\"categories\":[\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\",\"Distributed systems\",\"Semiconductors & chip manufacturing\"],\"tags\":[\"aws\",\"james hamilton\",\"datacenters\",\"custom silicon\",\"networking\"],\"quote\":\"The keynote by James Hamilton[1] is absolutely awesome.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"BickMFHAZR0\",\"title\":\"How Trees Bend the Laws of Physics\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium explains how trees lift water from roots to the top of tall trunks, using transpiration pull and the negative pressure that liquid water can sustain. It explains why trees do not need pumps or valves.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as one of Veritasium's best videos and one that has aged well.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"plant biophysics\",\"categories\":[\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Animals & nature\",\"Fundamental physics\"],\"tags\":[\"transpiration\",\"negative pressure\",\"trees\",\"water transport\",\"physics\"],\"quote\":\"I recommend watching, I think it's one of the best veritasium Dereck has ever produced.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"CaCcOwJPytQ\",\"title\":\"Special Topics - The Kalman Filter (1 of 55) What is a Kalman Filter?\",\"channel\":\"Michel van Biezen\",\"summary\":\"The first video in a 55-part series by Michel van Biezen that introduces the Kalman filter and builds from the rationale through algebraic examples to the full matrix treatment. The videos are short, blackboard-style lectures.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends this series as a clear, step-by-step way to really understand Kalman filtering.\",\"content_type\":\"course\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"kalman filtering\",\"categories\":[\"Data science & statistics\",\"Pure mathematics\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"kalman filter\",\"state estimation\",\"linear algebra\",\"control theory\",\"lecture series\"],\"quote\":\"It's really worth watching even just the first couple videos to get a deeper understanding of this very useful technique.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"DkGMY63FF3Q\",\"title\":\"Compost-Fueled Cars: Wouldn't That Be Great? - Onion Talks - Ep. 1\",\"channel\":\"The Onion\",\"summary\":\"An Onion Talks satire in which a young media professional pitches compost-powered cars, offering the idea itself and leaving all implementation to others.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as shorthand for vague, detail-free visionary pitches and as a satirical jab at TED-style techno-utopian talks.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ted talk satire\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\"],\"tags\":[\"the onion\",\"satire\",\"ideas guy\",\"ted talks\",\"startup culture\"],\"quote\":\"neatly summarizes so much of the breathless excitement of vague, detail-free scientific and entrepreneurial claims\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"EK32jo7i5LQ\",\"title\":\"Why do prime numbers make these spirals? | Dirichlet’s theorem and pi approximations\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"3Blue1Brown explains why plotting primes in polar coordinates produces spiral patterns, using modular arithmetic, Dirichlet's theorem and rational approximations of pi.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently recommended as the clearest explanation of prime spirals and the Ulam spiral.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"prime number spirals\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"primes\",\"ulam spiral\",\"modular arithmetic\",\"pi approximation\",\"3blue1brown\"],\"quote\":\"Here is one of the most enlightening explanations for these kinds of spirals I have seen by 3blue1brown\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"Eco2s3-0zsQ\",\"title\":\"Joe Rogan Experience #1361 - Cmdr. David Fravor & Jeremy Corbell\",\"channel\":\"PowerfulJRE\",\"summary\":\"A Joe Rogan podcast with retired Navy commander David Fravor, who describes his 2004 Tic Tac UFO encounter, and filmmaker Jeremy Corbell. Fravor walks through the incident and the released videos.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN refers to it as the direct first-person account from the pilot in UFO and UAP threads, though some find it unconvincing.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"tic tac ufo encounter\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"ufo\",\"uap\",\"david fravor\",\"navy pilot\",\"joe rogan\"],\"quote\":\"one of the most compelling eyewitness accounts I’ve seen\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"En_2T7KH6RA\",\"title\":\"Ted Nelson demonstrates Xanadu Space\",\"channel\":\"photonhunter\",\"summary\":\"Ted Nelson demonstrates Xanadu Space, his software for representing hypertext in three-dimensional space with linked, referenced content fragments.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the shortest way to grasp Nelson's idea of referential content and how the web could have been different.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"xanadu hypertext\",\"categories\":[\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"ted nelson\",\"xanadu\",\"hypertext\",\"transclusion\",\"history of computing\"],\"quote\":\"Too many great talks to mention, but if I had to pick one it would be Ted Nelson's few minutes of demonstration of Xanadu.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"FnEWBtCnFs8\",\"title\":\"2 1 1 Jason Scott   Rescuing The Prince of Persia from the sands of time\",\"channel\":\"Adrian Crenshaw\",\"summary\":\"Jason Scott's DerbyCon 2012 talk on rescuing the Prince of Persia source code from old floppy disks, working with the game's author Jordan Mechner. It is a story of digital archaeology and recovering data from ancient media.\",\"why_hn\":\"Repeatedly recommended as a favorite tech talk and a great example of digital preservation work.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"digital archaeology\",\"categories\":[\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"History of computing\",\"Hacker culture & free software\"],\"tags\":[\"prince of persia\",\"data recovery\",\"jason scott\",\"floppy disks\",\"software preservation\"],\"quote\":\"My favourite example of digital archaeology from recent years\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"G0R09YzyuCI\",\"title\":\"Collapse of Complex Societies by Dr. Joseph Tainter\",\"channel\":\"vlada888\",\"summary\":\"Joseph Tainter lectures at a 2010 sustainability conference on why complex societies such as Rome collapse. His thesis is that rising complexity brings diminishing returns until societies cannot afford to maintain it.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the best exposition of Tainter's theory, and used to explain slowing innovation, centralization and collapse risk.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"societal collapse\",\"categories\":[\"World history\",\"Futurism & technology and society\",\"Social issues & culture\"],\"tags\":[\"joseph tainter\",\"complexity\",\"diminishing returns\",\"roman empire\",\"sustainability\"],\"quote\":\"Spend some time watching Tainter's speech on complexity and collapse\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"GNO_CYUjMK8\",\"title\":\"[JSConfUS 2013] Steven Wittens: Making WebGL Dance\",\"channel\":\"JSConf\",\"summary\":\"Steven Wittens' JSConf US 2013 talk on drawing with WebGL, using animated visuals to walk through vectors, matrices and the rendering pipeline. Despite the title, it works as a visual primer on the linear algebra behind 3D graphics.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as one of the best intros to the math of 3D graphics and a staple answer to how to learn graphics programming.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"3d graphics math\",\"categories\":[\"Computer graphics & rendering\",\"Web development\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"webgl\",\"linear algebra\",\"matrices\",\"rendering\",\"visualization\"],\"quote\":\"It's a fantastic, FANTASTIC tour of 3D graphics fundamentals.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"GVsUOuSjvcg\",\"title\":\"Future Computers Will Be Radically Different (Analog Computing)\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium explains why analog computing may suit AI workloads, covering old analog computers, The Analog Thing and Mythic's analog chips that use flash memory for neural network inference. It argues digital hardware is hitting limits.\",\"why_hn\":\"Treated as the accessible reference on analog computing for neural nets, especially the Mythic AI segment.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"analog computing\",\"categories\":[\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"analog computers\",\"neural networks\",\"mythic ai\",\"in-memory compute\",\"veritasium\"],\"quote\":\"Veritasium explains it really well in general here (and demos the device)\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"GnBbhXBDmwU\",\"title\":\"LibreSSL with Bob Beck\",\"channel\":\"Jason Tubnor\",\"summary\":\"Bob Beck of OpenBSD describes the first month of LibreSSL, the fork aimed at cleaning up OpenSSL, including oddities found in the code base and why it was cleaned rather than audited cryptographically.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently linked as an entertaining and alarming account of OpenSSL code quality and why the fork happened.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"libressl and openssl cleanup\",\"categories\":[\"Cryptography\",\"Security & hacking\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\"],\"tags\":[\"libressl\",\"openssl\",\"openbsd\",\"heartbleed\",\"c code quality\"],\"quote\":\"The entire talk is amazing, even 7 years later.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"GoW8Tf7hTGA\",\"title\":\"Star Size Comparison 2\",\"channel\":\"morn1415\",\"summary\":\"A visual scale comparison of stars and cosmic objects, from planets up to some of the largest known stars, set to music. It is meant to convey the vastness of the universe.\",\"why_hn\":\"Shared as a go-to humbling look at cosmic scale, noted for its chilling audio and visuals pairing.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cosmic scale\",\"categories\":[\"Cosmology & astrophysics\",\"Data visualization & information design\"],\"tags\":[\"star sizes\",\"astronomy\",\"scale comparison\",\"universe\",\"visualization\"],\"quote\":\"The combination of audio and content of this video gives me chills.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"GqmsQeSzMdw\",\"title\":\"Constraints Liberate, Liberties Constrain — Runar Bjarnason\",\"channel\":\"Scala World\",\"summary\":\"Runar Bjarnason's Scal