ffee maker\",\"mr. coffee\",\"appliances\",\"simple design\",\"technology connections\",\"bimetal valve\"],\"quote\":\"Anyone interested in how the traditional american coffee maker works should watch this.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"SrU9YDoXE88\",\"title\":\"How To Count Past Infinity\",\"channel\":\"Vsauce\",\"summary\":\"Vsauce's Michael Stevens takes viewers past infinity using transfinite numbers, ordinals and cardinals, with input from mathematician Hugh Woodin. It is a fast-paced, mind-bending tour of different sizes of infinity.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the most accessible introduction to what lies beyond infinity.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"transfinite numbers\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Logic & formal methods\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"infinity\",\"ordinals\",\"cardinals\",\"set theory\",\"vsauce\"],\"quote\":\"The most accessible resource I found was this VSauce video\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"TgmA48fILq8\",\"title\":\"Dtrace Review\",\"channel\":\"GoogleTalksArchive\",\"summary\":\"Bryan Cantrill's 2007 Google Tech Talk on DTrace, showing how dynamic tracing improves debugging of both development and live production systems. It includes a vivid war story about tracking down a problem on a Sun Ray server.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN regards it as a gem of a technical talk that tackles a fundamental debugging problem and shows a new technology solving it.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"dynamic tracing with dtrace\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Operating systems & kernels\",\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\"],\"tags\":[\"dtrace\",\"debugging\",\"observability\",\"solaris\",\"bryan cantrill\"],\"quote\":\"I'm partial to Bryan Cantrill's Dtrace talk since it tackles a fundamental problem in software engineering and shows how to solve it using a new technology.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"U2huVf1l4UE\",\"title\":\"Climbing the Rating Ladder: Up to 1000\",\"channel\":\"John Bartholomew\",\"summary\":\"Chess player John Bartholomew plays opponents rated below 1000 on Chess.com and comments on optimal strategy and typical mistakes at that level. It is part of his Climbing the Rating Ladder series.\",\"why_hn\":\"Strongly recommended as a way to improve at chess, since it explains the basics and common mistakes at each rating level.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"chess improvement\",\"categories\":[\"Chess, board games & puzzles\"],\"tags\":[\"chess\",\"beginner strategy\",\"chess.com\",\"game analysis\",\"rating ladder\"],\"quote\":\"He explains the basics and mistakes players at each level makes and how to avoid them\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"URSWYvyc42M\",\"title\":\"Rails Conf 2013 The Magic Tricks of Testing by Sandi Metz\",\"channel\":\"Confreaks\",\"summary\":\"Sandi Metz's RailsConf 2013 talk offers practical guidelines for deciding what to test and how, so tests stay cheap and stable. She argues for testing the public interface rather than implementation and using mocks appropriately.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an incredible talk whose testing principles apply beyond Ruby.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"testing strategy\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Software architecture & design\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"unit testing\",\"ruby\",\"mocks\",\"object-oriented design\",\"sandi metz\"],\"quote\":\"watch Sandi Metz' incredible RailsConf talk\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"UcN7SGGoCNI\",\"title\":\"DAIRY IS SCARY!\",\"channel\":\"Erin Janus\",\"summary\":\"Vegan advocate Erin Janus explains the dairy industry in about five minutes, covering how milk is produced and the treatment of cows and calves. It is an advocacy piece and includes graphic material.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters in veganism and food threads link it as a short, info-dense explanation that some say opened their eyes, though others dispute it.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"dairy industry practices\",\"categories\":[\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\",\"Animals & nature\"],\"tags\":[\"dairy\",\"veganism\",\"animal welfare\",\"milk\",\"food system\"],\"quote\":\"I know it opened my eyes\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"UjZYMI1zB9s\",\"title\":\"Free to Play\",\"channel\":\"Valve\",\"summary\":\"Valve's feature-length documentary following three professional gamers competing in the first Dota 2 International tournament for a million dollar prize. It shows the sacrifices and pressures of top-level esports.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an engaging look at the life of pro Dota players and the early rise of esports, though one commenter notes it is somewhat promotional.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"esports\",\"categories\":[\"Gaming culture & analysis\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"dota 2\",\"esports\",\"valve\",\"the international\",\"professional gaming\"],\"quote\":\"I've enjoyed this documentary a lot.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"VUhlNx_-wYk\",\"title\":\"Ruby Manor 3 - Programming with Nothing by Tom Stuart\",\"channel\":\"Confreaks\",\"summary\":\"Tom Stuart's Ruby Manor 3 talk builds FizzBuzz using nothing but Ruby lambdas, working through Church encodings of numbers, booleans and data structures. It is a hands-on introduction to lambda calculus.\",\"why_hn\":\"Strongly recommended in lambda calculus and Y-combinator threads as a mind-expanding half hour that clarifies the ideas.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"lambda calculus\",\"categories\":[\"Computability & complexity theory\",\"Functional programming\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"lambda calculus\",\"church encoding\",\"ruby\",\"fizzbuzz\",\"y combinator\",\"functional programming\"],\"quote\":\"An awesome video which changed my perspective towards lambda calculus and programming in general\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"XI5frPV58tY\",\"title\":\"6 ways mushrooms can save the world | Paul Stamets | TED\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"Mycologist Paul Stamets presents six ways mycelium and fungi could help save the world, in an 18-minute TED talk. Topics include environmental cleanup and other applications of fungi.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an engaging, down-to-earth talk on the world-changing potential of fungi.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"mycology and fungi\",\"categories\":[\"Biology & biotechnology\",\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\"],\"tags\":[\"mycelium\",\"fungi\",\"paul stamets\",\"ted\",\"environment\"],\"quote\":\"He has a brilliant TED talk on the world-changing potential of fungi research [1].\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"XRr1kaXKBsU\",\"title\":\"What Everyone Gets Wrong About Gravity\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium explains, with expert input, that in general relativity gravity is not a force and objects follow straight paths through curved spacetime, including why standing on the ground means you are accelerating.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it as a go-to intuitive explanation whenever gravity as spacetime curvature or free fall comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"general relativity\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\",\"Cosmology & astrophysics\"],\"tags\":[\"gravity\",\"spacetime\",\"geodesics\",\"einstein\",\"free fall\"],\"quote\":\"his videos on special [1] and general relativity [2] were real eye-openers for me\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"XvDZLjaCJuw\",\"title\":\"UNIX: Making Computers Easier To Use -- AT&T Archives film from 1982, Bell Laboratories\",\"channel\":\"AT&T Tech Channel\",\"summary\":\"A 1982 AT&T Bell Labs film for students about the Unix system and how it can be used practically. It includes Lorinda Cherry building a talking calculator by piping together Unix commands, and a spell checker made from Unix utilities.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a demonstration of the Unix philosophy, with small tools composed through pipes.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"unix philosophy\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Operating systems & kernels\"],\"tags\":[\"unix\",\"bell labs\",\"pipes\",\"lorinda cherry\",\"history of computing\",\"kernighan\"],\"quote\":\"both are really excellent demonstrations and eludications of the Unix philosophy and mindset\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"Y6U728AZnV0\",\"title\":\"Run for your life! At a comfortable pace, and not too far: James O'Keefe at TEDxUMKC\",\"channel\":\"TEDx Talks\",\"summary\":\"Cardiologist James O'Keefe argues in a TEDx talk that moderate running confers the best longevity benefits, while extreme endurance training can damage the heart. He draws on data and his own experience as a lifelong runner.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it whenever people question whether more exercise is always better, especially extreme endurance running.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"exercise and heart health\",\"categories\":[\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"running\",\"cardiology\",\"endurance\",\"longevity\",\"tedx\"],\"quote\":\"I think about this TED Talk from 2012 a lot..\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.5}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"YyIQKBzIuBY\",\"title\":\"Alan Kay - Programming and Scaling\",\"channel\":\"Colin McDonnell\",\"summary\":\"An Alan Kay talk on programming and scaling, covering theoretical limits of code size, engineering versus tinkering, and biological cells as inspiration for object-oriented design.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN fans call it one of Kay's best talks and recommend it for rethinking why programming paradigms have barely changed.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software scaling and objects\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"alan kay\",\"oop\",\"smalltalk\",\"code size\",\"systems thinking\"],\"quote\":\"This talk is excellent and will cue you in on how to go about thinking about the fact\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"ZXJWO2FQ16c\",\"title\":\"Steve Reich, \\\"Music for 18 Musicians\\\" - FULL PERFORMANCE with eighth blackbird\",\"channel\":\"Vic Firth\",\"summary\":\"A full performance of Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians by eighth blackbird with Third Coast Percussion and others, recorded in Chicago in 2011. It is an hour-long minimalist piece built on phasing and repeating patterns.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in coding-music threads as lyric-free, repetitive music that aids focus.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"minimalist music\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"steve reich\",\"minimalism\",\"phasing\",\"focus music\",\"ensemble\"],\"quote\":\"\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"_yMMTVVJI4c\",\"title\":\"Why don't Americans use electric kettles?\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connections\",\"summary\":\"Technology Connections explains why electric kettles are uncommon in the US, centering on 120V circuits limiting power. He tests ways of boiling water on gas, electric ranges and kettles.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it constantly in threads on voltage, gas stoves and boiling water as the definitive measured comparison, with the kettle coming out fastest and most efficient.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"electric kettles and household power\",\"categories\":[\"Power grid, heating & buildings\",\"Cooking & food\"],\"tags\":[\"electric kettle\",\"120v vs 240v\",\"gas stove\",\"energy efficiency\",\"boiling water\"],\"quote\":\"Technology Connections on YouTube has a good video about how electric kettles are the superior way to boil water\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"aMcjxSThD54\",\"title\":\"Jordan Peterson debate on the gender pay gap, campus protests and postmodernism\",\"channel\":\"Channel 4 News\",\"summary\":\"Channel 4 News's full interview with Jordan Peterson, in which Cathy Newman presses him on the gender pay gap, patriarchy, campus protests and postmodernism. It was widely discussed for its confrontational style.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as the classic example of an interviewer misrepresenting a guest, though opinions on who came out better differ.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"jordan peterson interview\",\"categories\":[\"Social issues & culture\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"jordan peterson\",\"gender pay gap\",\"interviewing\",\"cathy newman\",\"straw man\"],\"quote\":\"A classic, even if you don't like the guy\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"arj7oStGLkU\",\"title\":\"Inside the Mind of a Master Procrastinator | Tim Urban | TED\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"Tim Urban's TED talk on why people procrastinate, covering YouTube binges and Wikipedia rabbit holes. It ends with the life-calendar idea as a nudge to act before time runs out.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a funny, empathetic take on procrastination and the origin of the life calendar concept.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"procrastination\",\"categories\":[\"Productivity & self-improvement\",\"Psychology & human behavior\"],\"tags\":[\"tim urban\",\"ted\",\"productivity\",\"life calendar\",\"psychology\"],\"quote\":\"absolutely legendary TedX talk that humanizes procrastination\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"bRCNenhcvpw\",\"title\":\"IBM Selectric Typewriter & its digital to analogue converter\",\"channel\":\"engineerguy\",\"summary\":\"Bill Hammack of engineerguy uses slow motion video to show how the IBM Selectric typewriter works. He focuses on its fully mechanical digital-to-analog converter, which turns key presses into rotation of the type ball.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it whenever the Selectric comes up, as a short, excellent explanation of its internals.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"mechanical engineering\",\"categories\":[\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"ibm selectric\",\"typewriter\",\"mechanical computing\",\"whiffletree\",\"digital-to-analog\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend watching\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"ceFyF9px20Y\",\"title\":\"Fat Chance: Fructose 2.0\",\"channel\":\"University of California Television (UCTV)\",\"summary\":\"Robert Lustig of UCSF updates his sugar talk, arguing that fructose and processed foods drive obesity and metabolic syndrome through their endocrine effects. It is a roughly 90-minute university lecture.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a follow-up to Lustig's original sugar talk for the metabolic details of fructose.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"fructose and obesity\",\"categories\":[\"Nutrition & diet science\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"robert lustig\",\"sugar\",\"metabolic syndrome\",\"nutrition\",\"endocrinology\"],\"quote\":\"I'd highly recommend these two talks by Robert Lustig on the subject.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"d0yGdNEWdn0\",\"title\":\"How to learn any language in six months | Chris Lonsdale | TEDxLingnanUniversity\",\"channel\":\"TEDx Talks\",\"summary\":\"Chris Lonsdale presents a TEDx talk on how adults can learn a language in about six months, centering on immersion, comprehensible input and a practical learning methodology.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an efficient, practical approach to adult language learning, with one commenter saying it changed their life.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"language learning\",\"categories\":[\"Language & linguistics\",\"Education & learning\"],\"tags\":[\"language acquisition\",\"ted talk\",\"immersion\",\"adult learning\",\"fluency\"],\"quote\":\"this talk which actually changed my life\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"d1EB1zsxW0k\",\"title\":\"Energy Investments Dialogue | Bill Gates | Global Energy Forum\",\"channel\":\"Stanford ENERGY\",\"summary\":\"Bill Gates at Stanford's Global Energy Forum argues that cheaper solar, wind and storage are not nearly enough to stop climate change. He discusses natural gas, a carbon tax, nuclear, battery costs and the responsibilities of developing versus developed economies.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in energy threads for Gates's argument on why batteries and renewables alone fall short and nuclear is needed.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"clean energy and climate\",\"categories\":[\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\",\"Renewables, batteries & storage\",\"Nuclear power & fusion\"],\"tags\":[\"bill gates\",\"nuclear power\",\"battery storage\",\"renewables\",\"carbon capture\"],\"quote\":\"which I highly recommend watching\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"dCSIXLIzhzk\",\"title\":\"How Far Are The Nearest Stars?\",\"channel\":\"Cody'sLab\",\"summary\":\"Cody's Lab builds a 200-billionth-scale model to show how far away the nearest stars are, using parallax and light speed. A short astronomy explainer on interstellar distances.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended whenever threads need an intuition for the vast distances between stars.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"interstellar distances\",\"categories\":[\"Cosmology & astrophysics\",\"Space exploration & spaceflight\"],\"tags\":[\"scale model\",\"alpha centauri\",\"astronomy\",\"parallax\",\"space\"],\"quote\":\"maybe watch this video that illustrates the distance only to the closest star, let alone any further away\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"fwcl17Q0bpk\",\"title\":\"NSA operation ORCHESTRA: Annual Status Report\",\"channel\":\"HUP\",\"summary\":\"Poul-Henning Kamp's FOSDEM 2014 talk is a hypothetical account of how an intelligence agency such as the NSA would sabotage open source software, standards and online discussion. It covers Skype's history, OpenSSL and psyops against standards.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returned to it repeatedly, especially after Heartbleed, as an eye-opening argument about subverting open source and standards.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"nsa sabotage of open source\",\"categories\":[\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Security & hacking\",\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\"],\"tags\":[\"poul-henning kamp\",\"nsa\",\"openssl\",\"surveillance\",\"standards\",\"fosdem\"],\"quote\":\"The entire talk is an eye opener.\",\"recommenders\":9,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"g1ib43q3uXQ\",\"title\":\"\\\"How to teach programming (and other things)?\\\" by Felienne Hermans\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Felienne Hermans's Strange Loop keynote on the history and pedagogy of teaching programming. She questions exploration-only approaches like Logo and discusses research supporting explicit instruction and rote practice.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in threads about teaching kids and beginners to code as research-based evidence that explicit instruction beats pure discovery.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming education\",\"categories\":[\"Education & learning\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"teaching programming\",\"pedagogy\",\"logo\",\"education research\",\"felienne hermans\"],\"quote\":\"The talk is on YouTube and I gotta say, it's very insightful.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"gEKSpZPByD0\",\"title\":\"Particles, Fields and The Future of Physics - A Lecture by Sean Carroll\",\"channel\":\"Fermilab\",\"summary\":\"Sean Carroll's 2013 Fermilab Users Meeting lecture on particles, fields and the future of particle physics. He explains that particles are vibrations in underlying quantum fields and touches on the Higgs, neutrinos and dark matter.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the clearest long-form explanation of the everything-is-fields view for non-physicists.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"quantum field theory\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum physics\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"sean carroll\",\"quantum fields\",\"particle physics\",\"higgs\",\"dark matter\"],\"quote\":\"Sean Carroll is a master of making quantum mechanics approachable to non-physicists.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"gd5uJ7Nlvvo\",\"title\":\"Plain Text - Dylan Beattie - NDC Copenhagen 2022\",\"channel\":\"NDC Conferences\",\"summary\":\"Dylan Beattie's conference talk explores the history and mess behind plain text: encodings, Unicode, line endings and the many ways text goes wrong. It argues that the tidy idea of plain text hides a lot of complexity underneath everyday software.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people recommend it as an entertaining, surprisingly deep talk that comes up whenever encodings, mojibake or Unicode are discussed.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"plain text and character encodings\",\"categories\":[\"Graphic design & typography\",\"Software (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"unicode\",\"encodings\",\"mojibake\",\"text files\",\"conference talk\"],\"quote\":\"you owe it to yourself to check out this super entertaining talk\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"h47zZrqjgLc\",\"title\":\"I Shot You First: Networking the Gameplay of Halo: Reach\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"Bungie's David Aldridge explains the networking programming behind Halo: Reach in a 2011 GDC session. It covers how gameplay stays in sync online, including forecasting and state reconciliation.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN considers it one of the best game networking talks, with insights that carry over to distributed systems generally.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"game networking\",\"categories\":[\"Game programming & engines\",\"Distributed systems\"],\"tags\":[\"halo reach\",\"netcode\",\"state synchronization\",\"gdc\",\"distributed systems\"],\"quote\":\"This talk from the Halo: Reach team might be one of my favorite game networking talks I've ever seen\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"hER0Qp6QJNU\",\"title\":\"Simon Sinek on Millennials in the Workplace\",\"channel\":\"David Crossman\",\"summary\":\"An excerpt of Simon Sinek on Inside Quest discussing millennials in the workplace. He covers parenting, technology, social media dopamine and what that means for managers.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it for its take on managing young employees and on how technology and approval-seeking affect behavior.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"millennials in the workplace\",\"categories\":[\"Management, leadership & organizations\",\"Psychology & human behavior\"],\"tags\":[\"simon sinek\",\"management\",\"millennials\",\"social media\",\"workplace culture\"],\"quote\":\"Simon Sinek explains it perfectly\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"hUyMNyrOHJQ\",\"title\":\"exercises for tendinitis (tendonitis) and carpal tunnel (cps)\",\"channel\":\"David Kuckhermann\",\"summary\":\"A short video of stretching and hand exercises for preventing and easing tendinitis and carpal tunnel syndrome, from a percussion teacher. The exercises were shown to him by a doctor.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently recommended in RSI and wrist pain threads by people who say the stretches relieved their own symptoms.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"rsi hand exercises\",\"categories\":[\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"carpal tunnel\",\"tendinitis\",\"rsi\",\"stretching\",\"ergonomics\"],\"quote\":\"This video saved me from debilitating hand and wrist pain.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"hlrv7DIHllE\",\"title\":\"College Lecture Series - Neil Postman - \\\"The Surrender of Culture to Technology\\\"\",\"channel\":\"College of DuPage\",\"summary\":\"Neil Postman's 1997 lecture at College of DuPage, based on his book, argues that Americans treat technology as a national religion and depend on it to solve larger human problems. It includes audience questions.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it to engineers as a skeptical counterweight to technology boosterism, especially for those building AI and consumer tech features.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"technology and culture\",\"categories\":[\"Futurism & technology and society\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Philosophy & ethics\"],\"tags\":[\"neil postman\",\"technopoly\",\"media criticism\",\"technology critique\",\"education\"],\"quote\":\"I suggest the people working on those features watch Neil Postman's \\\"The Surrender of Culture to Technology\\\"\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"iD9xk3SDSYc\",\"title\":\"The Black Lotus - Eon - Amiga Demo (50 FPS)\",\"channel\":\"RetroDemoScene\",\"summary\":\"Eon is a demoscene production by The Black Lotus, released at Revision 2019 and designed for an Amiga with OCS chipset, 512 kB chip and 512 kB fast memory. This is the party version, which has some known glitches.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN holds it up as a showcase of what a stock Amiga 500 can do and as one of the best demos ever in technique and storytelling.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"amiga demoscene\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\"],\"tags\":[\"amiga\",\"demoscene\",\"the black lotus\",\"retro computing\",\"revision 2019\"],\"quote\":\"I feel like this is one of the best demos of all time from both a technical point of view plus storytelling.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"iGFqfTCL2fs\",\"title\":\"Traffic Waves\",\"channel\":\"wbeaty\",\"summary\":\"A short video from the trafficwaves.org site showing how traffic jams and standing waves form, including the fight between cheaters and blockers at an exit-lane backup. It also touches on how individual driving behavior affects the flow.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN likes it as a fun, clear explainer of traffic waves and of the benefits of leaving gaps while driving.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"traffic waves\",\"categories\":[\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Transit, cities & urban planning\"],\"tags\":[\"traffic jams\",\"standing waves\",\"driving\",\"physics\",\"zen driving\"],\"quote\":\"One of my favorite videos of all time on this topic: Traffic Waves\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"iukBMY4apvI\",\"title\":\"Javascript in 2015\",\"channel\":\"Glen Maddern\",\"summary\":\"A short screencast tour of JSPM, SystemJS and ES6 features, building a small app with the then-new JavaScript module and package tooling. It shows how JSPM works as a package manager, bundler and loader.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters credit it with opening their eyes to JSPM and using it to choose build systems.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"javascript build tooling\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"jspm\",\"systemjs\",\"es6\",\"modules\",\"bundlers\"],\"quote\":\"This video really opened my eyes to what it could do\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"jAhiMCSvtCc\",\"title\":\"The Great Wave - Part 1\",\"channel\":\"David Bull\",\"summary\":\"The first part of a long series documenting Dave Bull's Tokyo woodblock workshop as it produces a reproduction of Hokusai's The Great Wave. It follows the carving, proofing and printing of the reward print for Kickstarter backers.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters hold it up as a top-tier long-form process video, recommended whenever the Great Wave or Hokusai comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"woodblock printmaking\",\"categories\":[\"Visual arts, photography & architecture\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"hokusai\",\"great wave\",\"ukiyo-e\",\"craft process\",\"japanese prints\"],\"quote\":\"The series is up there with Clickspring's skeleton clock in my pantheon of \\\"greatest long-form process videos\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"jHP942Livy0\",\"title\":\"Your dishwasher is better than you think (tips, tricks, and how they work)\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connections\",\"summary\":\"Technology Connections explains how dishwashers work and how to use them well, including detergent use, the prewash cycle and why pre-rinsing hurts. It follows his earlier dishwasher videos.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people recommend it as the definitive, thorough guide to getting dishwashers to clean properly and often send it to friends.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"dishwasher operation\",\"categories\":[\"Home, DIY & practical skills\",\"Engineering & making (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"technology connections\",\"dishwasher\",\"detergent\",\"household appliances\"],\"quote\":\"I’ve sent dozens of people the Technology Connections video on this topic to rave reviews\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"jwG_qR6XmDQ\",\"title\":\"Jeff Bezos - Regret Minimization Framework\",\"channel\":\"Micky Thompson\",\"summary\":\"A three-minute clip of Jeff Bezos describing his Regret Minimization Framework: imagining yourself at 80 to decide whether to leave a Wall Street job to start Amazon.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it whenever someone asks whether to quit a job or start a company, as a simple decision heuristic.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"career decision making\",\"categories\":[\"Startups & entrepreneurship\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"jeff bezos\",\"regret minimization\",\"startups\",\"quitting your job\",\"decision framework\"],\"quote\":\"Hearing Bezos's regret minimization framework was one of the things that helped to make an easier decision\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"kU3BceoMuaA\",\"title\":\"MiniDisc - An Appreciation\",\"channel\":\"Techmoan\",\"summary\":\"Techmoan's 45-minute retrospective on Sony's MiniDisc, covering the first and last machines, ATRAC, MD-LP, Net-MD and HiMD. It argues the format deserves more credit than its US failure narrative suggests.\",\"why_hn\":\"The standard recommendation for MiniDisc history, especially as a non-American counterpoint to the failed-format story.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"minidisc history\",\"categories\":[\"Audio & video engineering\",\"Consumer devices & gadgets\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"minidisc\",\"sony\",\"atrac\",\"retro tech\",\"audio formats\"],\"quote\":\"Wonderful video about the format\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"kUfF2MTnqAw\",\"title\":\"Why You Should Put YOUR MASK On First (My Brain Without Oxygen) - Smarter Every Day 157\",\"channel\":\"SmarterEveryDay\",\"summary\":\"Destin Sandlin of Smarter Every Day undergoes hypoxia training and experiences his own impaired thinking as oxygen is withdrawn. The video shows how quickly and unnoticeably oxygen deprivation degrades judgment, illustrating why you put your own mask on first.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the go-to demonstration of how hypoxia silently disables decision making, cited in threads on altitude, flying, diving, CO2 and breathwork.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"hypoxia\",\"categories\":[\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\",\"Neuroscience & the brain\"],\"tags\":[\"hypoxia\",\"aviation safety\",\"oxygen deprivation\",\"cognition\",\"smarter every day\"],\"quote\":\"has a wonderful video demonstrating this effect [0]. I’d recommend watching the whole thing\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"kZA9Hnp3aV4\",\"title\":\"Exponential Growth Arithmetic, Population and Energy, Dr. Albert A. Bartlett\",\"channel\":\"Hiroob\",\"summary\":\"A recording of physicist Albert Bartlett's well-known lecture on the arithmetic of exponential growth, applied to population and energy use. He argues that steady percentage growth in a finite world cannot continue and sneaks up suddenly.\",\"why_hn\":\"Treated as the classic lecture on why people misjudge exponential growth, and pulled out in threads on compound growth, epidemics, crypto and resource limits.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"exponential growth\",\"categories\":[\"University lectures & courses\",\"Pure mathematics\",\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\"],\"tags\":[\"albert bartlett\",\"population\",\"energy\",\"sustainability\",\"compound growth\"],\"quote\":\"The point is well illustrated in the talk, I highly recommend it.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"l70iRcSxqzo\",\"title\":\"Kim Nilsson - Cracking MtGox\",\"channel\":\"TheBitcoinArmy\",\"summary\":\"Kim Nilsson presents his investigation into how Mt. Gox was hacked and mismanaged, tracing the finances and the missing bitcoins. It was given at the 2017 Breaking Bitcoin meetup.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call it one of the best, most unbiased explanations of what actually happened to Mt. Gox and its missing coins.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"mt. gox collapse\",\"categories\":[\"Cryptography\",\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\"],\"tags\":[\"bitcoin\",\"mt gox\",\"exchange hack\",\"forensics\",\"cryptocurrency\"],\"quote\":\"One of the best summaries of the Mt Gox incident, as well as the fallout and subsequent investigations that I've watched.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"lnnsDi7Sxq0\",\"title\":\"Classic Game Postmortem: Ultima Online\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"A 2018 GDC panel where Raph Koster, Starr Long, Richard Garriott and Rich Vogel look back on what went right and wrong building and running Ultima Online. They share candid stories from the early days, including player behavior and server architecture.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a transparent MMO postmortem and as the source of the story that the term 'shard' originated with Ultima Online.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"mmo game development\",\"categories\":[\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Game design\",\"Distributed systems\"],\"tags\":[\"ultima online\",\"postmortem\",\"gdc\",\"sharding\",\"mmo\",\"game history\"],\"quote\":\"This is a much watch if MMOs fascinate you. They're very transparent. Absolutely great video.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"mVVNJKv9esE\",\"title\":\"Cheng Lou - On the Spectrum of Abstraction at react-europe 2016\",\"channel\":\"ReactEurope\",\"summary\":\"Cheng Lou's react-europe 2016 talk lays out a mental framework for thinking about abstraction levels in JavaScript tooling, from Angular and Grunt to Webpack and React. It uses that framework to reason about the cost of abstractions and how to choose between competing solutions.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as thought-provoking material on the tradeoffs and costs of abstraction, especially in frontend tooling debates.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software abstraction\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"react\",\"abstraction\",\"frontend tooling\",\"api design\",\"javascript\"],\"quote\":\"This talk is incredible food for thought\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"mZyvIHYn2zk\",\"title\":\"The Pervert's Guide to Computer Programming Languages - SXSW\",\"channel\":\"Vulk Coop\",\"summary\":\"A SXSW talk by W Watson that uses Lacanian psychoanalysis, with nods to Zizek, to critique popular programming languages. It argues that people choose and defend languages for emotional and ideological reasons rather than rational ones.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an entertaining and eye-opening look at the irrational psychology behind language preferences, though some note it is not scientifically precise.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"psychology of programming languages\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Software (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"psychoanalysis\",\"lacan\",\"programming languages\",\"developer culture\",\"sxsw\"],\"quote\":\"one of the best and eye-opening talks I've ever heard\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"nYkdrAPrdcw\",\"title\":\"Hacker Way: Rethinking Web App Development at Facebook\",\"channel\":\"Meta Developers\",\"summary\":\"Facebook engineers explain why they abandoned the traditional MVC paradigm for a more functional architecture, introducing Flux and React. They use the unread-message-count bug as a case study of the problems of bidirectional data flow at scale.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the original talk that explains the motivation behind React, Flux and Redux, and that sold some commenters on React.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"flux and react architecture\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Software architecture & design\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"react\",\"flux\",\"mvc\",\"redux\",\"facebook\",\"state management\"],\"quote\":\"these talks, they are the ones that sold me on React\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"o0fG_lnVhHw\",\"title\":\"HOW ROCKETS ARE MADE (Rocket Factory Tour - United Launch Alliance) - Smarter Every Day 231\",\"channel\":\"SmarterEveryDay\",\"summary\":\"Destin Sandlin tours United Launch Alliance's rocket factory with CEO Tory Bruno, showing friction stir welding, structural rocket skins and large milled parts.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a rare look inside a rocket factory and cites it for manufacturing details like welding methods.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"rocket manufacturing\",\"categories\":[\"Manufacturing & industrial processes\",\"Space exploration & spaceflight\"],\"tags\":[\"ula\",\"rockets\",\"friction stir welding\",\"factory tour\",\"tory bruno\",\"manufacturing\"],\"quote\":\"If you haven't watched Smarter Every Day's ULA factory tour I highly recommend it.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"o4-YnLpLgtk\",\"title\":\"Symbolics Lisp Machine demo Jan 2013\",\"channel\":\"Kalman Reti\",\"summary\":\"Symbolics developer Kalman Reti gives a short introductory demo of a Symbolics Lisp Machine running Genera in an emulator. It shows the context-aware command line, typed on-screen objects, and the live-editable system, including swapping in a C-compiled JPEG decoder.\",\"why_hn\":\"The go-to demo whenever HN discusses what Lisp machines were like and what modern shells and editors lack.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"lisp machines\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Functional programming\",\"GUI, HCI & interface history\"],\"tags\":[\"symbolics\",\"genera\",\"lisp\",\"repl\",\"operating systems\",\"user interfaces\"],\"quote\":\"It makes working on VSCode today look like banging rocks together\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"ovJcsL7vyrk\",\"title\":\"This equation will change how you see the world (the logistic map)\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"A Veritasium video on the logistic map, showing how a simple population equation leads to chaos and bifurcation. It connects the idea to convection, neuron firing and the Mandelbrot set, with interactive animations.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call it a favourite Veritasium video, praised for how a simple equation reveals surprisingly complex patterns.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"chaos theory\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"logistic map\",\"bifurcation\",\"mandelbrot set\",\"population dynamics\",\"fractals\"],\"quote\":\"But this is the one I think people on HN need to watch most\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"pOmXiapfCs8\",\"title\":\"KGB Defector Yuri Bezmenov 1985 Interview. Explains KGB Manipulation of US Public Opinion\",\"channel\":\"20th Century Time Machine \",\"summary\":\"A 1985 interview by G. Edward Griffin with former KGB operative Yuri Bezmenov. He describes long-running Soviet efforts at ideological subversion and manipulation of US public opinion.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters bring it up in threads on propaganda, influence operations and platforms like TikTok, saying it feels more relevant than ever.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ideological subversion\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"World history\",\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\"],\"tags\":[\"yuri bezmenov\",\"kgb\",\"propaganda\",\"psychological operations\",\"cold war\"],\"quote\":\"I feel like everyone should watch this 1985 interview of an ex-KGB agent[1]. It's more relevant today than ever before\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"pYXy-A4siMw\",\"title\":\"Intro to AI Safety, Remastered\",\"channel\":\"Robert Miles AI Safety \",\"summary\":\"Robert Miles gives a remastered introduction to AI safety, adapted from a talk in London. It lays out why advanced AI could go wrong and answers common objections.\",\"why_hn\":\"The default link for newcomers wanting an approachable, entertaining introduction to AI risk and alignment.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"ai safety\",\"categories\":[\"AI safety, AGI & impact\"],\"tags\":[\"ai alignment\",\"ai risk\",\"agi\",\"objective functions\",\"robert miles\"],\"quote\":\"It's quite an approachable intro, often entertaining\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"pkCLMl0e_0k\",\"title\":\"David Beazley - Lambda Calculus from the Ground Up - PyCon 2019\",\"channel\":\"PyCon 2019\",\"summary\":\"David Beazley's PyCon 2019 tutorial derives the lambda calculus from the ground up in Python, using only single-argument functions. He builds booleans, numerals and more with no other language features.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN calls it a brilliant, mind-bending introduction to lambda calculus for programmers.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"lambda calculus\",\"categories\":[\"Computability & complexity theory\",\"Functional programming\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"lambda calculus\",\"python\",\"functional programming\",\"david beazley\",\"pycon\"],\"quote\":\"David Beazley gives a brilliant introduction to lambda calculus here\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"qbIk7-JPB2c\",\"title\":\"Sparks of AGI: early experiments with GPT-4\",\"channel\":\"Sebastien Bubeck\",\"summary\":\"Sebastien Bubeck presents the Sparks of AGI paper at MIT, showing early experiments with GPT-4 across many domains and asking whether artificial intelligence has arrived. It includes the TikZ unicorn example.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a thought-provoking talk from a researcher with early GPT-4 access, especially for the unicorn degrading under safety tuning.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"gpt-4 capabilities\",\"categories\":[\"LLMs & generative AI\",\"AI safety, AGI & impact\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"gpt-4\",\"agi\",\"llm\",\"alignment tax\",\"microsoft research\"],\"quote\":\"Highly highly highly recommend listen to this lecture by Sebastien Bubeck\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"qp0HIF3SfI4\",\"title\":\"How Great Leaders Inspire Action | Simon Sinek | TED\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"Simon Sinek's TED talk presents the 'golden circle' model: great leaders and companies inspire action by starting with why they do something, before how or what. He illustrates it with Apple, Martin Luther King, the Wright brothers and, as a counterexample, TiVo.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it to explain why customers buy into a company's beliefs rather than its specs, especially in threads about Apple, marketing and product messaging.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"leadership and marketing\",\"categories\":[\"Management, leadership & organizations\",\"Product, marketing & sales\"],\"tags\":[\"start with why\",\"golden circle\",\"apple\",\"marketing\",\"leadership\",\"ted\"],\"quote\":\"I think this video by Simon Sinek explains very well why Apple is successful.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"qsEjV8DdSbs\",\"title\":\"Gimbal UFO - A New Analysis\",\"channel\":\"Mick West\",\"summary\":\"Mick West analyzes the Navy's Gimbal UFO video and gives four checkable observations suggesting it is an infrared glare that appears to rotate because the camera rotates while tracking. He argues the object is likely a hot craft hidden behind the glare.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to as the clearest, evidence-based debunk of the Gimbal footage, and a model of how to analyze such claims.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ufo video analysis\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Religion, belief & skepticism\",\"Military history & warfare\"],\"tags\":[\"gimbal ufo\",\"infrared camera\",\"lens glare\",\"debunking\",\"uap\"],\"quote\":\"The summary video is concise and only 20 minutes long. I challenge you to watch all of it and respond specifically.\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"qybUFnY7Y8w\",\"title\":\"OK Go - This Too Shall Pass - Rube Goldberg Machine - Official Video\",\"channel\":\"OK Go\",\"summary\":\"OK Go's music video for This Too Shall Pass, featuring a huge Rube Goldberg machine built in a warehouse in Los Angeles with Syyn Labs. The band interacts with the contraption in one continuous take.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as an example of ambitious, practical-effects creativity in music videos, made without CGI.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"music video\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Magic, puzzles & curiosities\"],\"tags\":[\"ok go\",\"rube goldberg\",\"practical effects\",\"creativity\"],\"quote\":\"The Rube Goldberg machine [0] one is particularily impressive.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"rQmqVVmMB3k\",\"title\":\"The \$100 Billion Dollar Ingredient making your Food Toxic\",\"channel\":\"Joseph Everett - WIL\",\"summary\":\"A What I've Learned video arguing that industrial seed and vegetable oils have displaced saturated fats and harmed health. It covers the history of vegetable oils, the American Heart Association's role, and rising consumption.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as an accessible summary of the case against seed oils, and some say it changed what fats they cook with.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"seed oils and nutrition\",\"categories\":[\"Nutrition & diet science\"],\"tags\":[\"seed oils\",\"vegetable oils\",\"omega-6\",\"nutrition\",\"heart association\"],\"quote\":\"one of the most important ones imo being the one on seed oils\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"re96UgMk6GQ\",\"title\":\"Simon Peyton-Jones: Escape from the ivory tower: the Haskell journey\",\"channel\":\"Churchill College, University of Cambridge\",\"summary\":\"Simon Peyton Jones gives Churchill College's annual computer science lecture on Haskell's birth and evolution. He covers the most influential ideas, from laziness to monads and type system evolution, plus current developments and guesses about the future.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a funny, accessible history of Haskell that is valuable even for people interested in language design in general.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"haskell history\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"haskell\",\"functional programming\",\"monads\",\"language design\",\"laziness\"],\"quote\":\"It's a funny talk and it's worth watching in full\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"rnmcRTnTNC8\",\"title\":\"I'll Let Myself In: Tactics of Physical Pen Testers\",\"channel\":\"Wild West Hackin' Fest\",\"summary\":\"Deviant Ollam, head of a physical penetration testing team, presents tactics for getting into buildings, from bypassing door levers with a wire to defeating bank doors. He argues that physical security is often surprisingly weak.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an entertaining overview of physical pen testing, and cites it when threads discuss how easily doors and locks are bypassed.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"physical penetration testing\",\"categories\":[\"Security & hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"deviant ollam\",\"physical security\",\"lock bypass\",\"social engineering\",\"pentesting\"],\"quote\":\"Deviant Ollam's talks are always first class.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"rvkEpstd9os\",\"title\":\"The Boeing 787: Broken Dreams l Al Jazeera Investigations\",\"channel\":\"Al Jazeera English\",\"summary\":\"An Al Jazeera investigation into Boeing's 787 Dreamliner, featuring current and former engineers who voice safety concerns. It covers quality control problems at the South Carolina plant and the roots of the battery problems that grounded the plane.\",\"why_hn\":\"Boeing threads cite it as early evidence of deep cultural and quality problems at Boeing, years before the 737 MAX crashes.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"boeing 787 manufacturing quality\",\"categories\":[\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Manufacturing & industrial processes\"],\"tags\":[\"boeing\",\"787 dreamliner\",\"aviation safety\",\"investigative journalism\",\"quality control\"],\"quote\":\"AlJazeera deserves respect for this excellent footage they got from inside the factory.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"rvskMHn0sqQ\",\"title\":\"A Selfish Argument for Making the World a Better Place – Egoistic Altruism\",\"channel\":\"Kurzgesagt – In a Nutshell\",\"summary\":\"Kurzgesagt makes a selfish case for improving the world, arguing that helping people far away raises prosperity and innovation for everyone, including yourself. The animated video frames this as egoistic altruism and positive-sum cooperation.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters link it to argue for cooperation and engagement over isolation, and as an accessible statement of positive-sum thinking.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"positive-sum cooperation\",\"categories\":[\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\",\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Futurism & technology and society\"],\"tags\":[\"altruism\",\"kurzgesagt\",\"global development\",\"economics\",\"cooperation\"],\"quote\":\"There is a video [0] I love that explains an argument for this well.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"t7tA3NNKF0Q\",\"title\":\"How Facebook is Stealing Billions of Views\",\"channel\":\"Kurzgesagt – In a Nutshell\",\"summary\":\"Kurzgesagt argues that Facebook's claimed 8 billion daily video views are inflated by freebooting, meaning re-uploads of creators' videos. It describes how this harms independent creators.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it to explain freebooting, Facebook's inflated view metrics and why Content ID matters for small creators.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"facebook video freebooting\",\"categories\":[\"Tech industry & business models\",\"Internet culture & memes\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"facebook\",\"freebooting\",\"content id\",\"video metrics\",\"creators\"],\"quote\":\"This video changed my opinion on Content ID when I saw it last year\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"tUAX0gnZ3Nw\",\"title\":\"\\\"Games as a service\\\" is fraud.\",\"channel\":\"Accursed Farms\",\"summary\":\"Ross Scott of Accursed Farms makes a 76-minute argument that selling video games as a service is a form of consumer fraud. He lays out legal arguments that games are goods, covers ownership and programmed obsolescence, and discusses game preservation.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the go-to explanation of why games that die when servers shut down harm consumers, and it is repeatedly cited in Stop Killing Games and game-delisting threads.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"games as a service\",\"categories\":[\"Gaming culture & analysis\",\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Game history & retro gaming\"],\"tags\":[\"game preservation\",\"consumer rights\",\"drm\",\"server shutdowns\",\"ownership\"],\"quote\":\"Just watch this video so that you get up to speed with what we are talking about\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"tWr39Q9vBgo\",\"title\":\"Richard Feynman on Pseudoscience\",\"channel\":\"Jacob Edward\",\"summary\":\"A two-minute clip of Richard Feynman explaining why he considers much of social science pseudoscience. He stresses how easy it is to fool yourself and how much care real knowledge takes.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters post it whenever a thread touches on soft sciences, replication problems, or what counts as real science.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"scientific method\",\"categories\":[\"Scientific method & science communication\",\"Philosophy & ethics\"],\"tags\":[\"feynman\",\"pseudoscience\",\"social science\",\"replication\",\"epistemics\"],\"quote\":\"it's very hard to know something and you have to be really careful that you've done the work and aren't just deluding yourself\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"uL2D3qzHtqY\",\"title\":\"A JVM Does That?\",\"channel\":\"Google TechTalks\",\"summary\":\"Cliff Click of Azul Systems asks what a JVM is supposed to do, in a Google Tech Talk from 2011. He covers the services a JVM provides, such as garbage collection and compilation, and the messy reality beneath the abstractions.\",\"why_hn\":\"A repeated recommendation for anyone interested in VMs, with Click's talks regarded as unusually informative on JVMs, JITs and the user/kernel boundary.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"jvm internals\",\"categories\":[\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"jvm\",\"garbage collection\",\"jit compilation\",\"cliff click\",\"azul\"],\"quote\":\"It's well worth watching if you are interested in vms at all.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"uOSnQM1Zu4w\",\"title\":\"What Is A Black Start Of The Power Grid?\",\"channel\":\"Practical Engineering\",\"summary\":\"Practical Engineering explains what a black start is: restarting a power grid after total collapse. It shows why most plants need outside power to start and why rebuilding a synchronized grid is delicate and high-stakes.\",\"why_hn\":\"Go-to reference whenever a major blackout happens, recommended as an excellent explanation of why black starts are hard.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"power grid black start\",\"categories\":[\"Power grid, heating & buildings\"],\"tags\":[\"blackout\",\"power grid\",\"black start\",\"grid restoration\",\"infrastructure\"],\"quote\":\"Practical Engineering did a really great video a few years ago on why black starts are hard\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"uk3A41U0iO4\",\"title\":\"clojure.core/typing - Matt Adereth\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"Matt Adereth's Clojure/conj talk covers the history of keyboards and how he used Clojure generating OpenSCAD to design and build his custom Dactyl ergonomic keyboard, including 3D printing and laser-cut parts.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a fantastic talk on code-driven CAD and custom keyboard design, linked in threads on OpenSCAD and keyboards.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"code-driven keyboard design\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"3D printing, CNC & maker projects\",\"Consumer devices & gadgets\"],\"tags\":[\"clojure\",\"openscad\",\"ergonomic keyboards\",\"3d printing\",\"dactyl\"],\"quote\":\"it is a fantastic talk but the part related to this starts at 17:35\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"vYA0f6R5KAI\",\"title\":\"GPUs to Mars: Full-Scale Simulation of SpaceX's Mars Rocket Engine\",\"channel\":\"insideHPC Report\",\"summary\":\"SpaceX engineers present at NVIDIA's GPU Technology Conference on a multi-physics code that simulates the full-scale Raptor methane engine. It covers GPU-based turbulent combustion modeling with adaptive grids and wavelet compression.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as evidence of SpaceX's serious simulation capability and as a showcase of GPU compute for CFD.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"rocket engine simulation\",\"categories\":[\"Space exploration & spaceflight\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Computer graphics & rendering\"],\"tags\":[\"cfd\",\"gpu computing\",\"spacex\",\"raptor\",\"combustion\"],\"quote\":\"This talk was given at NVIDIA GTC 2015 and inspired me to go into manufacturing\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"vwCdKU9uYnE\",\"title\":\"Avie Tevanian: CMU math whiz goes from Mach to NeXT to Apple Software Chief | Oral History Part 1\",\"channel\":\"Computer History Museum\",\"summary\":\"A Computer History Museum oral history with Avie Tevanian covering his math talent, work on the Mach microkernel at CMU, joining NeXT, leading NEXTSTEP development and the 1996 Apple acquisition.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a long, rich first-hand account of Mach, NeXT and Apple history and of working for Steve Jobs.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"mach, next and apple history\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Operating systems & kernels\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"avie tevanian\",\"mach\",\"nextstep\",\"steve jobs\",\"computer history\"],\"quote\":\"Must watch recollection of the history of Mach and NeXT and Apple by Avie Tevanian.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"xRel1JKOEbI\",\"title\":\"The Neuroscience of Consciousness – with Anil Seth\",\"channel\":\"The Royal Institution\",\"summary\":\"Neuroscientist Anil Seth gives a Royal Institution lecture on the science of consciousness. He separates conscious level, content and self, and argues that experience arises from the brain's predictive inference.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a clear, in-depth lecture on consciousness as a process of prediction.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"neuroscience of consciousness\",\"categories\":[\"Consciousness & cognition\",\"Neuroscience & the brain\"],\"tags\":[\"consciousness\",\"predictive processing\",\"anil seth\",\"brain\",\"royal institution\"],\"quote\":\"And The Neuroscience of Consciousness – with Anil Seth is really great\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"y71lli8MS8s\",\"title\":\"I made a NES emulator in 15 minutes\",\"channel\":\"Bisqwit\",\"summary\":\"Bisqwit writes a NES emulator in C++ in about 15 minutes of tool-assisted, sped-up coding, narrated with captions. It shows an accurate emulator built with a compile-time generated 6502 instruction table. Part 2 covers compiling and running it.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN programmers admire it as a clean, clever demonstration of emulator design and as the standout of a favorite programming YouTuber.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"emulator programming\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"nes\",\"6502\",\"c++\",\"emulation\",\"live coding\",\"opcode dispatch\"],\"quote\":\"Here's a very clean and clever NES emulator in C++ by Bisqwit of TAS fame\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"yErKTVdETpw\",\"title\":\"FULL INTERVIEW with Yuri Bezmenov: The Four Stages of Ideological Subversion (1984)\",\"channel\":\"Nicholas Marshall\",\"summary\":\"The full 1984 interview with Yuri Bezmenov, a former KGB operative, in which he lays out four stages of ideological subversion. It is a long interview with G. Edward Griffin.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in propaganda and disinformation threads, though some commenters present it more as a prediction of current events than established analysis.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ideological subversion\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\",\"World history\"],\"tags\":[\"yuri bezmenov\",\"kgb\",\"propaganda\",\"cold war\",\"disinformation\"],\"quote\":\"It is worth watching the whole interview\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"yQ16_YxLbB8\",\"title\":\"Complete Apple WWDC | Steve Jobs talk and answer developers questions | 1997\",\"channel\":\"TheAppleFanBoy - Apple & Computer Archives\",\"summary\":\"The full 1997 WWDC session in which Steve Jobs, newly back at Apple, takes developer questions for about an hour. He discusses OpenDoc, Microsoft, clones, Newton, advertising and Apple's strategy.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN readers recommend it as an unfiltered view of Jobs' strategic thinking and decisiveness, including the famous answer on why OpenDoc was killed.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"apple strategy under steve jobs\",\"categories\":[\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Tech company stories & documentaries\"],\"tags\":[\"steve jobs\",\"wwdc 1997\",\"opendoc\",\"apple history\",\"q&a\"],\"quote\":\"For those that haven't seen it the whole thing is worth it\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"ymaWq5yZIYM\",\"title\":\"ADL's Never Is Now 2019 | ADL International Leadership Award Presented to Sacha Baron Cohen\",\"channel\":\"Anti-Defamation League\",\"summary\":\"Sacha Baron Cohen's speech accepting the ADL International Leadership Award, in which he criticizes social media companies for amplifying hate and propaganda. He argues that freedom of speech is not freedom of reach.\",\"why_hn\":\"Quoted heavily in moderation and free speech debates, particularly the line about freedom of speech and reach.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"social media responsibility\",\"categories\":[\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"sacha baron cohen\",\"content moderation\",\"free speech\",\"misinformation\",\"facebook\"],\"quote\":\"This is a thought-provoking speech. Do yourself a favor and watch it in its entirety\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"zduSFxRajkE\",\"title\":\"Let's build the GPT Tokenizer\",\"channel\":\"Andrej Karpathy\",\"summary\":\"Andrej Karpathy builds the GPT tokenizer from scratch, implementing byte pair encoding along with encode and decode. He shows how many odd LLM behaviors trace back to tokenization, and covers Unicode byte encodings along the way.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the best deep dive on tokenizers, worth the two hours for anyone working with LLMs.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"llm tokenization\",\"categories\":[\"LLMs & generative AI\",\"Computer vision, speech & NLP\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\"],\"tags\":[\"byte pair encoding\",\"tokenizer\",\"gpt\",\"unicode\",\"llm\"],\"quote\":\"Worth spending 2h for the deeper understanding if you deal with LLMs.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.5},{\"vid\":\"-FENubmZGj8\",\"title\":\"\\\"You Have the Right to Remain Innocent\\\" (James Duane)\",\"channel\":\"The Cato Institute\",\"summary\":\"Law professor James Duane explains why you should not talk to the police even if you are innocent, drawing on case histories of wrongful convictions. It is a short version of the argument in his book on the right to remain silent.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters send it to anyone who thinks innocent people have nothing to fear from answering police questions.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"rights when questioned by police\",\"categories\":[\"Law, policing & justice\"],\"tags\":[\"self-incrimination\",\"fifth amendment\",\"police interrogation\",\"law\",\"wrongful conviction\"],\"quote\":\"this is a very important video for everyone to watch\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"-Ht4qiDRZE8\",\"title\":\"Massive-scale online collaboration | Luis von Ahn\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"Luis von Ahn, creator of CAPTCHA and reCAPTCHA, explains how small contributions from many people online can digitize books. He then introduces Duolingo as a way to translate the web while people learn languages.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as the clearest account of human computation and games with a purpose, straight from the inventor.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"crowdsourcing and human computation\",\"categories\":[\"Futurism & technology and society\",\"Computer vision, speech & NLP\",\"Ideas & learning (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"recaptcha\",\"captcha\",\"duolingo\",\"crowdsourcing\",\"book digitization\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend watching the TED talk by Luis von Ahn, creater of CAPTCHA and reCAPTCHA.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"-iCIZQX9i1A\",\"title\":\"I Watched Ancient Apocalypse So You Don't Have To (Part 1)\",\"channel\":\"Miniminuteman\",\"summary\":\"Miniminuteman begins a long, humorous point-by-point debunking of Graham Hancock's Netflix series Ancient Apocalypse and its claims of a lost ice-age civilization. It is part one of a multi-part critique.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points people to it as an entertaining, thorough takedown of Hancock's pseudoarchaeology whenever his claims come up.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"pseudoarchaeology debunking\",\"categories\":[\"Religion, belief & skepticism\",\"World history\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"graham hancock\",\"ancient apocalypse\",\"archaeology\",\"debunking\",\"netflix\"],\"quote\":\"lengthy, point-by-point takedown of the Ancient Apocalypse thing both entertaining and informative\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"04ERSb06dOg\",\"title\":\"Noether's Theorem and The Symmetries of Reality\",\"channel\":\"PBS Space Time\",\"summary\":\"PBS Space Time explains Noether's theorem: conservation laws such as energy and momentum are consequences of deeper symmetries. It also covers why those laws hold only under certain conditions.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as an approachable explanation of how symmetries give rise to conservation laws, including the story of Emmy Noether.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"noether's theorem\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\",\"Quantum physics\"],\"tags\":[\"noether's theorem\",\"symmetry\",\"conservation laws\",\"theoretical physics\",\"emmy noether\"],\"quote\":\"It's such an aha moment.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"0lvUByM-fZk\",\"title\":\"Cities: Skylines | Power, Politics, & Planning: Episode 1: Parking\",\"channel\":\"donoteat01\",\"summary\":\"The first episode of a Cities: Skylines series on urban politics and planning, focused on parking. It uses the game to show how parking minimums shape cities, drawing on Donald Shoup's book The High Cost of Free Parking.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a clear illustration of how parking requirements produce car-centered, unwalkable American urban design.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"parking and urban planning\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Gaming culture & analysis\"],\"tags\":[\"parking minimums\",\"cities skylines\",\"urban design\",\"walkability\",\"donald shoup\"],\"quote\":\"this video (shared recently in another HN discussion) is a _fantastic_ illustration of the problems of parking minimums\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"1PhArSujR_A\",\"title\":\"John Carmack's keynote at Quakecon 2013 part 4\",\"channel\":\"Kostiantyn Kostin\",\"summary\":\"Part 4 of John Carmack's QuakeCon 2013 keynote, covering OpenGL, functional programming, Haskell, Lisp and Scheme, typing, multithreading, events and garbage collection.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited again and again as Carmack's key public statement on functional programming and purity in game development, plus his remarks on garbage collection.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"functional programming in games\",\"categories\":[\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Functional programming\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"john carmack\",\"haskell\",\"functional programming\",\"garbage collection\",\"game engines\",\"quakecon\"],\"quote\":\"There's an old video [0] where he talks in-depth about his explorations of functional programming.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"1jHUY3qoBu8\",\"title\":\"Learn Low Poly Modeling in Blender 2.9 / 2.8\",\"channel\":\"Imphenzia\",\"summary\":\"Imphenzia's 83-minute tutorial covers the low poly modeling techniques he learned over five years of Blender use, with timestamps from navigation and extrusion onward. It uses Blender 2.83 with default keys.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the most-recommended beginner Blender tutorial on HN, with several people saying it made them comfortable in a couple of hours.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"blender low poly modeling\",\"categories\":[\"Animation & visual effects\",\"Indie game development\"],\"tags\":[\"blender\",\"low poly\",\"3d modeling\",\"beginner tutorial\",\"game art\"],\"quote\":\"until this tut by Imphenzia, which made me comfortable in under 2 hours\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"1uflg7LDmzI\",\"title\":\"Building a Better Web Browser - James Mickens - Harvard CS Colloquium 2015\",\"channel\":\"Paul Irish\",\"summary\":\"James Mickens's 2015 Harvard colloquium argues that browsers are bloated and insecure because feature races trump clean interface design. He presents a cleaner, more secure browser architecture (Atlantis) with a much smaller API surface.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it whenever people discuss rebuilding the web platform or what a better browser architecture could look like.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"browser architecture\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Software architecture & design\",\"Security & hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"james mickens\",\"web browsers\",\"atlantis\",\"web security\",\"api design\",\"rendering\"],\"quote\":\"which I definitely think this still the most forward-looking browser architecture out there\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"1xQeXOz0Ncs\",\"title\":\"Who Destroyed Three Mile Island? - Nickolas Means | The Lead Developer Austin 2018\",\"channel\":\"LeadDev\",\"summary\":\"Nickolas Means recounts the 1979 Three Mile Island accident minute by minute, from the control rod insertion to the operator's decision to switch off emergency cooling. He argues that finding systemic causes is more useful than blaming individuals.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it whenever a failure is blamed on human error, as a clear case study in looking for the systemic second story.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"incident analysis\",\"categories\":[\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\",\"Nuclear power & fusion\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"three mile island\",\"root cause\",\"human error\",\"systems failure\",\"postmortems\"],\"quote\":\"There's an excellent talk from Nick Means (GitHub) called \\\"Who Destroyed Three Mile Island?\\\".\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"2DIl3Hfh9tY\",\"title\":\"Leonard Susskind on The World As Hologram | TVO Docs\",\"channel\":\"TVO Docs\",\"summary\":\"Stanford physicist Leonard Susskind gives a public lecture on the holographic principle, the indestructibility of information and the nature of black holes. It asks whether the world could be a hologram.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as an accessible yet substantive introduction to the holographic principle and black hole information, often when related physics news comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"holographic principle\",\"categories\":[\"Cosmology & astrophysics\",\"Quantum physics\"],\"tags\":[\"black holes\",\"theoretical physics\",\"leonard susskind\",\"information paradox\",\"string theory\"],\"quote\":\"IIRC these are very good videos to start with\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"2PdJFbjWHEU\",\"title\":\"Paleo Diet & Strength Training Biochemistry | Doug McGuff M.D. | Full Length HD\",\"channel\":\"21 Studios\",\"summary\":\"Doug McGuff presents the biochemistry behind high-intensity strength training and the paleo diet, including how the body stores and burns fat. It is a roughly hour-and-a-half full-length lecture.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the theory behind Body by Science, explaining metabolism and why brief intense training works.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"high-intensity strength training\",\"categories\":[\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\",\"Nutrition & diet science\"],\"tags\":[\"doug mcguff\",\"body by science\",\"metabolism\",\"paleo diet\",\"fat storage\",\"exercise science\"],\"quote\":\"Doug McGuff covers med school biochemistry and metabolism in the last part of this presentation\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"2u6HTG8LuXQ\",\"title\":\"Nintendo - Putting Play First\",\"channel\":\"Game Maker's Toolkit\",\"summary\":\"Game Maker's Toolkit examines Nintendo's design approach of starting from a core play mechanic, making it fun, and building around it. Examples include Mario's jump and Luigi's Mansion's vacuum.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a concise video essay on why Nintendo games feel good and how the company iterates.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"game design\",\"categories\":[\"Game design\"],\"tags\":[\"nintendo\",\"game design\",\"mario\",\"iteration\",\"video essay\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend Mark Brown's video\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"36GT2zI8lVA\",\"title\":\"Richard Feynman. Why.\",\"channel\":\"firewalker\",\"summary\":\"A short clip of Richard Feynman answering an interviewer's question about why magnets repel, explaining that a 'why' question depends on a shared framework of understanding. It illustrates how hard it is to explain fundamentals to a layperson.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited constantly as the classic answer to 'why' questions, on explanation, frames of reference and the limits of physics.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"nature of scientific explanation\",\"categories\":[\"Scientific method & science communication\",\"Fundamental physics\"],\"tags\":[\"feynman\",\"why questions\",\"magnets\",\"explanation\",\"philosophy of science\"],\"quote\":\"I like to refer people to Feynman's answer to a question about why magnets repel each other[0].\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"3VZSJKbzyMc\",\"title\":\"Man Spends 30 Years Turning Degraded Land into Massive Forest – Fools & Dreamers (Full Documentary)\",\"channel\":\"Happen Films\",\"summary\":\"Fools & Dreamers is a 30-minute documentary about Hinewai Nature Reserve in New Zealand, where botanist Hugh Wilson let gorse grow as a nurse canopy to regenerate native forest. It shows the results after 30 years and the local skepticism he faced.\",\"why_hn\":\"Shared in climate and land-use threads as a vivid example of how letting land regrow can restore forest.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"forest regeneration\",\"categories\":[\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\",\"Animals & nature\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"rewilding\",\"reforestation\",\"new zealand\",\"hugh wilson\",\"land use\"],\"quote\":\"This is a great short film that goes into detail about the project and the challenges faced in getting it off the ground\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"43XaZEn2aLc\",\"title\":\"On the Expressive Power of Programming Languages by Shriram Krishnamurthi [PWLConf 2019]\",\"channel\":\"PapersWeLove\",\"summary\":\"Shriram Krishnamurthi's PWLConf 2019 talk on the formal notion of expressive power of programming languages, based on Felleisen's paper. It explains how to tell when a feature adds power by whether emulating it requires global rewriting.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the approachable way into a precise definition of language expressiveness for language-design arguments.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming language expressiveness\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"expressive power\",\"felleisen\",\"language design\",\"macros\",\"type systems\"],\"quote\":\"Shriram Krishnamurthi's talk from two years ago on this paper might be more approachable.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"4nigRT2KmCE\",\"title\":\"MC Frontalot - It Is Pitch Dark\",\"channel\":\"MC Frontalot\",\"summary\":\"MC Frontalot's nerdcore rap about the text adventure Zork, made for Jason Scott's Get Lamp documentary and featuring a cameo by Steve Meretzky. It was filmed over an evening in a basement.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN shares it whenever Zork or text adventures come up, as a fun tribute for geeks.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"zork nerdcore rap\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Computing culture (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"zork\",\"nerdcore\",\"text adventures\",\"get lamp\",\"mc frontalot\"],\"quote\":\"Now enjoy this immortal tribute\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"5hVmeOCJjOU\",\"title\":\"A Brief History of Quantum Mechanics - with Sean Carroll\",\"channel\":\"The Royal Institution\",\"summary\":\"Sean Carroll gives a Royal Institution talk tracing the history of quantum mechanics from Einstein and Bohr to today. He covers the Copenhagen interpretation and the many worlds interpretation.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an approachable entry point to quantum interpretations, and cited on many worlds questions.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"quantum mechanics interpretations\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum physics\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"quantum mechanics\",\"many worlds\",\"sean carroll\",\"copenhagen interpretation\",\"physics history\"],\"quote\":\"Sean Carroll is a master of making quantum mechanics approachable to non-physicists.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"6RhtiPefVzM\",\"title\":\"Are Electric Cars Worse For The Environment? Myth Busted\",\"channel\":\"Engineering Explained\",\"summary\":\"Engineering Explained works through whether electric cars are really worse for the environment than gasoline cars. It covers battery production emissions, the electricity mix used to charge, and how long an EV takes to break even on lifetime emissions.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters link it repeatedly as a clear, number-based rebuttal to the claim that EVs are worse for the climate than gas cars.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"electric vehicle emissions\",\"categories\":[\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\",\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\",\"Renewables, batteries & storage\"],\"tags\":[\"ev vs ice\",\"lifecycle emissions\",\"battery manufacturing\",\"grid mix\",\"climate\"],\"quote\":\"This video crunches the numbers pretty thoroughly\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"7J7X7aZvMXQ\",\"title\":\"How does Computer Memory Work? 💻🛠\",\"channel\":\"Branch Education\",\"summary\":\"A 36-minute animated deep dive from Branch Education into how DRAM works. It shows how memory connects to the CPU and how billions of bits are stored in nanoscale capacitor cells. It also covers why DRAM sits between SSDs and the processor.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters recommend it as a clear, well-animated explanation of memory, especially for visual learners.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"computer memory\",\"categories\":[\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Hardware (other)\",\"Semiconductors & chip manufacturing\"],\"tags\":[\"dram\",\"ram\",\"memory timings\",\"hardware\",\"animation\"],\"quote\":\"It is both detailed enough and clear thanks to the simplified visuals.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"7NfX0vlCa4k\",\"title\":\"E-Ink for Productivity: An Introduction\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connextras\",\"summary\":\"Technology Connections gives first impressions of using an Onyx Boox Max2 e-ink tablet for productivity, including using it as an external display. He shows what the device can and cannot do.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it, with its companion video, as an honest hands-on account of e-ink devices as monitors, including their slow software and limits.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"e-ink productivity devices\",\"categories\":[\"Consumer devices & gadgets\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"e-ink\",\"onyx boox\",\"e-paper\",\"external display\",\"gadget review\"],\"quote\":\"Technology Connections on youtube tried to use an eink display and thoroughly described his experiences.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"7Nw6qyyrTeI\",\"title\":\"Suburbia is Subsidized: Here's the Math [ST07]\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"Not Just Bikes explains, using Urban3's property-tax analysis, why car-dependent suburbs generate less tax revenue than they cost to maintain, so dense urban cores effectively subsidize them. It is part of a Strong Towns series.\",\"why_hn\":\"The go-to link in urban planning threads for the claim that suburban infrastructure costs more per resident than it pays back.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"suburban economics\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\"],\"tags\":[\"urban planning\",\"strong towns\",\"urban3\",\"sprawl\",\"municipal finance\"],\"quote\":\"Good overview of the research on this\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"7jb0FOcImdg\",\"title\":\"8 Frames in 16ms: Rollback Networking in Mortal Kombat and Injustice 2\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"A 2018 GDC talk by NetherRealm's Michael Stallone on the engine changes, optimizations and tooling needed to roll back and re-simulate up to 8 frames in 16 ms in Mortal Kombat and Injustice 2. It is a deep dive into deterministic rollback netcode.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the definitive practical talk on rollback networking in a shipped AAA fighting game.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"rollback netcode\",\"categories\":[\"Game programming & engines\",\"Networking & internet infrastructure\"],\"tags\":[\"netcode\",\"fighting games\",\"game engines\",\"determinism\",\"gdc\"],\"quote\":\"For an in-depth explanation, see this excellent GDC talk on its implementation in Mortal Kombat and Injustice 2\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"7y1xJAVZxXg\",\"title\":\"Televangelists: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO)\",\"channel\":\"LastWeekTonight\",\"summary\":\"John Oliver's Last Week Tonight segment on televangelists and the 'seed faith' prosperity gospel. It looks at how US tax law lets preachers extract money from poor viewers, and how easy it is to start a church.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it in threads on churches, tax exemption and religious fundraising, as the go-to explainer on seed faith televangelism.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"televangelism\",\"categories\":[\"Religion, belief & skepticism\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Law, policing & justice\"],\"tags\":[\"seed faith\",\"tax exemption\",\"religion\",\"john oliver\",\"fundraising\"],\"quote\":\"John Oliver did an excellent piece on a new crop of so-called \\\"seed faith\\\" televangelists\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"8TjGAWxL23c\",\"title\":\"Plagues & Pleasures on the Salton Sea | KQED Truly CA\",\"channel\":\"KQED Arts\",\"summary\":\"A KQED Truly CA documentary narrated by John Waters about the Salton Sea, a once-glamorous California vacation spot now home to an eccentric community. It covers the area's economic, political and environmental problems and features Salvation Mountain.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an engaging, funny and fascinating documentary, and cites it in threads on the Salton Sea's history and environmental decline.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"salton sea\",\"categories\":[\"Documentaries & storytelling\",\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\"],\"tags\":[\"california\",\"environmental disaster\",\"john waters\",\"salvation mountain\",\"documentary\"],\"quote\":\"Definitely one of my top 10 documentaries\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"8fHi36dvTdE\",\"title\":\"The Unanswered Question 1973 1  Musical Phonology Bernstein Norton\",\"channel\":\"cagin\",\"summary\":\"Leonard Bernstein's 1973 Norton Lectures at Harvard, starting with Musical Phonology. He draws analogies between music and linguistics, including references to Chomsky.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it for a deep theory of why music sounds the way it does.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"music theory and linguistics\",\"categories\":[\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Language & linguistics\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"leonard bernstein\",\"norton lectures\",\"music theory\",\"chomsky\",\"harmony\"],\"quote\":\"Check out the Norton Lectures especially, where Bernstein analyzes musical structure with references to Chomsky.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"9zinZmE3Ogk\",\"title\":\"Raymond Hettinger, Keynote on Concurrency, PyBay 2017\",\"channel\":\"SF Python\",\"summary\":\"Raymond Hettinger's PyBay 2017 keynote on concurrency in Python. It covers threads, processes, asyncio, locks and the GIL, with live shell-based examples.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a clear, charismatic starting point for understanding Python concurrency and the GIL.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"python concurrency\",\"categories\":[\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"python\",\"gil\",\"threading\",\"asyncio\",\"raymond hettinger\"],\"quote\":\"the best learning material for me on this was this lecture\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"AD5aAd8Oy84\",\"title\":\"A Look Inside Apple's \$130 USB-C Cable\",\"channel\":\"Adam Savage’s Tested\",\"summary\":\"Adam Savage and engineer Zach Radding examine Lumafield CT scans of Apple's \$130 Thunderbolt 4 cable alongside cheaper USB-C cables. They explain the internal components that distinguish Thunderbolt from ordinary USB-C.\",\"why_hn\":\"Linked whenever people ask why a Thunderbolt cable costs so much; the CT scans show the construction difference clearly.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"thunderbolt and usb-c cable construction\",\"categories\":[\"Repair, teardowns & right to repair\",\"Consumer devices & gadgets\",\"Electronics & circuit design\"],\"tags\":[\"thunderbolt 4\",\"usb-c\",\"ct scan\",\"cable engineering\",\"lumafield\"],\"quote\":\"if you're interested in why the Thunderbolt cable costs more than charging cables, some folks put one through a CT scanner recently\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"Auuk1y4DRgk\",\"title\":\"Marcus Aurelius' Meditations: The Stoic Ideal\",\"channel\":\"Michael Sugrue\",\"summary\":\"Michael Sugrue's lecture on Marcus Aurelius' Meditations and the Stoic ideal, covering the text and its philosophy. It is one of his humanities lectures known for their clarity.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a classic, clear introduction to Aurelius, with some saying it helped through hard times.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"stoic philosophy\",\"categories\":[\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"marcus aurelius\",\"stoicism\",\"meditations\",\"michael sugrue\",\"philosophy\"],\"quote\":\"have watched this lecture multiple times (each time I was going through some bad things in my life) and it helped me tremendously.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"BYRTvoZ3Rho\",\"title\":\"Was YOUR vote counted? (feat. homomorphic encryption) - Numberphile\",\"channel\":\"Numberphile\",\"summary\":\"Numberphile interviews cryptographer Ron Rivest about end-to-end verifiable voting. He explains how homomorphic encryption and receipts let a voter confirm their ballot was counted without being able to prove how they voted to anyone else.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it in election-security threads as an accessible explainer of how a vote can be verifiable but not sellable.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"verifiable voting\",\"categories\":[\"Cryptography\",\"Recreational & visual math\",\"US politics & elections\"],\"tags\":[\"homomorphic encryption\",\"e-voting\",\"cryptography\",\"ron rivest\",\"election security\"],\"quote\":\"Cryptographers are clever and have figured out a way to let you know your vote was counted without being able to prove it to a third party!\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"Bv_5Zv5c-Ts\",\"title\":\"JavaScript: Understanding the Weird Parts - The First 3.5 Hours\",\"channel\":\"Tony Alicea\",\"summary\":\"The first 3.5 hours of Tony Alicea's Udemy course on JavaScript's inner workings, covering topics such as first-class functions, closures, prototypal inheritance and how the language behaves under the hood.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call it one of the best JavaScript courses: rigorous and deep but not boring, and freely available.\",\"content_type\":\"course\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"javascript internals\",\"categories\":[\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Web development\"],\"tags\":[\"javascript\",\"closures\",\"prototypes\",\"this keyword\",\"free course\"],\"quote\":\"they are the holy grails of online education for me. Rigorous -- but not boring or too long.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"C25qzDhGLx8\",\"title\":\"Don't Be the Last Person to Die\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey argues that aging and death are a problem worth defeating, using a short animated essay. It is part of a pair with a follow-up from Kurzgesagt.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN longevity fans point to it as a concise, fun statement of the case for fighting aging, and some say it changed their minds.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ending aging\",\"categories\":[\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\",\"Futurism & technology and society\"],\"tags\":[\"longevity\",\"aging\",\"cgp grey\",\"mortality\",\"life extension\"],\"quote\":\"CGP gray's videos on YouTube are maybe the most concise and fun to watch materials I've seen on the subject.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"C_r5UJrxcck\",\"title\":\"1959 Chevrolet Bel Air vs. 2009 Chevrolet Malibu IIHS crash test\",\"channel\":\"IIHS\",\"summary\":\"A one-minute IIHS demonstration crash test of a 1959 Chevrolet Bel Air against a 2009 Chevrolet Malibu, showing how much better the modern car protects its occupants.\",\"why_hn\":\"The go-to demonstration in car-safety debates that old cars were not better, showing crumple zones and a rigid passenger cell at work.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"vehicle crash safety\",\"categories\":[\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\"],\"tags\":[\"crash test\",\"crumple zones\",\"iihs\",\"car safety\",\"old vs new cars\"],\"quote\":\"I always liked this IIHS crash test: 1959 Chevrolet Bel Air vs. 2009 Chevrolet Malibu It really shows the progress.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"Cj4y0EUlU-Y\",\"title\":\"Feynman: How to think 1 of 2  FUN TO IMAGINE 11\",\"channel\":\"Christopher Sykes\",\"summary\":\"An excerpt from the 1983 BBC series Fun to Imagine in which Richard Feynman describes how he and a mathematician friend counted in their heads in different ways, one verbally and one visually. It shows that people can reach the same goal through quite different mental methods.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it constantly in threads on inner monologue, visual versus verbal thinking and communication as the classic, entertaining demonstration that minds work differently.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"how different people think\",\"categories\":[\"Consciousness & cognition\",\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"richard feynman\",\"cognition\",\"internal monologue\",\"counting\",\"mental imagery\"],\"quote\":\"He does a great job of communicating this, despite our interpretive differences.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"DOWDNBu9DkU\",\"title\":\"Amazing Invention- This Drone Will Change Everything\",\"channel\":\"Mark Rober\",\"summary\":\"Mark Rober visits Zipline and looks at its autonomous delivery drones, including medical and blood deliveries in Rwanda. He demonstrates how the drones' propeller design and cable-lowered delivery pod make them quiet and precise.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it as the clearest look at Zipline's real-world drone operations, especially for medical delivery and the solved noise problem.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"delivery drones\",\"categories\":[\"Robotics\",\"Business & economics (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"zipline\",\"drone delivery\",\"mark rober\",\"medical logistics\",\"noise reduction\"],\"quote\":\"They actually solved that. Watch Mark Robers video at 13m50s\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"ExgxwKnH8y4\",\"title\":\"The Insane Battle To Sabotage a New Apartment Building Explains San Francisco's Housing Crisis\",\"channel\":\"ReasonTV\",\"summary\":\"ReasonTV tells the story of Bob Tillman, who spent nearly five years and \$1.4 million trying to convert his San Francisco laundromat into apartments. It walks through the permitting, appeals and neighborhood opposition he faced.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is HN's go-to concrete example of how local regulation and NIMBYism block new housing in San Francisco and California.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"housing regulation in san francisco\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Labor, work & inequality\"],\"tags\":[\"nimby\",\"zoning\",\"san francisco\",\"housing crisis\",\"permitting\",\"reason\"],\"quote\":\"Here is a terrific video that everyone should watch that shows how insanely difficult it is to make affordable housing.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"FjHJ7FmV0M4\",\"title\":\"Richard Feynman talks about light\",\"channel\":\"sdfhsfh\",\"summary\":\"A PBS clip in which Richard Feynman explains the strange nature of light and electromagnetic radiation. He conveys how little of it we actually see and how radio waves are present everywhere.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an entertaining, accessible way to get a feel for light and electric fields.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"nature of light\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\"],\"tags\":[\"feynman\",\"light\",\"electromagnetism\",\"physics\",\"radio waves\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend you watch this video of Feynman explaining light\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"FssULNGSZIA\",\"title\":\"Danny Carey | \\\"Pneuma\\\" by Tool (LIVE IN CONCERT)\",\"channel\":\"Vic Firth\",\"summary\":\"Drummer Danny Carey performs Tool's 'Pneuma' live in Boston, in a Vic Firth session with close views of his playing.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as a showcase of astonishing drumming and music that gives chills.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"drumming\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"danny carey\",\"tool\",\"pneuma\",\"live performance\",\"drum technique\"],\"quote\":\"here's Danny Carey, quite possibly the greatest living drummer\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"FtieBc3KptU\",\"title\":\"Emacs For Writers\",\"channel\":\"thoughtbot\",\"summary\":\"Science writer Jay Dixit explains how he uses Emacs as a non-programmer to write, edit and research more productively. He shows his plain-text writing workflow and what led him from tools like Scrivener to Emacs.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a favorite talk showing Emacs as a writing environment rather than just a programmer's editor.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"emacs for writing\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\",\"Literature & writing craft\"],\"tags\":[\"emacs\",\"writing workflow\",\"plain text\",\"org mode\",\"productivity\"],\"quote\":\"My favourite talk on this topic is \\\"Emacs for writers\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"FvNRlE4E9QQ\",\"title\":\"\\\"Finding bugs without running or even looking at code\\\" by Jay Parlar\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Jay Parlar's Strange Loop talk shows how model checkers such as Alloy can find complex bugs in a system's design without running or reading any code. He walks through modeling architectures and checking every possible sequence of operations, including a real security bug and a UI feature-interaction bug.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as the best practical introduction to Alloy and to lightweight formal modeling.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"formal modeling with alloy\",\"categories\":[\"Logic & formal methods\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"alloy\",\"model checking\",\"formal methods\",\"software design\",\"bug finding\"],\"quote\":\"There is a talk that gives a good overview on Alloy and how useful using something like this is\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"G0Cp7DrvNLQ\",\"title\":\"Joe Rogan Experience #606 - Randall Carlson\",\"channel\":\"PowerfulJRE\",\"summary\":\"A three-hour Joe Rogan podcast with Randall Carlson, builder and geomythologist, on catastrophic geological events, cosmic impacts and climate at the end of the last glacial period.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters in climate and impact-event threads send people to Carlson's Rogan appearances for his account of ancient cataclysms.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cosmic impacts and ancient climate\",\"categories\":[\"Geology & earth science\",\"Climate science & climate change\"],\"tags\":[\"randall carlson\",\"joe rogan\",\"younger dryas\",\"catastrophism\",\"geology\"],\"quote\":\"he gives an enthralling account of the climate events at the time.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"GTQnarzmTOc\",\"title\":\"Fight of the Century: Keynes vs. Hayek - Economics Rap Battle Round Two\",\"channel\":\"Radical Discourse\",\"summary\":\"A hip-hop music video from EconStories in which Keynes and Hayek rap-battle over how to handle recessions and government spending. It is the second round after the first rap battle.\",\"why_hn\":\"Offered as a quick, entertaining primer on Keynesian versus Austrian economics, especially for newcomers to Hayek.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"keynes vs hayek economics\",\"categories\":[