ers on the Amiga and how Doom's arrival affected the platform.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a well-researched piece of computing history on Amiga FPS games and Doom's impact.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"amiga fps history\",\"categories\":[\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"amiga\",\"doom\",\"fps\",\"retro gaming\",\"computing history\"],\"quote\":\"go watch Stuart Brown (aka XboxAhoy)'s excellent documentary on FPS on the Amiga\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"UBc7qBS1Ujo\",\"title\":\"The Bell Curve\",\"channel\":\"Shaun\",\"summary\":\"Shaun's roughly 2.7-hour video essay on The Bell Curve. It covers intelligence, the g factor, IQ tests, IQ versus environment and the politics behind the book, with a critical reading of the sources.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters most often cite it as a thorough, well-sourced debunking of The Bell Curve and of popular IQ claims.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"iq and the bell curve\",\"categories\":[\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Evolution & genetics\",\"Social issues & culture\"],\"tags\":[\"the bell curve\",\"iq\",\"psychometrics\",\"race science\",\"heritability\"],\"quote\":\"The video is well-researched; I've been a long-time fan of the creator for putting out quality stuff.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"UawS3_iuHoA\",\"title\":\"DEF CON 19 - Moxie Marlinspike - SSL And The Future Of Authenticity\",\"channel\":\"DEFCONConference\",\"summary\":\"Moxie Marlinspike's DEF CON 19 talk on the weaknesses of SSL and the certificate authority system. He explains how CA trust became a problem and proposes an alternative that removes the CA oligopoly without reducing security.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the classic overview of why TLS certificate trust is flawed, and it resurfaces whenever CA or HTTPS security comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"tls certificate authorities\",\"categories\":[\"Cryptography\",\"Security & hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"ssl\",\"tls\",\"certificate authorities\",\"security\",\"moxie marlinspike\",\"def con\"],\"quote\":\"I've found Moxie Marlinspike's talk [1] to be a great overview of these sorts of issues.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"Uwl3-jBNEd4\",\"title\":\"Renting vs. Buying a Home: The 5% Rule\",\"channel\":\"Ben Felix\",\"summary\":\"Ben Felix explains how to compare renting and buying a home by looking at unrecoverable costs, and distills it into the 5% rule of thumb. It is framed for a Canadian audience but broadly applicable.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it for a clean first-order framework for the rent versus buy decision.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"rent vs buy housing\",\"categories\":[\"Investing & personal finance\"],\"tags\":[\"5% rule\",\"personal finance\",\"real estate\",\"unrecoverable costs\",\"renting\"],\"quote\":\"This guy breaks down the analysis very cleanly to a first-order approximation, using 2019 figures\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"VAhHkJQ3KgY\",\"title\":\"Dwarf Fortress Creator Explains its Complexity & Origins | Noclip Interview\",\"channel\":\"/noclip\",\"summary\":\"A /noclip interview with Tarn Adams of Bay 12 Games about Dwarf Fortress: where the ASCII simulation came from, how it works, and the plans for its Steam release. It includes his telling of the famous bug in which cats were poisoned by alcohol.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it whenever Dwarf Fortress comes up, mainly for the alcohol-poisoned cats bug story and a developer's view of the game's depth.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"dwarf fortress development\",\"categories\":[\"Game design\",\"Indie game development\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"dwarf fortress\",\"tarn adams\",\"game design\",\"simulation\",\"bug stories\"],\"quote\":\"This interview with Tarn Adams (one of the creators of dwarf fortress) is amazing\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"VQKMoT-6XSg\",\"title\":\"iPhone 1 - Steve Jobs MacWorld keynote in 2007 - Full Presentation, 80 mins\",\"channel\":\"Protectstar Inc.\",\"summary\":\"The full 80-minute Macworld 2007 keynote in which Steve Jobs introduced the original iPhone. It includes the multitouch demos, visual voicemail and scrolling.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as a landmark tech presentation, especially for the audience's astonished reaction to touch scrolling and demo moments like visual voicemail.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"iphone launch keynote\",\"categories\":[\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Consumer devices & gadgets\",\"Tech company stories & documentaries\"],\"tags\":[\"steve jobs\",\"iphone\",\"keynote\",\"multitouch\",\"apple\",\"product launch\"],\"quote\":\"I consider AlphaGo - The Movie [1] to be a timeless classic that will never feel outdated.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"XRYN2xt11Ek\",\"title\":\"Netflix JavaScript Talks - Async JavaScript with Reactive Extensions\",\"channel\":\"Matt Marenghi\",\"summary\":\"A Netflix JavaScript talk on using Reactive Extensions (Rx) for asynchronous UI code. It covers Observables, composing events such as drag and drop, and sequencing Ajax requests.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a clear, mind-changing introduction to observables and async programming from engineers using Rx in production.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"reactive programming\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"rxjs\",\"observables\",\"async javascript\",\"netflix\",\"reactive extensions\"],\"quote\":\"have a look at this presentation about Observables at Netflix as it's quite mind changing\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"XfcvX0P1b5g\",\"title\":\"Bitcoin - Unmasking Satoshi Nakamoto\",\"channel\":\"Barely Sociable\",\"summary\":\"Barely Sociable presents a case that Bitcoin creator Satoshi Nakamoto is cryptographer Adam Back. It uses interview clips and circumstantial evidence, and also touches on the block size dispute.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a compelling, if not definitive, argument for Adam Back as Satoshi whenever the identity question comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"satoshi nakamoto identity\",\"categories\":[\"Cryptography\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"satoshi nakamoto\",\"adam back\",\"bitcoin\",\"block size debate\",\"cryptocurrency history\"],\"quote\":\"painted quite a compelling story of why Adam Beck is Satoshi\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"XpDsk374LDE\",\"title\":\"Elm Europe 2017 - Evan Czaplicki - The life of a file\",\"channel\":\"Elm Europe\",\"summary\":\"Elm creator Evan Czaplicki's talk on how a file grows and when to split code up. He argues for keeping code in one file until it hurts and warns against premature extraction.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the argument for code colocation and delaying abstraction.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"code organization\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Functional programming\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\"],\"tags\":[\"elm\",\"code structure\",\"premature abstraction\",\"colocation\",\"evan czaplicki\"],\"quote\":\"Just stick everything in the same file until it actually hurts. This presentation[0] gets it right.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"Y3n3c_8Nn2Y\",\"title\":\"fr-08: .the .product by farbrausch | 64k intro (2000) (720p HQ demoscene demo)\",\"channel\":\"Demoscene High-Quality Videos (Annikras)\",\"summary\":\"A recording of .the .product by Farbrausch, the first-place 64k intro at The Party 2000. It fits an 11-minute 3D demo with music into 64 kilobytes.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters use it as the standard example of how much a tiny program can do, in contrast to bloated modern software.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"demoscene 64k intros\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"Computer graphics & rendering\"],\"tags\":[\"demoscene\",\"farbrausch\",\"64k intro\",\"procedural graphics\",\"size coding\"],\"quote\":\"not very many 64Ks have reached the amazingness\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"Y5K9HBiJpuk\",\"title\":\"A NEW Trace! The FULL MH370 Story, so Far..\",\"channel\":\"Mentour Pilot\",\"summary\":\"Mentour Pilot's 56-minute account of the MH370 disappearance and what is now known, including the WSPR radio tracking technique and other sensing methods. It was made ahead of renewed search efforts.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a detailed, pilot's-eye summary of the whole MH370 story and the case for searching again.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"mh370 disappearance\",\"categories\":[\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"mh370\",\"aviation accidents\",\"wspr\",\"malaysia airlines\",\"search and rescue\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend Mentour Pilots videos on the topic, for an indepth introduction to the whole story and the new search\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"YpuX-5E7xoU\",\"title\":\"The Grille Trend that Kills 509 People per Year\",\"channel\":\"FortNine\",\"summary\":\"FortNine looks at the data on why newer pickup trucks with tall, blunt grilles kill so many pedestrians, cyclists and motorcyclists. It argues that lowering hood height would substantially cut fatalities.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people cite it in threads on vehicle size and safety as a clear explanation tying grille height to deaths.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"truck front-end pedestrian safety\",\"categories\":[\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\",\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Product & industrial design\"],\"tags\":[\"pickup trucks\",\"pedestrian safety\",\"hood height\",\"vehicle design\",\"road safety\"],\"quote\":\"This guy ties grill height directly to fatalities.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"Z1qyvQsjK5Y\",\"title\":\"Immediate-Mode Graphical User Interfaces - 2005\",\"channel\":\"Casey Muratori\",\"summary\":\"Casey Muratori's 2005 video introducing immediate-mode GUIs as an alternative to retained widget toolkits. He explains the motivation and shows minimal-state button logic.\",\"why_hn\":\"Seen as the origin of the IMGUI idea that inspired Dear ImGui, recommended to anyone curious about immediate-mode UI design.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"immediate mode gui\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Game programming & engines\",\"UX & UI design\"],\"tags\":[\"imgui\",\"gui programming\",\"game development\",\"dear imgui\",\"casey muratori\",\"api design\"],\"quote\":\"Check out this lecture/demo from nearly 20 years ago\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"ZMByI4s-D-Y\",\"title\":\"World's Roundest Object!\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium explains the effort to redefine the kilogram, which was still defined by a physical artifact, and how a near-perfect silicon sphere from the Avogadro project helps. It also covers the history of the unit's odd naming.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in kilogram-redefinition threads as a clear, short explainer of the Avogadro and Planck approaches.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"kilogram redefinition\",\"categories\":[\"Physics & space (other)\",\"Fundamental physics\"],\"tags\":[\"si units\",\"avogadro project\",\"metrology\",\"veritasium\",\"silicon sphere\"],\"quote\":\"this video is an imperative watch\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"_2LEgowbzSc\",\"title\":\"Riding through Iran on a small motorcycle as British person.\",\"channel\":\"c90adventures\",\"summary\":\"Ed March rides a Honda C90 through Iran on his journey from Malaysia to the UK, showing the country and its people. He argues Iran is far more welcoming than the press suggests.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended for its heartwarming picture of Iranian hospitality toward a foreign rider.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"motorcycle travel in iran\",\"categories\":[\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"iran\",\"honda c90\",\"motorcycle travel\",\"hospitality\",\"overland journey\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend watching this fairly recent documentary about a British guy riding a motorcycle through the country.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"_3loq22TxSc\",\"title\":\"Great Impractical Ideas in Computer Science: PowerPoint Programming\",\"channel\":\"Tom Wildenhain\",\"summary\":\"Tom Wildenhain's guest lecture for CMU's Esoteric Programming Languages course shows that PowerPoint transitions and slides are Turing complete without macros, building up to a mind-bending fractal ending.\",\"why_hn\":\"Repeatedly recommended in best-talks threads as a fun, mind-blowing demonstration of unexpected computation.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"esoteric programming\",\"categories\":[\"Computability & complexity theory\",\"University lectures & courses\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"powerpoint\",\"turing completeness\",\"esoteric languages\",\"computation\",\"cmu\"],\"quote\":\"Strong recommend!\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"_gXlauRB1EQ\",\"title\":\"Investigation: How Roblox Is Exploiting Young Game Developers\",\"channel\":\"People Make Games\",\"summary\":\"People Make Games investigates how Roblox relies on young, often child, developers to build its content while paying them very little. It covers Roblox's monetization model, the revenue split, and the harms to kids who make games on the platform.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it as the go-to evidence that Roblox exploits child developers, and parents are pointed to it before letting kids build games there.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"roblox developer exploitation\",\"categories\":[\"Indie game development\",\"Tech industry & business models\",\"Labor, work & inequality\"],\"tags\":[\"roblox\",\"child labor\",\"game monetization\",\"platform economics\",\"investigative journalism\"],\"quote\":\"has a couple of reports that I'd suggest parents consider watching first before going down that road\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"ainyK6fXku0\",\"title\":\"William Shatner - Common People\",\"channel\":\"dsjkhd\",\"summary\":\"William Shatner's spoken-word cover of Pulp's 'Common People', from his album Has Been.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters repeatedly recommend it as a surprisingly good cover that brings out the contempt in the lyrics.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"cover songs\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"william shatner\",\"common people\",\"pulp\",\"cover\",\"spoken word\"],\"quote\":\"I really like William Shatner's cover. It brings out the contempt in the lyrics. And it rocks.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"bRcu-ysocX4\",\"title\":\"1177 BC: The Year Civilization Collapsed (Eric Cline, PhD)\",\"channel\":\"National Capital Area Skeptics (NCAS)\",\"summary\":\"Archaeologist Eric Cline lectures on the Late Bronze Age collapse around 1177 BC, when interconnected Mediterranean empires fell in rapid succession. He discusses the Sea Peoples and the combined causes of the collapse.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an informative and funny hour that serves as a good substitute for his book.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"bronze age collapse\",\"categories\":[\"World history\"],\"tags\":[\"eric cline\",\"sea peoples\",\"ancient history\",\"mediterranean\",\"civilizational collapse\"],\"quote\":\"Excellent informative and hilarious talk about his (at-the-time) new scientific hypothesis\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"bkQJdaGGVM8\",\"title\":\"!!Con 2017: HDR Photography in Microsoft Excel?! by Kevin Chen\",\"channel\":\"Confreaks\",\"summary\":\"A short conference talk from !!Con 2017 in which Kevin Chen explains the HDR imaging algorithm from a foundational paper, framing it as a system of linear equations, and implements it in a Microsoft Excel spreadsheet. No prior image processing knowledge is needed.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a fun, short talk that shows how HDR photography actually works through an absurdly playful implementation.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"hdr photography\",\"categories\":[\"Software (other)\",\"Visual arts, photography & architecture\",\"Computer vision, speech & NLP\"],\"tags\":[\"hdr\",\"excel\",\"image processing\",\"linear equations\",\"!!con\"],\"quote\":\"It's short and fun, I recommend it to all HN readers.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"bnKIVX968PQ\",\"title\":\"The Lively & Liveable Neighbourhoods that are Illegal in Most of North America\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"Not Just Bikes explains how small pockets of shops and restaurants inside residential Dutch neighbourhoods create liveliness and help reduce traffic. It argues this kind of mixed-use design has been illegal in most of the US and Canada for a century because of zoning, and should be brought back.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters link it in zoning, housing and car-dependency threads as an accessible explanation of how single-use zoning and parking rules shape cities.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"mixed-use zoning\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Social issues & culture\"],\"tags\":[\"zoning\",\"urban planning\",\"netherlands\",\"car dependency\",\"walkable neighbourhoods\"],\"quote\":\"Not Just Bikes talks about zoning in the US vs Dutch cities in this video\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"cKxRvEZd3Mw\",\"title\":\"Hello World - Machine Learning Recipes #1\",\"channel\":\"Google for Developers\",\"summary\":\"The first episode of Google's Machine Learning Recipes series. It introduces machine learning and builds a first supervised-learning classifier in about six lines of Python using scikit-learn.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as an approachable first step into machine learning for programmers, with an apples-vs-oranges classifier.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"intro machine learning\",\"categories\":[\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"machine learning\",\"scikit-learn\",\"python\",\"classifier\",\"supervised learning\"],\"quote\":\"Very approachable! The first lesson is a classifier to separate apples and oranges.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"cbeJIwF1pVY\",\"title\":\"Economics of Nuclear Reactor\",\"channel\":\"Illinois EnergyProf\",\"summary\":\"A professor from Illinois compares the cost of building, fueling and operating a nuclear plant against a natural gas plant, using visual charts. It shows how construction time and interest rates dominate, when the investment pays off and when costs cross over.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the go-to short primer HN commenters cite to explain why nuclear is expensive upfront but cheap to run once the construction debt is paid.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"nuclear power economics\",\"categories\":[\"Nuclear power & fusion\",\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\"],\"tags\":[\"nuclear power\",\"capital costs\",\"interest rates\",\"natural gas\",\"energy economics\"],\"quote\":\"His videos are great. Worth your time.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"ciStnd9Y2ak\",\"title\":\"Why I changed my mind about nuclear power | Michael Shellenberger | TEDxBerlin\",\"channel\":\"TEDx Talks\",\"summary\":\"In this TEDxBerlin talk, environmental activist Michael Shellenberger explains why he changed from opposing nuclear power to advocating it, after looking at the evidence on Chernobyl, Fukushima and air pollution.\",\"why_hn\":\"Frequently linked in nuclear-power threads as an eye-opening pro-nuclear argument from a former anti-nuclear activist.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"nuclear power\",\"categories\":[\"Nuclear power & fusion\",\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\"],\"tags\":[\"nuclear energy\",\"chernobyl\",\"fukushima\",\"environmentalism\",\"ted talk\"],\"quote\":\"This is a self-professed anti-nuclear hippy giving a talk about how rationally looking at chernobyl and fukushima made him a nuclear power advocate.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"dNs_kNilSjk\",\"title\":\"Revolutionary Paint: How to Make Surfaces Stay Cool in the Sun\",\"channel\":\"Tech Ingredients\",\"summary\":\"Tech Ingredients shows how to make a homemade radiative cooling paint, with a custom liquid panel that cools below ambient temperature outdoors. The video covers the materials and how to apply the paint.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as an impressive, hands-on DIY demonstration of passive radiative cooling that works in practice.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"radiative cooling paint\",\"categories\":[\"Materials science\",\"Chemistry\",\"Renewables, batteries & storage\"],\"tags\":[\"passive cooling\",\"paint\",\"diy\",\"materials science\",\"reflectivity\"],\"quote\":\"Great video about making home-made high reflectivity paint\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"e3msMuwqp-o\",\"title\":\"31 Hamming, Learning to Learn: You and Your Research, 6 June 1995\",\"channel\":\"Hamming on Hamming: Learning to Learn\",\"summary\":\"The final lecture of Richard Hamming's Learning to Learn course, from 1995, based on his well-known talk 'You and Your Research'. It covers how to do important work and build a career.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people recommend it as the capstone of Hamming's course and as career advice about working on important problems.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"research and career advice\",\"categories\":[\"Productivity & self-improvement\",\"University lectures & courses\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"richard hamming\",\"career\",\"research\",\"important problems\",\"learning to learn\"],\"quote\":\"It's the last lecture in a wonderful 31-lecture course\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"eMlx5fFNoYc\",\"title\":\"Attention in transformers, step-by-step | Deep Learning Chapter 6\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"3Blue1Brown's visual walkthrough of the attention mechanism inside transformers, covering self-attention, multiple heads and cross-attention. It is chapter 6 of his deep learning series.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the go-to visual explanation of attention, recommended to people who find text-based transformer explainers hard to follow.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"transformer attention\",\"categories\":[\"LLMs & generative AI\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\"],\"tags\":[\"attention\",\"transformers\",\"llm\",\"deep learning\",\"visualization\",\"3blue1brown\"],\"quote\":\"what bade me finally understand the transformer architecture [0] and attention [1], are 3Blue1Brown's videos.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"eOti3kKWX-c\",\"title\":\"How an Atomic Clock Really Works: Inside the HP 5061A Cesium Clock\",\"channel\":\"CuriousMarc\",\"summary\":\"CuriousMarc powers up a vintage HP 5061A cesium beam clock and explains both the quantum principle and the HP implementation in detail. It is part of a longer atomic clock series.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN calls it one of the best explanations of how atomic clocks actually work.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"atomic clocks\",\"categories\":[\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"cesium clock\",\"hp 5061a\",\"timekeeping\",\"vintage electronics\",\"quantum physics\"],\"quote\":\"I've yet to see a description better than in CuriousMarc's HP 5061A series\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"eqQBubilSXU\",\"title\":\"Crash Course - The SMIDSY\",\"channel\":\"lbtv\",\"summary\":\"A motorcycle safety video on the SMIDSY (sorry mate, I didn't see you) accident, explaining the perception and optical illusion that makes drivers fail to see riders, and how riders can spot the signs and avoid it.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN considers it an outstanding explanation of why drivers miss motorcycles and cyclists, linked in threads on visibility and junction crashes.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"motorcycle safety\",\"categories\":[\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\",\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\"],\"tags\":[\"smidsy\",\"perception\",\"road safety\",\"motorcycles\",\"visibility\"],\"quote\":\"This is an outstanding vid on the whole subject illustrating the problem and some avoidance methods.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":5.0}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"f7MzFfuNOtY\",\"title\":\"The Truth about Hydrogen\",\"channel\":\"Real Engineering\",\"summary\":\"Real Engineering examines the efficiency of hydrogen as an energy carrier. It follows production, storage and fuel cell conversion losses and compares hydrogen cars with battery electric vehicles.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it in hydrogen threads as a clear breakdown of why compounded inefficiencies make hydrogen a poor fit for cars, though one commenter calls it outdated.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"hydrogen energy efficiency\",\"categories\":[\"Renewables, batteries & storage\",\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\"],\"tags\":[\"hydrogen\",\"fuel cells\",\"battery electric vehicles\",\"energy storage\",\"efficiency\"],\"quote\":\"This video gives a good breakdown on how the inefficiencies in hydrogen fuel cell based cars add up.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"fFSPwJFXVlw\",\"title\":\"Autonomous Testing and the Future of Software Development - Will Wilson\",\"channel\":\"The Linux Foundation\",\"summary\":\"Will Wilson explains autonomous testing: using deterministic simulation and fuzzing, building on FoundationDB's approach, to test software continuously. It sets out the ideas behind his later company Antithesis.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an excellent talk on generalizing deterministic simulation testing, and Antithesis hiring posts link it to explain the work.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"deterministic simulation testing\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Distributed systems\"],\"tags\":[\"autonomous testing\",\"foundationdb\",\"fuzzing\",\"antithesis\",\"distributed systems\"],\"quote\":\"I would encourage you to listen to Will Wilson’s excellent talk on the subject of Autonomous Testing\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"g3Ud6mHdhlQ\",\"title\":\"How Do Nuclear Submarines Make Oxygen?- Smarter Every Day 251\",\"channel\":\"SmarterEveryDay\",\"summary\":\"A Smarter Every Day episode from aboard a nuclear submarine on how the crew makes breathable air. It covers oxygen generation from seawater, oxygen candles and CO2 scrubbing with amine.\",\"why_hn\":\"Go-to link when oxygen candles, CO2 capture or submarine air quality come up.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"submarine air systems\",\"categories\":[\"Ships, submarines & maritime\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Chemistry\"],\"tags\":[\"nuclear submarine\",\"oxygen candles\",\"co2 scrubbing\",\"life support\",\"electrolysis\"],\"quote\":\"Smarter Every Day did an interesting series of videos about nuclear submarines.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"hEx5DNLWGgA\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2015: Herb Sutter \\\"Writing Good C++14... By Default\\\"\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Herb Sutter's CppCon 2015 plenary on writing safe, modern C++ by default using the C++ Core Guidelines. It includes a demo of lifetime-checking static analysis tooling.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it in C++ versus Rust safety debates for its lifetime profile demo, and recommends it as a modern C++ talk.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"modern C++ safety\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\"],\"tags\":[\"c++ core guidelines\",\"lifetime profile\",\"static analysis\",\"herb sutter\",\"cppcon\"],\"quote\":\"Take a look at Herb Sutter's CppCon talk this year\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":5.0},{\"vid\":\"iffTJ1vPCSo\",\"title\":\"Replace Your Exploit-Ridden Firmware with Linux - Ronald Minnich, Google\",\"channel\":\"The Linux Foundation\",\"summary\":\"Ronald Minnich of Google explains how UEFI firmware is a large closed attack surface and describes Google's work replacing it with Linux-based boot firmware. It also touches on Intel ME and other security coprocessors.\",\"why_hn\":\"