osting\",\"python\"],\"quote\":\"The launch video is well worth a quick watch\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"1K5oDtVAYzk\",\"title\":\"Your Post Code Is the Directions, Not the Destination\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey explains that postal codes are routing instructions for mail rather than geographic regions. He looks at the quirks and problems this causes.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it whenever zip codes, addresses or geospatial data come up, as a clear explanation of the hidden pattern in post codes.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"postal codes\",\"categories\":[\"Business & economics (other)\",\"Ideas & learning (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"zip codes\",\"addresses\",\"geography\",\"postal system\",\"cgp grey\"],\"quote\":\"If you find this interesting and you haven't watched it, Go watch\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"1L2ef1CP-yw\",\"title\":\"Best fan placement to move air through the house\",\"channel\":\"Matthias random stuff\",\"summary\":\"Matthias Wandel uses anemometer measurements to find where to place a fan to move the most air through a house at night. He finds that placing the fan some distance back from the window works better.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in cooling and fan-noise threads as a short video with actual data on fan placement.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"fan placement and airflow\",\"categories\":[\"Power grid, heating & buildings\",\"Home, DIY & practical skills\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\"],\"tags\":[\"fans\",\"ventilation\",\"airflow\",\"home cooling\",\"experiments\"],\"quote\":\"Matthias Wandel made a great video showing how it's better to place the fan a meter or two away from the window to get more air flow\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"2JB1_e5wZmU\",\"title\":\"Domain Modeling Made Functional - Scott Wlaschin - KanDDDinsky 2019\",\"channel\":\"KanDDDinsky\",\"summary\":\"Scott Wlaschin shows how statically typed functional languages, using F# examples, can model a business domain so that invalid states cannot be represented. The type system acts almost like an instant unit test at compile time.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the canonical, approachable example of domain-driven design with types and 'make invalid states unrepresentable'.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"type-driven domain modeling\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Software architecture & design\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"f#\",\"functional programming\",\"domain-driven design\",\"type systems\",\"scott wlaschin\"],\"quote\":\"this talk opened my eyes to the power of f# philosophy and type system\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"2NBG-sKFaB0\",\"title\":\".kkrieger - The FPS Game in 96KB (Full Gameplay)\",\"channel\":\"Daniel Hands\",\"summary\":\"Full gameplay of .kkrieger, a first-person shooter by Farbrausch that fits into a 96KB executable by generating its textures, models and sounds procedurally.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the standard example of procedural content generation and extreme size optimization in games.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"procedural generation\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"Game programming & engines\",\"Computer graphics & rendering\"],\"tags\":[\"kkrieger\",\"demoscene\",\"96kb\",\"farbrausch\",\"fps\"],\"quote\":\"I suggest that you look at the result in awe\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"2S0k12uZR14\",\"title\":\"Velocity 2012:  Richard Cook, \\\"How Complex Systems Fail\\\"\",\"channel\":\"O'Reilly\",\"summary\":\"Dr. Richard Cook, a physician and patient safety researcher, presents his ideas on how complex systems fail, at Velocity 2012. He argues for designing for resiliency rather than trying to exhaustively handle every failure mode.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people recommend it as a must-watch for anyone running complex systems, and some rewatch it regularly to remember its principles.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"complex systems failure\",\"categories\":[\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\",\"Distributed systems\"],\"tags\":[\"resilience\",\"operations\",\"safety engineering\",\"incident analysis\",\"richard cook\"],\"quote\":\"I would watch this talk every couple of months to remind myself of the principles.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"2gFsOoKAHZg\",\"title\":\"Flat Earth: A Measured Response\",\"channel\":\"hbomberguy\",\"summary\":\"hbomberguy investigates the modern flat earth movement and why people come to believe it. The video treats the community with a measured, humorous tone and looks at the social causes behind the belief.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as an entertaining and thoughtful look at flat earthers that frames the belief as a distrust of failing institutions.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"flat earth belief\",\"categories\":[\"Religion, belief & skepticism\",\"Internet culture & memes\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"flat earth\",\"conspiracy theories\",\"youtube culture\",\"debunking\",\"hbomberguy\"],\"quote\":\"I strongly recommend watching hbomberguy's video about flat earthers\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"2yDCox-qKbk\",\"title\":\"Ringing in Ears Tinnitus Treatment\",\"channel\":\"ringinginearsv\",\"summary\":\"A short promotional video demonstrating a head-thumping technique that is claimed to relieve tinnitus. Covering the ears and tapping the back of the head is said to silence the ringing temporarily.\",\"why_hn\":\"Tinnitus sufferers share it as a free trick that briefly stops the ringing, though not as a cure.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"tinnitus relief\",\"categories\":[\"Medicine & infectious disease\",\"Life sciences (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"tinnitus\",\"ringing in ears\",\"home remedy\",\"temporary relief\",\"health\"],\"quote\":\"this trick helped me be in silence for the first time in decades.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"3-son3EJTrU\",\"title\":\"RSA ANIMATE: Language as a Window into Human Nature\",\"channel\":\"Royal Society of Arts\",\"summary\":\"An RSA Animate in which Steven Pinker explains how the mind builds infinite meanings from finite language, and why people often speak indirectly. It covers ambiguity, politeness and mutual knowledge, including the emperor's new clothes example.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a short, clear explanation of why people avoid saying things explicitly and how common knowledge works socially and politically.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"psycholinguistics\",\"categories\":[\"Language & linguistics\",\"Psychology & human behavior\"],\"tags\":[\"steven pinker\",\"indirect speech\",\"common knowledge\",\"language\",\"rsa animate\"],\"quote\":\"this wonderful RSA Animate of Steve Pinker's discussion of how we use language\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"34LGPIXvU5M\",\"title\":\"Noam Chomsky: The five filters of the mass media machine | The Listening Post\",\"channel\":\"Al Jazeera English\",\"summary\":\"A short Al Jazeera animation narrated by Amy Goodman presenting Noam Chomsky's five filters of the mass media: ownership, advertising, media elite sourcing, flak and a common enemy. It is part of the MediaTheorised series.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a quick, accessible introduction to the propaganda model from Manufacturing Consent.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"media propaganda model\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"noam chomsky\",\"manufacturing consent\",\"propaganda model\",\"mass media\",\"media criticism\"],\"quote\":\"Manufacturing Consent (there's a documentary form of it too) changed my entire outlook on media. Highly recommend.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"3GJUM6pCpew\",\"title\":\"Why is TV 29.97 frames per second?\",\"channel\":\"Stand-up Maths\",\"summary\":\"Matt Parker explains the history behind NTSC television running at 29.97 frames per second rather than 30, tracing it to the backward-compatible addition of color. He walks through the arithmetic behind the odd ratio.\",\"why_hn\":\"Go-to explanation whenever the strange 29.97 frame rate comes up, praised for its clarity and presenter.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ntsc video frame rates\",\"categories\":[\"Audio & video engineering\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"29.97 fps\",\"ntsc\",\"television history\",\"color tv\",\"standupmaths\"],\"quote\":\"definitely go and watch Matt Parker's legendary explanation of why NTSC runs at 29.97fps\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"3GVO-MfIl1Q\",\"title\":\"Pop an ollie and innovate! | Rodney Mullen | TED\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"Skateboarding pioneer Rodney Mullen talks about how the open skate community and its environments drive the invention of new tricks out of pure passion. He draws parallels to collaboration and open source.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people share it as an inspiring talk linking skateboarding creativity to hacking and open-source knowledge sharing.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"creativity and innovation in skateboarding\",\"categories\":[\"Sports & athletics\",\"Futurism & technology and society\"],\"tags\":[\"rodney mullen\",\"skateboarding\",\"innovation\",\"open source\",\"ted talk\"],\"quote\":\"talks a bit about the relationship between hacking and skateboarding in his TED talk\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"3dl9Ovwmnxw\",\"title\":\"Super Pointy Sprint Missile - 0 to Mach 10 in 15 Seconds - 100Gs & 6000°F\",\"channel\":\"Dark Footage\",\"summary\":\"A short video on the US Sprint anti-ballistic missile, which accelerated to around Mach 10 in seconds under extreme g-forces and heat. It includes striking launch footage and a brief development history.\",\"why_hn\":\"Shared in hypersonic and missile threads as a compact, impressive overview of what Sprint achieved in the 1960s.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"sprint missile\",\"categories\":[\"Military history & warfare\",\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"sprint missile\",\"hypersonic\",\"anti-ballistic missile\",\"cold war\",\"nike-x\"],\"quote\":\"It's a great overview of the development of the Sprint though, worth watching if it's of interest.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"3s7h2MHQtxc\",\"title\":\"Hilbert's Curve: Is infinite math useful?\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"3Blue1Brown explains space-filling curves and the Hilbert curve, and how infinite mathematical constructions connect to finite, practical approximations. It touches on mapping between dimensions and sensory ideas.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the best visual introduction to Hilbert curves, which come up in indexing, image layout and dimensionality mapping.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"space-filling curves\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"hilbert curve\",\"3blue1brown\",\"mathematics\",\"dimensionality\",\"visualization\"],\"quote\":\"This is a great video on the Hilbert curve\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{