cript) and Lars Bak (Dart). They discuss the two languages and different approaches to making JavaScript development safer, including runtime type checking.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a rare side-by-side of the TypeScript and Dart designers, often to show how the two approaches to improving JavaScript differed.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"typescript vs dart\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Web development\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"typescript\",\"dart\",\"javascript\",\"language design\",\"type systems\"],\"quote\":\"Debate (almost 10 years ago) TypeScript vs. Dart about the best way to improve JavaScript\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"5WPB2u8EzL8\",\"title\":\"How to Enjoy the End of the World\",\"channel\":\"Sid Smith\",\"summary\":\"A Virginia Tech presentation by Sid Smith about collapse, fossil fuels and overshoot, arguing that industrial civilization faces a decline and how one might live with that. It was later expanded into a video series.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in collapse and energy threads as a sobering thermodynamic view of civilization, often with a warning that it is heavy.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"societal collapse and energy\",\"categories\":[\"Futurism & technology and society\",\"Earth & energy (other)\",\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\"],\"tags\":[\"collapse\",\"fossil fuels\",\"overshoot\",\"energy return\",\"sustainability\"],\"quote\":\"I recommend few videos for you. I must warn you ... it may hamper your ability to enjoy parties.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"5fG_lyNuEAw\",\"title\":\"Jordan Walke - React to the Future\",\"channel\":\"ReasonConf\",\"summary\":\"Jordan Walke, creator of React, talks at ReasonConf 2019 about the ideas behind ReasonML and how it extends React's programming model with OCaml. He also discusses why familiar syntax matters.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the best introduction to Reason's motivations, straight from React's creator.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"reasonml and react\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"reasonml\",\"ocaml\",\"react\",\"functional programming\",\"jordan walke\"],\"quote\":\"I'd highly recommend Jordan Walke's (creator of React) talk on the ideas behind ReasonML\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"62tIvfP9A2w\",\"title\":\"The most feared song in jazz, explained\",\"channel\":\"Vox\",\"summary\":\"Vox Earworm explains why John Coltrane's Giant Steps is so hard to improvise over: it is fast and moves through three keys. Musicians Braxton Cook and Adam Neely walk the host through the underlying music theory.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a well-produced, accessible explanation of Giant Steps and the harmonic ideas behind Coltrane's work.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"jazz harmony\",\"categories\":[\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"john coltrane\",\"giant steps\",\"music theory\",\"jazz\",\"vox earworm\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend this Vox explainer on John Coltrane's\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"66YRCjkxIcg\",\"title\":\"World's Largest Batteries - (Pumped Storage)\",\"channel\":\"Practical Engineering\",\"summary\":\"Practical Engineering explains pumped storage hydropower, which makes up most grid-scale electricity storage. It covers how it works and why it fits only certain geographies.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a clear explanation of pumped hydro and its limits in energy storage discussions.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"pumped hydro storage\",\"categories\":[\"Renewables, batteries & storage\",\"Civil engineering & construction\",\"Power grid, heating & buildings\"],\"tags\":[\"pumped storage\",\"grid storage\",\"hydropower\",\"energy\",\"civil engineering\"],\"quote\":\"There's a good watch on pumped hydro, and how it is kinda sucky\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"6EghiY_s2ts\",\"title\":\"How I overcame alcoholism | Claudia Christian | TEDxLondonBusinessSchool\",\"channel\":\"TEDx Talks\",\"summary\":\"Actress Claudia Christian tells how she overcame alcoholism using the Sinclair Method, which uses naltrexone to gradually reduce cravings. She discusses fresh perspectives on treating alcohol use disorder.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters share it as the talk that introduced them to the Sinclair Method as an alternative approach to alcohol dependence.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"alcohol use disorder treatment\",\"categories\":[\"Mental health & neurodiversity\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"sinclair method\",\"naltrexone\",\"alcoholism\",\"addiction\",\"recovery\"],\"quote\":\"This TEDx talk was what convinced me to try it\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"6wBrOV2FJM8\",\"title\":\"Brick Assembly\",\"channel\":\"Ancient\",\"summary\":\"A maker's time-lapse of building a working computer inside a LEGO-style brick, using a tiny 0.42-inch OLED screen, with a demo at the end.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN finds the miniaturization mind-blowing and cites it as a standout example of surreal hobbyist technology.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"miniature computer build\",\"categories\":[\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\",\"3D printing, CNC & maker projects\"],\"tags\":[\"lego\",\"miniaturization\",\"oled\",\"maker\",\"embedded\"],\"quote\":\"This is a mind blowing level of miniaturization\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"7QmCUDHpNzE\",\"title\":\"DropBox Demo\",\"channel\":\"theragax\",\"summary\":\"The original five-minute Dropbox demo video, which presented the product's features before a finished product existed. It was aimed at technical early adopters and became famous as a launch strategy.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as the classic example of validating a product idea with a demo video, and many recall how compelling it was.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"startup product demos\",\"categories\":[\"Startups & entrepreneurship\",\"Product, marketing & sales\"],\"tags\":[\"dropbox\",\"drew houston\",\"mvp validation\",\"launch strategy\",\"demo video\"],\"quote\":\"I still remember seeing the original 5 minute demo (1). My jaw was on the floor, I instantly needed it.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"7g1Acy5eGbE\",\"title\":\"\\\"Performance (Really) Matters\\\" with Emery Berger\",\"channel\":\"Association for Computing Machinery (ACM)\",\"summary\":\"Emery Berger argues that performance is hard to measure and deliver on modern systems, showing how code layout and link order can swamp optimization effects. He presents tools that evaluate performance more reliably.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it whenever benchmarking pitfalls and layout effects come up, as an eye-opening talk on how unreliable naive measurements are.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"performance measurement\",\"categories\":[\"Software performance & optimization\",\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\"],\"tags\":[\"benchmarking\",\"code layout\",\"compilers\",\"profiling\",\"systems\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend it. It's mind blowing, but also terrifying\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"8wlld3Z9wRc\",\"title\":\"What People Get Wrong About Induced Demand\",\"channel\":\"Oh The Urbanity!\",\"summary\":\"Explains induced demand beyond roads, showing that it applies to transit, walking and cycling infrastructure too but with different consequences. It corrects common misunderstandings of the concept.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a clear treatment of induced demand across transport modes whenever threads debate road expansion versus transit.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"induced demand\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\"],\"tags\":[\"urbanism\",\"traffic congestion\",\"public transit\",\"cycling\",\"transport policy\"],\"quote\":\"had a pretty good video on induced demand as it applies to both car transit and public transit\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"92w5doU68D8\",\"title\":\"EV Road Trips Suck Now (Except in a Tesla)\",\"channel\":\"Aging Wheels\",\"summary\":\"Aging Wheels compares a road trip in a Polestar 2 with an identical drive in a Tesla, showing the charging problems of non-Tesla EVs. It argues that outside Tesla's network, EV road trips have become frustrating.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as a clear side-by-side showing the papercuts of non-Tesla fast charging in EV debates.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ev road trip charging\",\"categories\":[\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\",\"Renewables, batteries & storage\"],\"tags\":[\"electric vehicles\",\"tesla supercharger\",\"polestar 2\",\"fast charging\",\"road trip\"],\"quote\":\"Aging Wheels did an excellent video[1] on how frustrating the experience can be.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"9CO6M2HsoIA\",\"title\":\"Slaughterbots\",\"channel\":\"Stop Autonomous Weapons\",\"summary\":\"Slaughterbots is a fictional short film, backed by Stuart Russell, depicting swarms of cheap autonomous drones that use facial recognition to assassinate targets. It was made to argue for banning autonomous weapons.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN invokes it as the standard reference whenever drone swarms, autonomous weapons or military AI come up in the news.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"autonomous weapons\",\"categories\":[\"Robotics\",\"AI safety, AGI & impact\",\"Military history & warfare\"],\"tags\":[\"killer robots\",\"drone swarms\",\"military ai\",\"stuart russell\",\"dystopian fiction\"],\"quote\":\"see Stuart Russell's compelling short film\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"9cNmUNHSBac\",\"title\":\"Most People Don't Know How Bikes Work\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium explains why bicycles are stable, arguing that gyroscopic effects are not the answer. The video shows countersteering and an experiment where riders blocked from steering the opposite way cannot turn.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it in threads on bikes, countersteering and control systems as the clearest demonstration of how bikes actually steer and balance.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"bicycle physics\",\"categories\":[\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Fundamental physics\",\"Mechanical & precision engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"countersteering\",\"balance\",\"gyroscopic effect\",\"physics\",\"veritasium\"],\"quote\":\"It shows what happens if you prevent the rider from performing the countersteer before leaning into a turn [1].\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"AD4b-52jtos\",\"title\":\"Hamming, Intro to The Art of Doing Science and Engineering: Learning to Learn (March 28, 1995)\",\"channel\":\"securitylectures\",\"summary\":\"The introductory lecture of Richard Hamming's capstone course, The Art of Doing Science and Engineering: Learning to Learn. It focuses on style of thinking and on educating rather than training, using technical material only as illustration.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN regularly recommends Hamming's lecture series as a fascinating, wisdom-rich course on how to approach hard technical problems and a career, often preferred to the book.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"learning to learn and doing science\",\"categories\":[\"Productivity & self-improvement\",\"University lectures & courses\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"richard hamming\",\"learning\",\"engineering\",\"research\",\"career advice\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend Richard Hamming's wonderful course The Art of Doing Science and Engineering: Learning to Learn [1]\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"AbgsfeGvg3E\",\"title\":\"Old Is the New New • Kevlin Henney • GOTO 2018\",\"channel\":\"GOTO Conferences\",\"summary\":\"Kevlin Henney's GOTO 2018 talk argues that much of what looks new in software is a recycling of concepts with long histories. It draws on history to show the slow, steady current beneath constant churn in frameworks and trends.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it for historical perspective on software ideas and for dispelling myths such as the origins of waterfall.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of software ideas\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"kevlin henney\",\"software history\",\"waterfall\",\"patterns\",\"programming\"],\"quote\":\"There's a talk well worth watching by Kevlin Henney called 'Old Is the New New' [1]\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"Ak4on5uTaTg\",\"title\":\"Neom - The Line - The Rise and Fall of Saudi Arabia's Linear City.\",\"channel\":\"Patrick Boyle\",\"summary\":\"Patrick Boyle's deadpan account of Saudi Arabia's Neom and The Line megaproject, a planned 170 km linear city that has been scaled back to a fraction of its size. He covers the feasibility problems and the financing.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the entertaining, skeptical overview of Neom whenever its progress comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"neom the line\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Civil engineering & construction\",\"Business & economics (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"neom\",\"saudi arabia\",\"megaprojects\",\"urban planning\",\"patrick boyle\"],\"quote\":\"is rather epic if you haven't seen it.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"B5NULPSiOGw\",\"title\":\"CRDTs and the Quest for Distributed Consistency\",\"channel\":\"InfoQ\",\"summary\":\"Martin Kleppmann's QCon London 2018 talk on keeping data consistent in distributed systems without a central leader. It surveys CRDT algorithms for syncing devices and collaborative editing over peer-to-peer networks.\",\"why_hn\":\"Strongly recommended as a clear, accessible survey of the CRDT landscape.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"crdts\",\"categories\":[\"Distributed systems\",\"Algorithms & data structures\"],\"tags\":[\"crdt\",\"distributed systems\",\"collaborative editing\",\"martin kleppmann\",\"consistency\",\"local-first\"],\"quote\":\"I also highly recommend the talk\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"B965f8AcNbw\",\"title\":\"2. The Bronze Age Collapse - Mediterranean Apocalypse\",\"channel\":\"Fall of Civilizations\",\"summary\":\"An episode of Fall of Civilizations on the Late Bronze Age Collapse around 1100 BC, when many Eastern Mediterranean societies were destroyed in a short span. It explores the Sea Peoples and the lessons for modern globalised societies.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as an engaging, high-quality introduction to the Bronze Age Collapse and praise the channel generally.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"bronze age collapse\",\"categories\":[\"World history\"],\"tags\":[\"bronze age\",\"sea peoples\",\"ancient history\",\"civilization collapse\",\"history documentary\"],\"quote\":\"is also a great introduction\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"Bkm2Vfj42FY\",\"title\":\"Mark Blyth ─ Global Trumpism\",\"channel\":\"Watson School of International and Public Affairs\",\"summary\":\"Mark Blyth's 2016 Brown University lecture on global Trumpism, explaining the rise of populism through economics, the decline of Western middle classes and revolt against technocracy.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a concise, chart-backed explanation of the economics behind anti-establishment politics.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"political economy of populism\",\"categories\":[\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\",\"US politics & elections\",\"Labor, work & inequality\"],\"tags\":[\"mark blyth\",\"populism\",\"neoliberalism\",\"trump\",\"economics\"],\"quote\":\"which concisely outlines the economics behind the decline of western middle classes\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"CCOdQsZa15o\",\"title\":\"The Houses that Can't be Built in America - The Missing Middle\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"Not Just Bikes explains missing middle housing, such as duplexes, townhouses and small apartment buildings. The video argues that zoning in the US and Canada has made these options almost illegal.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the standard short overview of missing middle housing and its link to zoning reform.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"missing middle housing\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Social issues & culture\"],\"tags\":[\"zoning\",\"missing middle\",\"urban planning\",\"density\",\"not just bikes\"],\"quote\":\"you need to watch the NotJustBikes overview of the Missing Middle problem\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"CaPgDQkmqqM\",\"title\":\"The Alt-Right Playbook: Control the Conversation\",\"channel\":\"Innuendo Studios\",\"summary\":\"Innuendo Studios' video from the Alt-Right Playbook series on how bad-faith actors control the conversation through debate tactics and moving goalposts.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends the series for explaining the tactics and mentality of bad-faith online arguments.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"bad-faith argument tactics\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"US politics & elections\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\"],\"tags\":[\"alt-right\",\"rhetoric\",\"video essay\",\"debate tactics\",\"online discourse\"],\"quote\":\"I found it enormously helpful to understand the mentality and tactics of a group that kind of doesn't make any sense.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"Cym4TZwTCNU\",\"title\":\"Rich Hickey: Deconstructing the Database\",\"channel\":\"InfoQ\",\"summary\":\"Rich Hickey, creator of Clojure and designer of Datomic, gives a JaxConf 2012 talk that takes apart traditional database architecture. He presents a different way to think about databases and explains the reasoning behind Datomic's fact-based design.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points people to it as the clearest account of why Datomic is designed the way it is and of the problems with conventional database architecture.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"database architecture\",\"categories\":[\"Databases & storage\",\"Functional programming\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"rich hickey\",\"datomic\",\"clojure\",\"immutable data\",\"database design\"],\"quote\":\"Rich Hickey's talk on the design of Datomic is also something every programmer should see at least once\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"DEcwa68f-jY\",\"title\":\"dotJS 2019 - James Long - CRDTs for Mortals\",\"channel\":\"dotconferences\",\"summary\":\"James Long's dotJS 2019 talk on how CRDTs enable local-first apps and how simple they can be to implement. He shows how to use them to build robust offline-capable frontends.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an approachable introduction to CRDTs and the local-first idea behind apps like Actual.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"crdts\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Distributed systems\",\"Algorithms & data structures\"],\"tags\":[\"crdt\",\"local-first\",\"sync\",\"javascript\",\"offline apps\"],\"quote\":\"the same one who made a great talk on using CRDTs called CRDTs for Mortals\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"DRq2NgeFcO0\",\"title\":\"Edwin Brady - Idris 2 - Type-driven Development of Idris\",\"channel\":\"Curry On!\",\"summary\":\"Edwin Brady, creator of Idris, presents Idris 2 and demonstrates type-driven development, where the compiler assists by filling holes and suggesting implementations from types.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the best demo of interactive, compiler-assisted dependently typed programming in action.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"dependently typed programming\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Functional programming\",\"Logic & formal methods\"],\"tags\":[\"idris 2\",\"type-driven development\",\"dependent types\",\"edwin brady\",\"interactive programming\"],\"quote\":\"Watch Edwin Brady's demo of Idris 2 [2] to see this form of interactive development in action.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"Dddbe9OuJLU\",\"title\":\"BBS The Documentary: Episode 1 of 8: BAUD (The Beginning)\",\"channel\":\"Jason Scott\",\"summary\":\"The first episode of Jason Scott's eight-part BBS documentary, covering the predecessors and beginnings of the dial-up bulletin board system. It includes interviews with pioneers such as Ward Christensen and Randy Suess, who created the first dial-up BBS in 1978.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend the series as the definitive history of BBS culture and its Fidonet roots, especially for nostalgia and the origins of the demoscene.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"bbs history\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Internet culture & memes\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"bulletin board systems\",\"jason scott\",\"fidonet\",\"dial-up\",\"computer history\"],\"quote\":\"He also gave us the brilliant BBS: The Documentary\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"DsZdfnlh_d0\",\"title\":\"Depth-first search in APL\",\"channel\":\"Dyalog\",\"summary\":\"John Scholes builds a depth-first tree-search operator step by step in a Dyalog APL session. It covers higher-order functions and runs the search on the N-Queens problem.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call it a beautiful, brilliant demonstration of APL's expressiveness and a model of clear narration.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"apl programming\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Algorithms & data structures\"],\"tags\":[\"apl\",\"depth-first search\",\"higher-order functions\",\"array programming\",\"dyalog\"],\"quote\":null,\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"DxL2HoqLbyA\",\"title\":\"The Most Misunderstood Concept in Physics\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium's explainer on entropy, presented as one of physics' most important and least understood concepts. It uses examples such as heat engines and the sun's energy flowing through the Earth.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters say it made entropy intuition click and point to it in threads on energy, heat and equilibrium.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"entropy\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\"],\"tags\":[\"entropy\",\"thermodynamics\",\"physics\",\"veritasium\",\"energy\"],\"quote\":\"This veritasium video explained it in a way that finally clicked for me\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"EOwxxsPaogY\",\"title\":\"How Harley-Davidson Killed Itself\",\"channel\":\"FortNine\",\"summary\":\"FortNine explains how Harley-Davidson ended up in decline, from a narrow lifestyle niche and an aging customer base to the pandemic hitting a company already in trouble.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an entertaining overview of Harley's decline and as a case study in selling a lifestyle rather than a product.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"harley-davidson decline\",\"categories\":[\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\",\"Business & economics (other)\",\"Product, marketing & sales\"],\"tags\":[\"harley-davidson\",\"motorcycles\",\"brand strategy\",\"business case study\",\"demographics\"],\"quote\":\"This very entertaining 11 minute video from Fortnine (still one of the best channels on YouTube)\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"EWX5B6cD4_4\",\"title\":\"How Myst Almost Couldn't Run on CD-ROM | War Stories | Ars Technica\",\"channel\":\"Ars Technica\",\"summary\":\"Myst co-creator Rand Miller recounts how the HyperCard-built game struggled to run on 1x CD-ROM drives. He and his brother Robyn compressed image and audio data heavily to get acceptable performance. Part of Ars Technica's War Stories series.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as the first-person story of how Myst grew out of HyperCard and fit on CD-ROM, especially in threads about HyperCard and retro game constraints.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"game development history\",\"categories\":[\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"myst\",\"hypercard\",\"cd-rom\",\"rand miller\",\"retro games\"],\"quote\":\"is actually more fascinating and entertaining in my opinion.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"F3ntGDm6hOs\",\"title\":\"Intro to C on Windows - Day 1\",\"channel\":\"Molly Rocket\",\"summary\":\"Day 1 of Handmade Hero's introduction to C on Windows, covering setting up Visual Studio, creating a Win32 project, the WinMain entry point and writing a first function. It is the starting point for Casey Muratori's from-scratch game series.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an approachable way into C and low-level programming that gets to something visible quickly.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"c programming on windows\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"handmade hero\",\"c language\",\"win32\",\"visual studio\",\"casey muratori\"],\"quote\":\"I think Handmade Hero's[0] intro to C is good\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"FF-tKLISfPE\",\"title\":\"Steve Jobs Insult Response\",\"channel\":\"Mike Cane\",\"summary\":\"A clip from Steve Jobs's 1997 WWDC session, in which he answers a hostile question from an audience member about Apple killing OpenDoc. He says to start with the customer experience and work back to the technology, and that saying no is part of focus.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters link it as a canonical statement of Jobs's product philosophy and as an example of handling an insult calmly and articulately.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"product philosophy\",\"categories\":[\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Product & industrial design\",\"Tech company stories & documentaries\"],\"tags\":[\"steve jobs\",\"wwdc 1997\",\"opendoc\",\"customer experience\",\"focus\"],\"quote\":\"Watch Jobs in his prime a year later describing his philosophy\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"FihU5JxmnBg\",\"title\":\"Debugging with the Scientific Method - Stuart Halloway\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"Stuart Halloway of Cognitect argues for debugging with the scientific method: form hypotheses, try to falsify them, and write everything down. The talk is given to the Clojure community but applies to any language.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a talk that changed how people debug, worth rewatching periodically.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"debugging methodology\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"debugging\",\"scientific method\",\"clojure\",\"hypothesis testing\",\"stuart halloway\"],\"quote\":\"changed how I debug problems. I try to watch it once every year or two.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"G8cbIWMv0rI\",\"title\":\"Carl Sagan - Cosmos - Eratosthenes\",\"channel\":\"carlsagandotcom\",\"summary\":\"A short clip from Carl Sagan's Cosmos in which he explains how Eratosthenes measured the circumference of the Earth using shadows in two Egyptian cities. It is a clear, visual account of an ancient scientific feat.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters link it whenever the question of how the Earth's size was first measured comes up, calling it a wonderful explanation.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"measuring earth's circumference\",\"categories\":[\"World history\",\"Scientific method & science communication\",\"Pure mathematics\"],\"tags\":[\"eratosthenes\",\"carl sagan\",\"cosmos\",\"astronomy history\",\"geometry\"],\"quote\":\"Here is a wonderful video of Carl Sagan explaining it[0].\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"GcJxRqTs5nk\",\"title\":\"Moral behavior in animals | Frans de Waal\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"Frans de Waal's TED talk shows videos of behavioral experiments on primates and other mammals that reveal empathy, cooperation, fairness and reciprocity. He argues these moral traits are not uniquely human.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters call it a must-watch, funny and entertaining demonstration that morality has natural roots in animals.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"animal morality\",\"categories\":[\"Animals & nature\",\"Psychology & human behavior\"],\"tags\":[\"frans de waal\",\"primates\",\"fairness\",\"empathy\",\"ted talk\"],\"quote\":\"Here is one of his excellent must-watch Ted talks -\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"GhsZUZmJvaM\",\"title\":\"Mars Helicopter (before it went to Mars)\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium's Derek Muller visits NASA JPL to see the Mars helicopter Ingenuity before it was mounted on the rover. He explains how it can fly in Mars's thin atmosphere with large, fast-spinning contra-rotating rotors and low mass, and covers power and cold.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN repeatedly recommends it as an excellent explanation of why flying on Mars is hard and how the helicopter was built.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"mars helicopter\",\"categories\":[\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\",\"Space exploration & spaceflight\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\"],\"tags\":[\"ingenuity\",\"nasa jpl\",\"aerodynamics\",\"mars\",\"veritasium\"],\"quote\":\"a really excellent video that answers all your questions\",\"recommenders\":10,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"GoJsr4IwCm4\",\"title\":\"Why Age? Should We End Aging Forever?\",\"channel\":\"Kurzgesagt – In a Nutshell\",\"summary\":\"Kurzgesagt's short animated video asks whether humanity should try to end aging, and argues the case for curing age-related disease. It is part one of a pair, with a second part by CGP Grey.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a compelling, concise argument for longevity research and a rebuttal to common objections to ending aging.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ending aging\",\"categories\":[\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\",\"Philosophy & ethics\"],\"tags\":[\"longevity\",\"aging\",\"immortality\",\"biology\",\"ethics\"],\"quote\":\"A good counter-argument to commonly cited objections to biological immortality\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"H46ON2FB30U\",\"title\":\"A quick tour of TeXmacs\",\"channel\":\"TeXmacs Videos\",\"summary\":\"A four-minute tour of the TeXmacs scientific editor, showing math typing, figures, citations, a drawing tool, document styles, HTML export, presentations and animations.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the quickest way to see what TeXmacs can do, as a WYSIWYG alternative to LaTeX.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"texmacs scientific editor\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Graphic design & typography\"],\"tags\":[\"texmacs\",\"wysiwyg\",\"latex alternative\",\"math typesetting\",\"scientific writing\"],\"quote\":\"See here a short video describing its features\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"HdHlfiOAJyE\",\"title\":\"Ses 1: Introduction and Course Overview\",\"channel\":\"MIT OpenCourseWare\",\"summary\":\"First session of MIT 15.401 Finance Theory I (Fall 2008) taught by Andrew Lo, giving a course overview and introducing core finance concepts. The course ran during the 2007-08 financial crisis.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a free, first-principles finance course for software engineers wanting to learn business and finance.\",\"content_type\":\"course\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"finance theory\",\"categories\":[\"Investing & personal finance\",\"University lectures & courses\",\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\"],\"tags\":[\"mit\",\"andrew lo\",\"finance\",\"corporate finance\",\"financial crisis\"],\"quote\":\"The course is excellent.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"Hvx4xXhZMrU\",\"title\":\"The Making of: ROM City Rampage (Retro City Rampage)\",\"channel\":\"VblankEntertainment\",\"summary\":\"Making-of video about ROM City Rampage, the NES version of Retro City Rampage, covering how the game's core was crunched to run on real 8-bit hardware and how NES games are made.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a fascinating look at fitting a modern game into NES constraints.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"nes homebrew development\",\"categories\":[\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"Indie game development\"],\"tags\":[\"retro city rampage\",\"nes\",\"6502\",\"game porting\",\"8-bit\"],\"quote\":\"There's also a fascinating video of him getting the core of the game to run on NES spec constraints\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"Hy4QjmKzF1c\",\"title\":\"How to Make an Attractive City\",\"channel\":\"The School of Life\",\"summary\":\"The School of Life sets out principles for making attractive cities, arguing that the art has been lost in the modern world.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in urban planning and housing threads as a concise summary of what makes cities livable and beautiful.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"city planning\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Visual arts, photography & architecture\"],\"tags\":[\"urbanism\",\"beautiful cities\",\"architecture\",\"livability\",\"school of life\"],\"quote\":\"This video is really informative in this regard and has changed my opinion on a few things\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"IS5ycm7VfXg\",\"title\":\"\\\"Z2\\\" - Upgraded Homemade Silicon Chips\",\"channel\":\"Sam Zeloof\",\"summary\":\"Sam Zeloof shows the upgraded second iteration of his homemade silicon integrated circuit fabrication process, producing chips with around a thousand transistors in his garage. It is a short overview of what the home fab can now do.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as the go-to proof that small-scale, hobbyist chip fabrication is real, and as an entry point to the topic.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"homemade semiconductor fabrication\",\"categories\":[\"Semiconductors & chip manufacturing\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\"],\"tags\":[\"diy chips\",\"semiconductor fab\",\"integrated circuits\",\"hardware\",\"garage lab\"],\"quote\":\"It's fantastic and I highly recommend watching it.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"Imquk_3oFf4\",\"title\":\"\\\"Memories\\\" - 256 byte MSDOS intro\",\"channel\":\"Héll Mood\",\"summary\":\"Memories is a 256-byte MS-DOS demo that packs eight effects and MIDI soundscapes into a tiny program. It won first place at Revision 2020.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people share it as a mind-blowing example of demoscene craft and the reigning champion of 256-byte demos.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"256 byte demoscene\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\"],\"tags\":[\"demoscene\",\"256 bytes\",\"ms-dos\",\"revision 2020\",\"size coding\"],\"quote\":\"producing stuff that is just as mindblowing as it was when it all began.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"IrbJYsep45E\",\"title\":\"The Mathematics of Quantum Computers | Infinite Series\",\"channel\":\"PBS Infinite Series\",\"summary\":\"A PBS Infinite Series episode explaining the mathematics of quantum computers, including how qubits store complex numbers and how quantum computation differs from classical computation. Hosted by Kelsey Houston-Edwards.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as one of the most accessible yet accurate introductions to quantum computing, and part of a wider series that covers Shor's algorithm.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"quantum computing\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum computing\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"qubits\",\"quantum computers\",\"complex numbers\",\"shor's algorithm\",\"pbs infinite series\"],\"quote\":\"perhaps the most accessible and yet accurate explanations of quantum computing I have found so far.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"J-M98KLgaUU\",\"title\":\"Drone Delivery Was Supposed to be the Future. What Went Wrong?\",\"channel\":\"Wendover Productions\",\"summary\":\"Wendover Productions explains why drone delivery has not become the future once promised. It covers no-fly zones, costs and the difficulty of the final drop-off.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the standard answer when someone asks what happened to drone delivery.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"drone delivery\",\"categories\":[\"Robotics\",\"Business & economics (other)\",\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"drones\",\"logistics\",\"last-mile delivery\",\"faa regulation\",\"wendover\"],\"quote\":\"I think that Wendover has the best summary\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"Jes3bD6P0To\",\"title\":\"\\\"Tree-sitter - a new parsing system for programming tools\\\" by Max Brunsfeld\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Max Brunsfeld introduces Tree-sitter, a parsing system for developer tools that offers incremental parsing and robust error recovery through a uniform C API. The talk explains the design, based on LR extended to GLR parsing.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the best explanation of tree-sitter's design choices and as an accessible introduction to parser generators.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"incremental parsing with tree-sitter\",\"categories\":[\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"tree-sitter\",\"parsing\",\"glr\",\"error recovery\",\"developer tools\",\"editors\"],\"quote\":\"Highly recommend Max Brunsfield's strange loop talk if you want to learn more about the design choices behind tree-sitter\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"JfmTagWcqoE\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2016: Herb Sutter “Leak-Freedom in C++... By Default.”\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Herb Sutter's CppCon 2016 talk on lifetime safety in C++, aiming for code free of leaks and dangling pointers by default. It covers smart pointers, ownership, reference counting pitfalls and deferred reclamation resembling garbage collection.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it in memory management debates for its treatment of reference counting problems, destructor stack overflows and deferred pointers.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"c++ memory management\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\"],\"tags\":[\"c++\",\"smart pointers\",\"reference counting\",\"garbage collection\",\"raii\"],\"quote\":\"Check all of Herb Sutter's CppCon videos from 2015-2016, especially this one\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"KDRnEm-B3AI\",\"title\":\"Making Infrared Cooling Paint From Grocery Store Items (w/Novel CaCO₃ Microsphere Synthesis)\",\"channel\":\"NightHawkInLight\",\"summary\":\"NightHawkInLight shows how to make radiative sky cooling paint from grocery and hardware store materials, including a novel calcium carbonate microsphere synthesis. The paint can cool a surface below ambient air temperature in direct sunlight without electricity.\",\"why_hn\":\"It comes up whenever radiative cooling coatings are in the news, as a hands-on DIY guide that shows the approach working with household materials.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"radiative cooling paint\",\"categories\":[\"Chemistry\",\"Home, DIY & practical skills\",\"Materials science\"],\"tags\":[\"passive cooling\",\"diy chemistry\",\"calcium carbonate\",\"sky cooling\",\"coatings\"],\"quote\":\"Here is a frankly incredible video of how you could make such a paint\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"KJe9H6qS82I\",\"title\":\"SmoothLifeL\",\"channel\":\"Tim Hutton\",\"summary\":\"Tim Hutton's demonstration of SmoothLifeL, a continuous version of Conway's Game of Life using floating point values. It shows gliders traveling in any direction, rotating pairs, and elastic cord-like structures, with config details to run it yourself.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it whenever Game of Life or cellular automata come up, as the best visual example of a mesmerizing continuous-domain alternative.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"continuous cellular automata\",\"categories\":[\"Creative coding & generative art\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"smoothlife\",\"game of life\",\"cellular automata\",\"artificial life\",\"simulation\"],\"quote\":\"Must see evolution of GOL\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"KPUlgSRn6e0\",\"title\":\"The Gym of Life\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"Not Just Bikes argues that people in walkable, bikeable Dutch cities stay healthy because exercise is built into daily life, which he calls the gym of life. It contrasts this with car-dependent cities that require deliberate workouts.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it whenever fitness, aging or cycling comes up, as the clearest statement that city design is the best public health investment.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"urban planning and health\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\"],\"tags\":[\"not just bikes\",\"walkable cities\",\"cycling\",\"exercise\",\"netherlands\",\"public health\"],\"quote\":\"The best public investment to promote physical activity is designing cities to enable \\\"the gym of life\\\"!\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"KTHOwgpMIiU\",\"title\":\"Liberating Distributed Consensus - Heidi Howard\",\"channel\":\"Reactive Summit\",\"summary\":\"Heidi Howard's Reactive Summit 2018 talk on generalizing Paxos by weakening its original requirements. She shows Paxos is one point on a broad spectrum of consensus approaches.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an illuminating, approachable explanation of distributed consensus.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"distributed consensus\",\"categories\":[\"Distributed systems\"],\"tags\":[\"paxos\",\"raft\",\"heidi howard\",\"distributed systems\",\"flexible paxos\"],\"quote\":\"Heidi Howard is incredible and her work on distributed consensus is illuminating.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"KTJs-0EInW8\",\"title\":\"Stanford Seminar - Google's Steve Yegge on GROK\",\"channel\":\"Stanford Online\",\"summary\":\"Steve Yegge's Stanford seminar on GROK, Google's internal large-scale, cross-language source code analysis system. It covers how the system helps with code search and understanding across Google's huge codebase.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN refers to it as the main public description of Google's internal code analysis tooling, and as a precursor of ideas like LSP.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"code analysis tooling\",\"categories\":[\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"steve yegge\",\"grok\",\"google\",\"code search\",\"static analysis\",\"language tooling\"],\"quote\":\"this talk by Steve Yegge was both entertaining and informative\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"KePNhUJ2reI\",\"title\":\"How Time Becomes Space Inside a Black Hole | Space Time\",\"channel\":\"PBS Space Time\",\"summary\":\"A PBS Space Time episode explaining how space and time swap roles inside a black hole's event horizon. It is part of the channel's black hole series.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the cleanest visual explanation of time and space switching roles past the horizon, and a gateway to the channel's black hole videos.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"black hole physics\",\"categories\":[\"Cosmology & astrophysics\",\"Fundamental physics\"],\"tags\":[\"black holes\",\"event horizon\",\"general relativity\",\"spacetime\",\"pbs space time\"],\"quote\":\"I think this is the cleanest explanation with actual visual representation.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"Ljzj9Msli5o\",\"title\":\"Mike Mullane - Normalisation of deviance - IAFF - Part 1\",\"channel\":\"Stuart Walker\",\"summary\":\"Astronaut Mike Mullane explains normalisation of deviance, where teams gradually come to accept violations of their own safety standards. He uses the Challenger shuttle disaster as the main case, drawing on NASA's ignored warnings before the launch. It is the first part of a talk given to a fire-fighters' association.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it whenever a thread involves ignored warnings, shortcuts or routine risk, as the clearest introduction to the concept.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"normalisation of deviance\",\"categories\":[\"Space exploration & spaceflight\",\"Management, leadership & organizations\",\"Psychology & human behavior\"],\"tags\":[\"challenger disaster\",\"safety culture\",\"risk\",\"nasa\",\"organizational failure\"],\"quote\":\"He describes how the Challenger shuttle disaster happened despite internal memos about the problem years prior.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"LtFyP0qy9XU\",\"title\":\"Margin Call (9/9) Movie CLIP - It's Just Money (2011) HD\",\"channel\":\"Movieclips\",\"summary\":\"A clip from the 2011 film Margin Call in which a firm's CEO, played by Jeremy Irons, tells a senior executive about the cycles of financial crashes. Kevin Spacey's character says he wants out, and the scene ends on the line that it is just money.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN shares it in bubble and crash threads as a memorable scene about market cycles and about information work versus physical labor.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"financial crises\",\"categories\":[\"Film & TV clips\",\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\"],\"tags\":[\"margin call\",\"market crashes\",\"bubbles\",\"film scene\",\"wall street\"],\"quote\":\"It's a great scene and line that's always stayed with me.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"M3BM9TB-8yA\",\"title\":\"10 Things I Regret About Node.js - Ryan Dahl - JSConf EU\",\"channel\":\"JSConf\",\"summary\":\"Node.js creator Ryan Dahl discusses ten design mistakes he made in Node, including package.json, npm, and module resolution. The talk sets out the motivations behind his later project, Deno.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the classic talk on why Deno exists and on lessons from design mistakes in a widely used platform.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"nodejs design mistakes\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"node.js\",\"deno\",\"npm\",\"module system\",\"ryan dahl\",\"javascript\"],\"quote\":\"Ryan Dahl's great talk on design mistakes he made with node.js is something I think about all the time\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"MBT1OK6VAIU\",\"title\":\"Pitfall Classic Postmortem With David Crane Panel at GDC 2011 (Atari 2600)\",\"channel\":\"GameSpot\",\"summary\":\"David Crane, designer of Pitfall for the Atari 2600, gives a GDC 2011 postmortem on the game's design challenges. He covers how he fit a large world into the console's tiny memory. The session ends with Q&A.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it for the story of how Pitfall's levels are generated procedurally from a seeded counter, within 4K of ROM.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"atari 2600 game design\",\"categories\":[\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Game design\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"pitfall\",\"procedural generation\",\"gdc\",\"retro games\",\"memory constraints\"],\"quote\":\"This is a great talk for those who love such things\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"MRqh8oLY7Ik\",\"title\":\"Why Air Quality Matters\",\"channel\":\"David Heinemeier Hansson\",\"summary\":\"David Heinemeier Hansson explains what he learned about indoor air quality after his new house made his family sick. He covers CO2, VOCs, off-gassing, HVAC and ventilation, and cognitive effects, presented at a Basecamp meetup.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a fascinating talk that convinced many to buy air quality monitors, with citations to studies.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"indoor air quality\",\"categories\":[\"Power grid, heating & buildings\",\"Home, DIY & practical skills\"],\"tags\":[\"co2\",\"voc\",\"hvac\",\"air monitors\",\"cognitive performance\"],\"quote\":\"Fascinating talk, worth watching.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"MdmQUlD7P40\",\"title\":\"Matt Might - Winning the War on Error Solving the Halting Problem and Curing Cancer\",\"channel\":\"Curry On!\",\"summary\":\"Matt Might's Curry On talk on ending errors in code, both software and the human genome. He covers conservative approximation of the halting problem via static analysis and a computational approach to precision medicine.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a jaw-dropping, must-see talk that gives an amazing intro to static analysis alongside Might's personal story.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"static analysis and precision medicine\",\"categories\":[\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"Computability & complexity theory\",\"Biology & biotechnology\"],\"tags\":[\"static analysis\",\"halting problem\",\"abstract interpretation\",\"genomics\",\"software security\"],\"quote\":\"If you haven't seen it, Might's talk \\\"Winning the War on Error: Solving the Halting Problem and Curing Cancer\\\" is amazing, a must-see.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"MkJkyMuBm3g\",\"title\":\"Toybox vs BusyBox - Rob Landley, hobbyist\",\"channel\":\"The Linux Foundation\",\"summary\":\"Rob Landley compares Toybox and BusyBox and surveys options for embedded system command-line tools, including GNU. He also tells the story of leaving BusyBox, Toybox becoming Android's command line, and licensing.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it for the toybox intro and for Landley's candid account of software licensing and why he created the 0BSD license.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"toybox and busybox\",\"categories\":[\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\",\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Operating systems & kernels\"],\"tags\":[\"toybox\",\"busybox\",\"rob landley\",\"0bsd\",\"software licensing\",\"embedded linux\"],\"quote\":\"A great talk on software licensing made by the creator of the 0BSD license\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"MsbiO8EAsGw\",\"title\":\"E-paper hacking: fastest possible refresh rate\",\"channel\":\"Applied Science\",\"summary\":\"Ben Krasnow of Applied Science explains how electrophoretic e-paper displays work and how he modified the driver firmware and waveform lookup tables of a 4.2-inch panel to reach roughly 3Hz partial refresh. It covers the tweakable constants in the refresh process and touches on colored e-ink microcapsules.\",\"why_hn\":\"Whenever e-ink refresh speed or driving waveforms come up, HN points to this as the clearest hands-on explanation of how these displays work and how to hack them.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"e-paper display hacking\",\"categories\":[\"Consumer devices & gadgets\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\",\"Audio & video engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"e-ink\",\"e-paper\",\"refresh rate\",\"firmware\",\"reverse engineering\",\"displays\"],\"quote\":\"Here's a fascinating video on how these displays work, and how you can modify the firmware of the driver to get faster refresh rates\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"MtzCLd93SyU\",\"title\":\"Sid Meier's Psychology of Game Design\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"Civilization creator Sid Meier's GDC 2010 talk on applying psychology to game design. He discusses player perception, randomness, and win states, and why designers must account for how players experience events.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people cite it when discussing perceived randomness, luck and player experience in games, and say more designers should heed it.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"game design psychology\",\"categories\":[\"Game design\",\"Psychology & human behavior\"],\"tags\":[\"sid meier\",\"gdc\",\"randomness\",\"player psychology\",\"civilization\"],\"quote\":\"More people need to listen to what Sid Meier has said on this.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"N3bJnKmeNJY\",\"title\":\"How To Make Infrared Cooling Paint (Electricity Free Air Conditioning)\",\"channel\":\"NightHawkInLight\",\"summary\":\"NightHawkInLight shows how to make ultra-white radiative paint that cools surfaces below ambient temperature without electricity. He walks through the research papers and the household-materials process.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a practical, well-researched DIY demonstration of passive radiative cooling.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"passive radiative cooling\",\"categories\":[\"Materials science\",\"Chemistry\",\"Power grid, heating & buildings\"],\"tags\":[\"radiative cooling\",\"diy chemistry\",\"paint\",\"passive cooling\",\"materials science\"],\"quote\":\"This YouTuber[0] does an excellent job recreating chemistry research from papers and improving on the techniques to be accomplished with household materials.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"NP9AIUT9nos\",\"title\":\"Lone Star Ruby Conference 2010 Real Software Engineering by Glenn Vanderburg\",\"channel\":\"Confreaks\",\"summary\":\"Glenn Vanderburg argues at Lone Star Ruby Conference 2010 that university-style software engineering does not work, and asks what real software engineering would look like. He revisits the waterfall model and what engineering means in other fields.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it for changing minds about whether programmers can be called engineers, and for its take on how waterfall was misread.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software engineering\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"waterfall\",\"engineering discipline\",\"software process\",\"agile\",\"conference talk\"],\"quote\":\"I always thought calling programmers engineers was very weird until I watched this great talk\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"NcOb3Dilzjc\",\"title\":\"James Burke Legend Connections 1 The Trigger Effect\",\"channel\":\"Tim Callinan\",\"summary\":\"The first episode of James Burke's Connections series, 'The Trigger Effect', explores how modern society depends on interlinked technological systems and how quickly it breaks down when they fail, starting with a power blackout.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended whenever outages and cascading failures come up, as a classic on interdependent systems.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"technological interdependence\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\",\"Power grid, heating & buildings\"],\"tags\":[\"james burke\",\"connections\",\"history of technology\",\"power grid\",\"systems failure\"],\"quote\":\"You may enjoy the first episode of James Burke's Connections (\\\"The Trigger Effect\\\"), if you've not seen it.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"Nrp_LZ-XGsY\",\"title\":\"The Functional Programmer's Toolkit - Scott Wlaschin\",\"channel\":\"NDC Conferences\",\"summary\":\"Scott Wlaschin demystifies functional programming terms like monads, monoids, functors and catamorphisms by showing how they fit into a small toolkit. He applies them to problems such as missing data, list handling, and functional dependency injection.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the best beginner-friendly introduction to monoids, monads and other FP vocabulary.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"functional programming\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"monads\",\"monoids\",\"functors\",\"f#\",\"design patterns\",\"wlaschin\"],\"quote\":\"This was by far the best introduction to monoid, monads and few other functional programming terms.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"OKY6BGcx37k\",\"title\":\"Talking Funny - HBO\",\"channel\":\"Ken Jones\",\"summary\":\"An HBO special in which Jerry Seinfeld, Chris Rock, Ricky Gervais and Louis CK discuss the craft of stand-up comedy. They talk about writing jokes, technique and how they handle old material.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a rare look at how top comedians think about craft, motivation and process.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"stand-up comedy craft\",\"categories\":[\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"comedy\",\"joke writing\",\"seinfeld\",\"chris rock\",\"creative process\"],\"quote\":\"is worth watching for anyone interested in that aspect of comedy\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"ObhK8lUfIlc\",\"title\":\"GDC 2018: John McDonald (Valve) - Using Deep Learning to Combat Cheating in CSGO\",\"channel\":\"max4warn\",\"summary\":\"A GDC 2018 talk by Valve's John McDonald on VACNet, a deep learning system that analyzes CS:GO gameplay to detect cheaters. It covers how the model is trained on human-reviewed Overwatch cases and how its output feeds human moderation.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the definitive example of server-side, behavioral, non-invasive anti-cheat using machine learning, often as a counterpoint to kernel-level anti-cheat.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"machine learning anti-cheat\",\"categories\":[\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"Game programming & engines\",\"Security & hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"vacnet\",\"cs:go\",\"anti-cheat\",\"deep learning\",\"valve\",\"gdc\"],\"quote\":\"The actual presentation is worth listening to if you have the time.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"Obt-vMVdM8s\",\"title\":\"Understanding the Python GIL\",\"channel\":\"David Beazley\",\"summary\":\"David Beazley's PyCon 2010 talk explains how the Python Global Interpreter Lock works and shows experiments where multithreaded code runs slower, even on more cores, due to lock contention and context-switching overhead.\",\"why_hn\":\"The canonical recommendation for understanding the GIL, praised both for its clarity and for how well it explains a deep technical topic.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"python gil\",\"categories\":[\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"Software performance & optimization\"],\"tags\":[\"python\",\"gil\",\"concurrency\",\"threading\",\"david beazley\",\"performance\"],\"quote\":\"My favorite example is Beazley's GIL talk\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"P55t6eryY3g\",\"title\":\"The Alt-Right Playbook: How to Radicalize a Normie\",\"channel\":\"Innuendo Studios\",\"summary\":\"A video essay from the Alt-Right Playbook series that explains how extremist movements draw ordinary people in, using deniable rhetoric, bad-faith games, and gradual escalation. It draws on books about the alt-right, male radicalization, and cult-style brainwashing.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it in moderation and politics threads as a clear account of why hard rules fail against dogwhistles and how isolated people get recruited.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"online radicalization\",\"categories\":[\"Social issues & culture\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"alt-right\",\"radicalization\",\"content moderation\",\"video essay\",\"extremism\"],\"quote\":\"I think Innuendo Studios' video on this problem is one of the best explanations\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"P73REgj-3UE\",\"title\":\"Primitive Technology: Tiled Roof Hut\",\"channel\":\"Primitive Technology\",\"summary\":\"The Primitive Technology channel shows the construction of a tiled roof hut in the bush using only primitive tools and materials made in earlier videos. It includes tiles, a kiln, clay walls and underfloor heating.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN shares it as a mesmerizing example of building shelter and tools entirely from scratch.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"primitive building\",\"categories\":[\"Home, DIY & practical skills\",\"Civil engineering & construction\"],\"tags\":[\"primitive technology\",\"roof tiles\",\"kiln\",\"hut\",\"handmade\"],\"quote\":\"His videos are a great watch\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"P9M__yYbsZ4\",\"title\":\"Thorium: An energy solution - THORIUM REMIX 2011\",\"channel\":\"gordonmcdowell\",\"summary\":\"A 2011 remix of over six hours of talks by Kirk Sorensen and other thorium advocates, explaining thorium as a nuclear fuel and the liquid-fluoride thorium reactor (LFTR). It argues LFTRs could produce energy without long-lived transuranic waste.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN users send it to anyone asking about thorium or molten salt reactors as a compact overview that answers common anti-nuclear objections.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"thorium reactors\",\"categories\":[\"Nuclear power & fusion\"],\"tags\":[\"lftr\",\"molten salt reactor\",\"kirk sorensen\",\"nuclear energy\",\"thorium\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend this video on liquid-fluoride thorium reactors (LFTR)\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"PLyEO0jNt6M\",\"title\":\"Explained: \\\"Go Fast\\\" UFO Video - Not Low and Not Fast - Like a Balloon!\",\"channel\":\"Mick West\",\"summary\":\"Mick West analyzes the Navy \\\"Go Fast\\\" UFO footage and shows with simple trigonometry and the data overlay that the object is slow and high up, consistent with a balloon. He explains how parallax makes it look fast and argues the original TV claims were wrong.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters repeatedly cite it as a checkable, math-based debunking of the famous UFO clips whenever UAP stories come up.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ufo video debunking\",\"categories\":[\"Religion, belief & skepticism\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"go fast\",\"parallax\",\"trigonometry\",\"uap\",\"skepticism\"],\"quote\":\"See for yourself. This is the most clear case of misidentification.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"PY9DcIMGxMs\",\"title\":\"Everything you think you know about addiction is wrong | Johann Hari | TED\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"Johann Hari's TED talk arguing that addiction is driven largely by disconnection and environment rather than chemical hooks alone. He draws on his personal experience with addicted relatives and on research and policy examples from around the world.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters quote it for the line that the opposite of addiction is connection, and recommend it as a more plausible account of addiction than the standard model.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"addiction\",\"categories\":[\"Mental health & neurodiversity\",\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Psychology & human behavior\"],\"tags\":[\"addiction\",\"ted talk\",\"drug policy\",\"connection\",\"johann hari\"],\"quote\":\"This is my favourite addiction talk.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"PbmJkMhmrVI\",\"title\":\"dark matter is not a theory\",\"channel\":\"Angela Collier \",\"summary\":\"Physicist Angela Collier argues that dark matter is not a theory but a set of observations, covering rotation curves, the CMB and why modified gravity struggles to explain them.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it to correct the common misconception about dark matter and as a counterpoint to popular science coverage, though one commenter found it muddled.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"dark matter\",\"categories\":[\"Cosmology & astrophysics\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"dark matter\",\"rotation curves\",\"modified gravity\",\"cosmology\",\"angela collier\"],\"quote\":\"If you're interested in learning more I recommend you watch \\\"dark matter is not a theory\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"QMYfkOtYYlg\",\"title\":\"ASCII fluid dynamics -- IOCCC2012 endoh1.c\",\"channel\":\"Yusuke Endoh\",\"summary\":\"A 3-minute demo of a tiny IOCCC 2012 winning C program that simulates fluid dynamics in an 80x25 terminal, using any text (including its own source) as input. The video shows the simulation running to Handel's Water Music.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters repeatedly point to it as an astonishing, beautiful piece of obfuscated-C code golf.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"obfuscated c programming\",\"categories\":[\"Creative coding & generative art\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Computer graphics & rendering\"],\"tags\":[\"ioccc\",\"fluid simulation\",\"ascii art\",\"c\",\"code golf\"],\"quote\":\"truly a work of art\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"Qam5BkXIEhQ\",\"title\":\"Civilizations at the End of Time: Black Hole Farming\",\"channel\":\"Isaac Arthur\",\"summary\":\"An Isaac Arthur episode on civilizations in the dark, post-stellar universe, including black hole farming, accretion disks and the Penrose process as energy sources.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it for black hole energy and colonization ideas and recommends the whole channel.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"black hole civilizations\",\"categories\":[\"Cosmology & astrophysics\",\"Futurism & technology and society\"],\"tags\":[\"black hole farming\",\"penrose process\",\"far future\",\"energy harvesting\",\"isaac arthur\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend the whole channel.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"QsBT5EQt348\",\"title\":\"Overpopulation – The Human Explosion Explained\",\"channel\":\"Kurzgesagt – In a Nutshell\",\"summary\":\"A Kurzgesagt explainer on why the human population exploded and why growth is slowing, drawing on Our World in Data. It argues that birth rates fall as countries develop, easing overpopulation fears.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it in population, climate, and birth-rate threads as a clear, light-hearted overview of the demographic transition.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"world population growth\",\"categories\":[\"Social issues & culture\",\"Data visualization & information design\"],\"tags\":[\"overpopulation\",\"demographics\",\"birth rates\",\"kurzgesagt\",\"our world in data\"],\"quote\":\"Kurzgesagt has a good video on this\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"RKT-sKtR970\",\"title\":\"Simulating an Entire Car Engine (yes, it makes noise)\",\"channel\":\"AngeTheGreat\",\"summary\":\"AngeTheGreat describes building a physically realistic internal combustion engine simulator in C++, drawing on fluid dynamics, physics and chemistry. It generates procedural audio for engines such as a V8.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a standout example of physics-based sound synthesis and engine simulation.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"engine simulation\",\"categories\":[\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\",\"Game programming & engines\",\"Computer graphics & rendering\"],\"tags\":[\"engine simulator\",\"procedural audio\",\"physics simulation\",\"c++\",\"game development\"],\"quote\":\"AngeTheGreat's incredible video series showing his engine simulator- absolutely worth checking out\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"RMxB7zA-e4Y\",\"title\":\"Electric car chargers aren't chargers at all – EVSE Explained\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connections\",\"summary\":\"Technology Connections explains that the so-called EV charger in an electric car's charging cable or wall unit is really an EVSE, a smart switch that safely delivers AC power. The actual charger is inside the car. The video covers the safety signaling and mechanisms involved.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the clearest introduction to what is inside an AC EV charging station and why it is much simpler than people assume.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"EV charging equipment\",\"categories\":[\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\",\"Power grid, heating & buildings\",\"Electronics & circuit design\"],\"tags\":[\"evse\",\"electric vehicles\",\"ac charging\",\"electrical safety\",\"j1772\"],\"quote\":\"A quick intro into EVSE and why there is no power 'charger' in them\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"RQY6WGOoYis\",\"title\":\"Do Your Buses Get Stuck in Traffic? (The Downs-Thomson Paradox)\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"Not Just Bikes explains the Downs-Thomson paradox using Amsterdam and Toronto: road widening doesn't fix congestion, and buses stuck in traffic make transit slow. It argues for giving transit its own priority infrastructure.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a clear introduction to why widening roads fails and why transit-only lanes matter.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"urban transit and congestion\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\"],\"tags\":[\"downs-thomson paradox\",\"public transit\",\"urban planning\",\"traffic congestion\",\"netherlands\"],\"quote\":\"NotJustBikes has a good introduction to it\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"RmIgJ64z6Y4\",\"title\":\"34C3 -  Dude, you broke the Future!\",\"channel\":\"media.ccc.de\",\"summary\":\"Charles Stross's 34C3 talk on how science fiction gets the future wrong. He argues that corporations are slow, human-powered AIs that optimize for profit, and that regulation lags behind accelerating technology.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN returns to it whenever corporations as paperclip-maximizer AIs or AI risk come up.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"corporations as artificial intelligence\",\"categories\":[\"Futurism & technology and society\",\"AI safety, AGI & impact\",\"Literature & writing craft\"],\"tags\":[\"charles stross\",\"ai risk\",\"corporations\",\"science fiction\",\"regulation\"],\"quote\":\"I cannot recommend enough Charles Stross' 34C3 talk on this very topic.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"RrpajcAgR1E\",\"title\":\"Identity 2.0 Keynote\",\"channel\":\"Dan Lewis\",\"summary\":\"Dick Hardt's fast-paced OSCON keynote on Identity 2.0 and the move toward user-centric digital identity. It is also famous for its rapid-fire, many-slide presentation style.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters hold it up as one of the most engaging tech talks ever given, both for its identity ideas and its slide technique.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"digital identity and presentation style\",\"categories\":[\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"identity 2.0\",\"oscon\",\"presentation style\",\"dick hardt\",\"user-centric identity\",\"slides\"],\"quote\":\"if you’ve never seen it, I encourage you to watch it\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"RuCnZClWwpQ\",\"title\":\"Smelting Iron in Africa (A DEMONSTRATION)\",\"channel\":\"Christopher Roy\",\"summary\":\"A 105-minute film of a West African smith family in Burkina Faso smelting iron from ore, from making charcoal and building the kiln to forging tools. It is a cultural recreation filmed for the camera, not current practice.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an amazing documentary of traditional ironmaking, often used as a baseline for the labor required.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"traditional iron smelting\",\"categories\":[\"Woodworking, metalworking & workshop skills\",\"Materials science\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"bloomery\",\"ironmaking\",\"burkina faso\",\"metallurgy\",\"charcoal\"],\"quote\":\"One of my favorite videos is a documentary of a village in Africa that goes through the process of producing iron tools from scratch\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"S27pHKBEp30\",\"title\":\"LSTM is dead. Long Live Transformers!\",\"channel\":\"Seattle Applied Deep Learning\",\"summary\":\"Leo Dirac explains how transformer models have replaced LSTMs in NLP. He gives background from bag of words through RNNs and LSTMs, then a technical deep dive into multi-headed self-attention and positional encoding, with sample code.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a clear explanation of why RNNs fall short and how transformers solve it.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"transformers in nlp\",\"categories\":[\"LLMs & generative AI\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"Computer vision, speech & NLP\"],\"tags\":[\"transformers\",\"lstm\",\"self-attention\",\"positional encoding\",\"nlp\"],\"quote\":\"has gotten tons of compliments and keeps people watching year after year\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"Sx0J7dIlL7c\",\"title\":\"Democrats Need to Face Why Trump Won | The Ezra Klein Show\",\"channel\":\"The Ezra Klein Show\",\"summary\":\"Ezra Klein interviews Democratic pollster David Shor, who walks through survey data and charts explaining the 2024 election results. They discuss voter shifts, the education divide and what Democrats got wrong.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a data-driven explanation of the 2024 election and voter trends.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"2024 us election\",\"categories\":[\"US politics & elections\",\"Data science & statistics\"],\"tags\":[\"david shor\",\"ezra klein\",\"polling\",\"democrats\",\"voter demographics\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend this interview between Ezra Klein and Democrat strategist David Shor\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"TTFoJQSd48c\",\"title\":\"If Brains are Computers, Who Designs the Software? - with Daniel Dennett\",\"channel\":\"The Royal Institution\",\"summary\":\"Daniel Dennett's Royal Institution lecture on whether the brain is a computer and where its software comes from. He argues that minds are built by evolution and culture, with competence arising without comprehension.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as an excellent entry point to Dennett's thinking on minds, memes and competence without comprehension.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"philosophy of mind\",\"categories\":[\"Consciousness & cognition\",\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Evolution & genetics\"],\"tags\":[\"daniel dennett\",\"consciousness\",\"cognitive science\",\"evolution\",\"memes\"],\"quote\":\"I discovered him with the excellent Royal Institution talk\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"TlLIOgWYVpI\",\"title\":\"Herbert Wolverson - Procedural Map Generation Techniques\",\"channel\":\"Roguelike Celebration\",\"summary\":\"Herbert Wolverson's Roguelike Celebration 2020 talk surveying procedural map generation techniques, including integration of hand-crafted prefabs and vaults.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a fun, informative survey of easy-to-implement dungeon generation methods from the author of the Rust roguelike tutorial.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"procedural map generation\",\"categories\":[\"Game programming & engines\",\"Creative coding & generative art\"],\"tags\":[\"procedural generation\",\"roguelike\",\"dungeon generation\",\"game development\",\"rust\"],\"quote\":\"My favorite from that collection on map generation is by Herbert Wolverson\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"UEfP1OKKz_Q\",\"title\":\"British Plugs Are Better Than All Other Plugs, And Here's Why\",\"channel\":\"Tom Scott\",\"summary\":\"Tom Scott explains the design of the British BS 1363 plug and socket, including fuses, shutters and safety features, and why he thinks it beats other plugs.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN treats it as the obligatory video to share whenever plugs and sockets come up, praised for its safety reasoning.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"british plug design\",\"categories\":[\"Product & industrial design\",\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Engineering & making (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"bs 1363\",\"power plugs\",\"electrical safety\",\"tom scott\",\"fuses\"],\"quote\":\"There's the obligatory Tom Scott video here, which always gets shared, deservedly so\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"VO6XEQIsCoM\",\"title\":\"The paradox of choice | Barry Schwartz | TED\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"Psychologist Barry Schwartz argues in this TED talk that abundant choice makes people more paralyzed and dissatisfied rather than freer and happier.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the standard talk on how too many options lead to paralysis and second-guessing.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"paradox of choice\",\"categories\":[\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\",\"Ideas & learning (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"choice overload\",\"decision making\",\"happiness\",\"psychology\",\"ted\"],\"quote\":\"My favourite TED talk pairing is Dan Gilbert's Surprising Science of Happiness\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"Vjm8P8utT5g\",\"title\":\"[TAS] Pokémon Yellow - Arbitrary Code Execution\",\"channel\":\"MrWint\",\"summary\":\"A tool-assisted speedrun of Pokemon Yellow by MrWint that uses arbitrary code execution to push Game Boy hardware to its limits. Through crafted inputs it injects code and ends up running other programs, such as Tetris, inside the game.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the standard demonstration of arbitrary code execution on a Game Boy, and of glitches and bugs being turned into a working computer.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"game boy arbitrary code execution\",\"categories\":[\"Speedrunning, glitches & game hacking\",\"Game history & retro gaming\"],\"tags\":[\"pokemon yellow\",\"tool-assisted speedrun\",\"code injection\",\"game boy\",\"glitches\"],\"quote\":\"One of the crazier uses of ACE in Gameboy Pokemon games exploits\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"Wp-WiNXH6hI\",\"title\":\"Carl Sagan testifying before Congress in 1985 on climate change\",\"channel\":\"carlsagandotcom\",\"summary\":\"Carl Sagan's December 1985 testimony to Congress on the greenhouse effect, about 15 minutes long. He lays out CO2 warming, the risk of runaway effects and the danger of continued coal burning.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters use it as proof that the climate problem was clearly explained decades ago, and as a model of clear communication to a broad audience.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"climate change\",\"categories\":[\"Climate science & climate change\",\"US politics & elections\"],\"tags\":[\"carl sagan\",\"greenhouse effect\",\"congress testimony\",\"coal\",\"science communication\"],\"quote\":\"The address Carl Sagan made in 1985 is well worth watching\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"X1XoCQm5JSQ\",\"title\":\"Learn Jacques Pépin's famous omelet techniques\",\"channel\":\"American Masters PBS\",\"summary\":\"In this American Masters clip, Jacques Pepin shows two different techniques for making a perfect omelet, a dish he is famous for teaching America.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people call it a favorite cooking video and a model of mastery, showing two omelet styles in about five minutes.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"omelet technique\",\"categories\":[\"Cooking & food\"],\"tags\":[\"jacques pepin\",\"omelet\",\"french cooking\",\"cooking technique\",\"eggs\"],\"quote\":\"I've watched this Jacques Pépin video countless times\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"X8jsijhllIA\",\"title\":\"But what are Hamming codes? The origin of error correction\",\"channel\":\"3Blue1Brown\",\"summary\":\"3Blue1Brown's discovery-oriented introduction to error correction codes, building up Hamming codes from the puzzle of locating a flipped bit. It explains the parity-check idea and why it is so efficient.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an invaluable, intuitive explanation of Hamming codes that pairs well with hardware-oriented treatments.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"error correcting codes\",\"categories\":[\"Information theory & signal processing\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"hamming codes\",\"error correction\",\"parity\",\"information theory\",\"3blue1brown\"],\"quote\":\"I would strongly recommend anyone interested in the topic to check out any of these videos\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"XDM3MqHln8U\",\"title\":\"29C3 Panel: Jesselyn Radack, Thomas Drake, William Binney on whistleblowing and surveillance\",\"channel\":\"thisdayinwikileaks\",\"summary\":\"A 29C3 panel with whistleblowers Jesselyn Radack, Thomas Drake and William Binney. They talk about what happens when exposing secret government power becomes a crime, and about surveillance abuses.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a first-hand account from NSA whistleblowers, often linked in surveillance and Snowden threads.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"whistleblowing and surveillance\",\"categories\":[\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\"],\"tags\":[\"nsa\",\"whistleblowers\",\"thomas drake\",\"william binney\",\"29c3\"],\"quote\":\"You may be interested in watching this talk from 29c3 where Drake and Binney told their stories in their own words\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"XUT8ec24anM\",\"title\":\"Missing Teen's Friends Go On TV To Plead For Her Release, Gossip About Ugly Classmates\",\"channel\":\"The Onion\",\"summary\":\"An Onion News Network sketch in which two teenage girls appear on TV to plead for their kidnapped friend's release, then start gossiping about classmates. It is a satirical parody of teen behavior and sensational news.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters call it one of the best and funniest Onion News Network videos and a sharp parody of teen girls.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"satirical news parody\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\"],\"tags\":[\"the onion\",\"onn\",\"satire\",\"teens\",\"parody\"],\"quote\":\"this parody made by The Onion is the best depiction I've ever seen\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"YSLV7t9DvN8\",\"title\":\"Jorge Luis Borges: This Craft of Verse Lectures - thepostarchive\",\"channel\":\"thepostarchive\",\"summary\":\"The six Norton Lectures Jorge Luis Borges gave in 1967-68, titled This Craft of Verse, on topics including the riddle of poetry, metaphor, storytelling, translation and a poet's creed. The recording runs over four hours.\",\"why_hn\":\"Strongly recommended as under-appreciated lectures showing Borges' erudition, delivered in a hypnotic voice from memory.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"poetry and literature\",\"categories\":[\"Literature & writing craft\",\"Language & linguistics\"],\"tags\":[\"borges\",\"norton lectures\",\"poetry\",\"metaphor\",\"translation\"],\"quote\":\"These lectures by Borges are seriously under-appreciated\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"YbZ3zDzDnrw\",\"title\":\"Raft lecture (Raft user study)\",\"channel\":\"Diego Ongaro\",\"summary\":\"John Ousterhout lectures on the Raft consensus algorithm, made for the Raft user study comparing how students learn Raft and Paxos. It covers leader election and log repair.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a clearer introduction to Raft than the paper and as part of a well-designed understandability study.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"distributed consensus\",\"categories\":[\"Distributed systems\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"raft\",\"paxos\",\"leader election\",\"john ousterhout\",\"distributed systems\"],\"quote\":\"The 2 videos do an excellent job of explaining both the Paxos and Raft algorithms and the key differences between them.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"Z2DU0qLfPIY\",\"title\":\"ElixirConf 2018 - Keynote - Chris McCord\",\"channel\":\"ElixirConf\",\"summary\":\"Chris McCord's ElixirConf 2018 keynote introduces Phoenix LiveView, which renders HTML on the server and updates the page over websockets. It includes a demo showing that latency is not the bottleneck one might expect.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the original LiveView announcement and demo, a compelling argument for server-side rendering as an alternative to JavaScript front ends.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"phoenix liveview\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Web development\"],\"tags\":[\"elixir\",\"phoenix\",\"liveview\",\"server-side rendering\",\"websockets\"],\"quote\":\"you can't ignore it, the coolest thing happening right now about server-side rendering\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"ZKC3hiyLeRc\",\"title\":\"Dr. Jason Fung - 'The Aetiology of Obesity'\",\"channel\":\"Low Carb Down Under\",\"summary\":\"Dr. Jason Fung, a nephrologist, presents his view of the causes of obesity and type 2 diabetes, focusing on hormones such as insulin rather than calories alone. The talk draws on research and his Intensive Dietary Management program.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a research-heavy introduction to how food type and hormones affect fat storage.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"obesity and insulin\",\"categories\":[\"Nutrition & diet science\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"jason fung\",\"obesity\",\"insulin\",\"low carb\",\"fasting\",\"type 2 diabetes\"],\"quote\":\"Here's a good talk by a doctor, heavy on research, that's a decent introduction\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"Ztc6QPNUqls\",\"title\":\"Your Mass is NOT From the Higgs Boson\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium explains that the Higgs boson accounts for only a small fraction of everyday mass. Most of a proton's mass comes from the energy of the gluon field binding its quarks.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a clear explanation of where mass actually comes from.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"origin of mass\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum physics\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"higgs boson\",\"quarks\",\"gluons\",\"proton mass\",\"particle physics\"],\"quote\":\"This video explains where the mass of a proton comes from\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"_-mBeYC2KGc\",\"title\":\"Portable Air Conditioners - Why you shouldn't like them\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connections\",\"summary\":\"Technology Connections explains why single-hose portable air conditioners are inefficient and often a poor choice compared with window units. It details how the single hose pulls in warm outside air and wastes energy.\",\"why_hn\":\"The go-to recommendation to watch before buying a portable AC, cited in nearly every thread on cooling.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"portable air conditioners\",\"categories\":[\"Power grid, heating & buildings\",\"Consumer devices & gadgets\"],\"tags\":[\"air conditioning\",\"hvac\",\"energy efficiency\",\"single-hose\",\"window unit\"],\"quote\":\"If you are in the market for a portable AC I highly recommend you watch this video first\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"_L3gNaAVjQ4\",\"title\":\"George Hotz: Hacking the Simulation & Learning to Drive with Neural Nets | Lex Fridman Podcast #132\",\"channel\":\"Lex Fridman\",\"summary\":\"Lex Fridman interviews George Hotz, founder of Comma.ai, in a three-hour conversation. Topics include self-driving, Tesla and Waymo, crypto, GPT-3, immortality and book recommendations.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters recommend it as an entertaining, wide-ranging interview, and cite it for Hotz's views on end-to-end neural-net driving.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"self-driving and comma.ai\",\"categories\":[\"Self-driving & autonomous vehicles\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\"],\"tags\":[\"george hotz\",\"comma.ai\",\"lex fridman\",\"self-driving\",\"tesla\"],\"quote\":\"Lots of topics covered: self-driving, immortality, UFOs, cryptocurrencies, Tesla/Waymo/OpenAI, GTP-3, AI, book recommendations.\",\"recommenders\":6,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"_P9HqHVPeik\",\"title\":\"Stephen Wolfram's Introduction to the Wolfram Language\",\"channel\":\"Wolfram\",\"summary\":\"Stephen Wolfram gives a 13-minute tour of the Wolfram Language, a symbolic, functional programming language with a built-in knowledgebase. He demos querying large datasets, interactivity and deployment.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters point to it as a compact, impressive demo of what the language can do.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"wolfram language\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Programming language design\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"wolfram language\",\"symbolic programming\",\"mathematica\",\"functional programming\",\"live coding\"],\"quote\":\"The demos are all pretty impressive\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"_bDXXWQxK38\",\"title\":\"A New Way to Achieve Nuclear Fusion: Helion\",\"channel\":\"Real Engineering\",\"summary\":\"Real Engineering's Brian McManus explains Helion's approach to nuclear fusion: colliding two plasma rings, compressing them magnetically, and capturing energy directly from the changing magnetic flux rather than boiling water. It covers why this pulsed design sidesteps problems of tokamaks and laser fusion.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the go-to accessible explainer whenever HN discusses Helion or alternative fusion designs.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"nuclear fusion\",\"categories\":[\"Nuclear power & fusion\",\"Fundamental physics\"],\"tags\":[\"helion\",\"fusion reactor\",\"aneutronic fusion\",\"pulsed fusion\",\"energy\"],\"quote\":\"I recommend you watch this excellent video by Real Engineering\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"_bP80DEAbuo\",\"title\":\"Datacenters Behaving Like Acoustic Weapons\",\"channel\":\"Benn Jordan\",\"summary\":\"Benn Jordan investigates infrasound and low-frequency noise from data centers, including xAI's Colossus and a crypto mine, and reports his own experiments on the effects. The video covers the health and community impacts on nearby residents.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in data center siting debates as evidence that noise and infrasound can seriously affect people living nearby.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"data center infrasound noise\",\"categories\":[\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\",\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\"],\"tags\":[\"infrasound\",\"data centers\",\"noise pollution\",\"xai colossus\",\"community impact\"],\"quote\":\"an insightful, scary 30 min video covering the datacenter infrasound noise\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"aDejwCGdUV8\",\"title\":\"Joe Rogan Experience #725 - Graham Hancock & Randall Carlson\",\"channel\":\"PowerfulJRE\",\"summary\":\"A three-hour Joe Rogan Experience conversation with Graham Hancock and Randall Carlson about lost civilizations, the Younger Dryas impact hypothesis and Earth's cataclysmic history.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people point to it as an entry point to the Hancock and Carlson lost-civilization theories, while noting the ideas are controversial.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"lost civilizations\",\"categories\":[\"World history\",\"Geology & earth science\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"graham hancock\",\"randall carlson\",\"younger dryas\",\"ancient history\",\"joe rogan\"],\"quote\":\"Here's where to go to get on the Graham Hancock/Randall Carlson train\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"aHun58mz3vI\",\"title\":\"Charlie Brooker's How to Report the News - Newswipe - BBC\",\"channel\":\"BBC\",\"summary\":\"A short satirical segment from Charlie Brooker's Newswipe that lampoons the formulaic conventions of television news reporting, from stock shots to filler street interviews and news-speak.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN invokes it as the best spoof of how news is formulaically produced, often when discussing media quality or automated news.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"news media satire\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"charlie brooker\",\"newswipe\",\"bbc\",\"satire\",\"news reporting\"],\"quote\":null,\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"aO3JgPUJ6iQ\",\"title\":\"Chinese Invisibility Cloak Hoax DESTROYED!!!\",\"channel\":\"Captain Disillusion\",\"summary\":\"Captain Disillusion debunks a viral Chinese invisibility cloak video while parodying popular educational channels. Along the way he explains chroma keying and green screen techniques.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an entertaining explanation of chroma keying, green screens and how cameras process color.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"chroma keying and vfx\",\"categories\":[\"Animation & visual effects\",\"Satire & parody\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"captain disillusion\",\"green screen\",\"chroma key\",\"debunking\",\"visual effects\"],\"quote\":\"My favorite video about green screens and chroma keying, from Captain Disillusion\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"bY2FlayomlE\",\"title\":\"C++ Exceptions for Smaller Firmware - Khalil Estell - CppCon 2024\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Khalil Estell's CppCon 2024 talk argues C++ exceptions can reduce binary size in firmware. He digs into how exceptions work and how to shrink their code footprint on embedded platforms.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to as the in-depth talk on exception mechanics and code size for embedded C++.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"c++ exceptions in embedded\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\",\"Software performance & optimization\"],\"tags\":[\"c++\",\"exceptions\",\"firmware\",\"binary size\",\"embedded\"],\"quote\":\"In particular, his 2019 presentation [2] describes different error handling techniques\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"bbyr7jZOllI\",\"title\":\"The Thorium Molten-Salt Reactor: Why Didn't This Happen (and why is now the right time?)\",\"channel\":\"Google TechTalks\",\"summary\":\"Kirk Sorensen's Google Tech Talk on thorium molten-salt reactors (LFTR). He covers why the technology was shelved after early development and argues that now is the right time to revisit it.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the go-to pitch for liquid fluoride thorium reactors whenever nuclear power comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"thorium molten-salt reactors\",\"categories\":[\"Nuclear power & fusion\"],\"tags\":[\"thorium\",\"lftr\",\"nuclear energy\",\"molten salt\",\"kirk sorensen\"],\"quote\":\"Kirk Sorensen has been a major advocate of molten-salt reactors and gave a great Google tech talk on the subject.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"bx3KuE7UjGA\",\"title\":\"MINIX 3: a Modular, Self-Healing POSIX-compatible Operating System\",\"channel\":\"FOSDEM\",\"summary\":\"Andrew Tanenbaum's FOSDEM 2010 talk on MINIX 3, a microkernel OS built for high reliability and security. It shows how the system detects and recovers from device driver crashes, and addresses microkernel performance concerns.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as an accessible explanation of microkernel isolation and fault-tolerant design, and as Tanenbaum's answer to performance objections.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"microkernel operating systems\",\"categories\":[\"Operating systems & kernels\",\"Distributed systems\"],\"tags\":[\"minix 3\",\"microkernel\",\"fault tolerance\",\"tanenbaum\",\"self-healing\"],\"quote\":\"it's a very interesting but accessible talk for those interested in lower-level systems and fault-tolerant architectures.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"cUzklzVXJwo\",\"title\":\"How Imaginary Numbers Were Invented\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium tells the history of how imaginary numbers arose from the quest for a general solution to the cubic equation, including the duels and secrecy among Renaissance mathematicians. It argues math had to give up the requirement that it mirror reality.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a well-visualized, story-driven history of complex numbers, suitable for beginners; some quibble with its framing of algebra and geometry.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of imaginary numbers\",\"categories\":[\"Pure mathematics\",\"Recreational & visual math\"],\"tags\":[\"complex numbers\",\"cubic equation\",\"math history\",\"algebra\",\"veritasium\"],\"quote\":\"I also highly recommend Veritasium's video[1] as an excellent explainer as well, especially regarding the roots of imaginary/complex numbers in geometry.\",\"recommenders\":7,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"cj02_UeUnGQ\",\"title\":\"\\\"A Political History of X\\\" - Keith Packard (LCA 2020)\",\"channel\":\"linux.conf.au\",\"summary\":\"Keith Packard traces the 35-year history of the X Window System at linux.conf.au 2020. He uses X to examine licensing, corporate interests and community dynamics in free software, including Unix workstation vendors and Stallman's influence.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as the authoritative, firsthand account of how X came to be and why it isn't GPL.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"x window system history\",\"categories\":[\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\",\"Hacker culture & free software\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"x11\",\"free software\",\"licensing\",\"unix history\",\"keith packard\"],\"quote\":\"I invite anyone interested by the subject to watch Keith Packard's amazing presentation on this at LCA 2020\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"dpw9EHDh2bM\",\"title\":\"React Today and Tomorrow and  90% Cleaner React With Hooks\",\"channel\":\"React Conf\",\"summary\":\"The first three talks from React Conf 2018, by Sophie Alpert, Dan Abramov and Ryan Florence. They introduce React Hooks and explain the problems with classes that hooks solve.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the canonical introduction to hooks, given by the people who built them, and HN uses it to answer questions about why hooks exist.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"react hooks\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\"],\"tags\":[\"react\",\"hooks\",\"javascript\",\"frontend\",\"react conf\"],\"quote\":\"It's absolutely worth the watch, even if you're somewhat familiar with hooks.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"eU5Dn-WaElI\",\"title\":\"Making of \\\"The Prodigy - Smack My Bitch Up\\\" in Ableton by Jim Pavloff\",\"channel\":\"Jim Pavloff\",\"summary\":\"Jim Pavloff rebuilds the Prodigy track Smack My Bitch Up in Ableton, identifying the samples used and recreating the track by hand.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a fun, in-depth look at how sampling and music production work, and as proof that skilled humans can reconstruct tracks from scratch.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"music production\",\"categories\":[\"Music theory, synthesis & production\"],\"tags\":[\"ableton\",\"sampling\",\"the prodigy\",\"remake\",\"electronic music\"],\"quote\":\"I'm amazed at the level of musical knowledge needed to do this.\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"elvOZm0d4H0\",\"title\":\"Infinity is bigger than you think - Numberphile\",\"channel\":\"Numberphile\",\"summary\":\"Numberphile's James Grime explains Cantor's result that some infinities are bigger than others, including why the reals outnumber the integers. It is a short, accessible introduction to countable and uncountable infinity.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people recommend it as a clear introduction to different sizes of infinity that non-mathematicians can follow.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"infinity and set theory\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Logic & formal methods\"],\"tags\":[\"cantor\",\"uncountable infinity\",\"diagonal argument\",\"numberphile\",\"mathematics\"],\"quote\":\"Numberphile did a pretty good introductory video to this if you want to learn more\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"ez773teNFYA\",\"title\":\"Stephen Wolfram: Cellular Automata, Computation, and Physics | Lex Fridman Podcast #89\",\"channel\":\"Lex Fridman\",\"summary\":\"Lex Fridman interviews Stephen Wolfram for about three hours on cellular automata, computation, the Wolfram Physics Project and his career with Mathematica and Wolfram Alpha.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as an accessible long-form introduction to Wolfram's ideas and a window into his passion, though some are critical of his claims.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"cellular automata and computation\",\"categories\":[\"Computability & complexity theory\",\"Fundamental physics\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"stephen wolfram\",\"cellular automata\",\"wolfram physics project\",\"computation\",\"mathematica\"],\"quote\":\"accessible intro\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"ezVk1ahRF78\",\"title\":\"Religions and babies | Hans Rosling\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"Hans Rosling's TEDxSummit talk asks whether some religions have higher birth rates and how that affects population growth. He charts data across time and religions and reaches a surprising conclusion about fertility.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended whenever population growth comes up, as a data-driven correction to common assumptions.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"global population growth\",\"categories\":[\"Social issues & culture\",\"Data visualization & information design\",\"Religion, belief & skepticism\"],\"tags\":[\"hans rosling\",\"fertility rates\",\"demographics\",\"religion\",\"ted talk\"],\"quote\":\"Then watch this video to understand the real trends in population growth.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"f0gMdGrVteI\",\"title\":\"The Extreme Physics Pushing Moore’s Law to the Next Level\",\"channel\":\"Seeker\",\"summary\":\"Seeker's Focal Point short documentary on ASML's extreme ultraviolet lithography machines, which make the most advanced chips. It shows how plasma-generated 13.5 nm light is used to push Moore's Law forward.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it in semiconductor threads as an accessible overview of why EUV lithography is so hard.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"euv lithography\",\"categories\":[\"Semiconductors & chip manufacturing\"],\"tags\":[\"asml\",\"semiconductors\",\"moore's law\",\"chip manufacturing\",\"photolithography\"],\"quote\":\"Seeker released a very insightful feature about the ASML Extreme UV / soft X-ray (13.5 nanometers) photolithography system\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"ftcIcn8AmSY\",\"title\":\"Four Solutions to a Trivial Problem - Guy Steele Jr.\",\"channel\":\"Google TechTalks\",\"summary\":\"Guy Steele presents a small geometric problem and compares four computational approaches: a classic sequential solution and three parallel-friendly ones. Each is illustrated pictorially and with working code, weighing total work against time to solution.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a clear lesson that algebraic properties like associativity unlock parallelism, applicable in any language.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"parallel algorithms\",\"categories\":[\"Algorithms & data structures\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"parallelism\",\"associativity\",\"functional programming\",\"guy steele\",\"algorithms\"],\"quote\":\"watch Guy Steele's talks [1][2][3] if you are interested in data parallelism!\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"gAk7aX5hksU\",\"title\":\"You Need To Listen To This Leading COVID-19 Expert From South Korea | STAY CURIOUS #15\",\"channel\":\"Asian Boss\",\"summary\":\"Asian Boss interviews Professor Kim Woo-joo of Korea University Guro Hospital about COVID-19 in early 2020. He covers masks, testing, aerosol spread, and South Korea's response.\",\"why_hn\":\"In early pandemic threads, HN commenters pointed to it as an uninterrupted, practical expert view from Korea on masks, testing and airborne spread.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"covid-19 response\",\"categories\":[\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"covid-19\",\"south korea\",\"masks\",\"testing\",\"aerosol transmission\",\"epidemiology\"],\"quote\":\"The videos are excellent because the doctors are not interrupted with questions.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"gV5obrYaogU\",\"title\":\"SYMBOLICS S-PACKAGES 3D GRAPHICS AND ANIMATION DEMO\",\"channel\":\"Red Martian\",\"summary\":\"A demo of the Symbolics Graphics Division's S-Packages, a Lisp-based 3D color graphics and animation system from the 1980s. It was later used in developing for the Nintendo 64.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as a startling example of how advanced Lisp machine interactive graphics were, often in Lisp and REPL-driven development discussions.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"lisp machine graphics\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Computer graphics & rendering\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"symbolics\",\"lisp\",\"3d animation\",\"retrocomputing\",\"s-packages\"],\"quote\":\"I really enjoyed the Symbolics graphics and animations demo[0] linked in the article.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"gWVmPtr9O0g\",\"title\":\"TiTAN - Overdrive 2 (RGB 50fps) - SEGA Mega Drive - Revision 2017 (1st Place)\",\"channel\":\"Oerg866\",\"summary\":\"Overdrive 2 by TiTAN, a Sega Mega Drive demo that won first place in the Oldskool competition at Revision 2017. It shows effects and 3D graphics on the stock console.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN plugs it as a favorite example of what skilled demoscene coders can pull out of 30-year-old hardware.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"demoscene\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"Game history & retro gaming\"],\"tags\":[\"sega mega drive\",\"demoscene\",\"retro hardware\",\"revision 2017\",\"3d graphics\"],\"quote\":\"Overdrive 2 is my favorite example\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"hC3VTgIPoGU\",\"title\":\"\\\"CHASING ICE\\\" captures largest glacier calving ever filmed - OFFICIAL VIDEO\",\"channel\":\"Exposure Labs\",\"summary\":\"Footage from the Chasing Ice project of the largest glacier calving event ever filmed, at Greenland's Ilulissat Glacier in 2008. A 75-minute event is condensed to a few minutes of cameras watching ice collapse.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended whenever glaciers come up, as a stunning look at the scale of calving.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"glacier calving\",\"categories\":[\"Climate science & climate change\",\"Geology & earth science\"],\"tags\":[\"glaciers\",\"greenland\",\"climate\",\"chasing ice\",\"time-lapse\"],\"quote\":\"If you have never seen a glacier calve before, check out these videos\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"hzyFZcuHmeI\",\"title\":\"The Milk-Industrial Complex: Why You Don't Need to Drink Milk\",\"channel\":\"Healthcare Triage\",\"summary\":\"A short Healthcare Triage episode by Aaron Carroll arguing that milk gets an undeserved pass in efforts to improve diets. It examines claims about calcium and bone strength and concludes you don't need to drink milk.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters link it as a concise evidence-based rebuttal to common beliefs about milk's health benefits.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"nutrition and dairy\",\"categories\":[\"Nutrition & diet science\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"milk\",\"nutrition\",\"calcium\",\"healthcare triage\",\"diet\"],\"quote\":\"Here's a great video on the topic from one of the co-authors of this article\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"iDulhoQ2pro\",\"title\":\"Attention Is All You Need\",\"channel\":\"Yannic Kilcher\",\"summary\":\"Yannic Kilcher walks through the Attention Is All You Need paper, which introduced the Transformer, a sequence model based solely on attention without recurrence or convolutions. He reviews the architecture and the paper's translation results.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an accessible, clear walkthrough of the transformer paper for people with some AI background.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"transformer architecture\",\"categories\":[\"LLMs & generative AI\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\"],\"tags\":[\"transformers\",\"attention\",\"paper review\",\"deep learning\",\"nlp\",\"yannic kilcher\"],\"quote\":\"The Yannic kilcher review is quite good.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"j5v8D-alAKE\",\"title\":\"This is why we can't have nice things\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium explores planned obsolescence, from the Phoebus lightbulb cartel to printers and phones. It explains how companies deliberately shortened product lifetimes and why products aren't better.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters link it as the engaging go-to explainer on planned obsolescence and the lightbulb cartel.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"planned obsolescence\",\"categories\":[\"Repair, teardowns & right to repair\",\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\",\"Tech industry & business models\"],\"tags\":[\"phoebus cartel\",\"lightbulbs\",\"obsolescence\",\"consumer products\",\"history\"],\"quote\":\"Veritasium made a video that reviews the history and presents the topic in an engaging way.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"jA1C9VFqJKo\",\"title\":\"Flying Dream\",\"channel\":\"Gary Garrett\",\"summary\":\"Gary Garrett's stop-motion animation of his song Flying Dream, with notes arranged on a lattice of fifths and major thirds. It shows how harmony looks in just intonation, inspired by Harmonic Experience.\",\"why_hn\":\"Shared as a beautiful example of lattice-based harmony visualization.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"harmony visualization\",\"categories\":[\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Data visualization & information design\"],\"tags\":[\"just intonation\",\"tonal lattice\",\"music theory\",\"animation\",\"harmonic experience\"],\"quote\":\"this beautiful song using it\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"kH5APw_SLUU\",\"title\":\"Build a Do-It-Yourself Air Purifier for About \$40\",\"channel\":\"Michigan Medicine\",\"summary\":\"A University of Michigan Medicine demonstration of building a cheap DIY air purifier from a box fan and a HEPA or high-MERV filter using hardware-store parts. It explains why this helps against dust, smoke and viruses and ties it to local air quality readings.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people link it during wildfire smoke and air quality threads as a practical, cheap fix, though a few doubt how rigorously its effectiveness was shown.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"diy air filtration\",\"categories\":[\"Home, DIY & practical skills\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"air purifier\",\"hepa filter\",\"box fan\",\"wildfire smoke\",\"air quality\"],\"quote\":\"Here's a video from the University of Michigan showing just how effective these cheap DIY air filters can be\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"kd2KEHvK-q8\",\"title\":\"The Vanishing of Flight 370\",\"channel\":\"LEMMiNO\",\"summary\":\"LEMMiNO's 25-minute documentary on the 2014 disappearance of Malaysia Airlines Flight 370. It walks through the official investigation's findings and the leading theories, including satellite data and hydrophone recordings.\",\"why_hn\":\"Regularly shared as the best well-made overview of the MH370 mystery, in threads on the search and ocean mapping.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"mh370 disappearance\",\"categories\":[\"Aviation & aerospace engineering\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"mh370\",\"aviation mystery\",\"investigation\",\"satellite data\",\"hydrophones\"],\"quote\":\"There is a really well put together video[1] by a YouTuber, who goes by the name of Lemmino\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"kfg_e6YG37U\",\"title\":\"Deskbound | Kelly Starrett | Talks at Google\",\"channel\":\"Talks at Google\",\"summary\":\"Kelly Starrett's Talks at Google presentation on staying healthy while desk bound, mixing anatomy and practical mobility advice. It shows how to set up a desk and sit and move to avoid pain.\",\"why_hn\":\"Often the first thing suggested to HN programmers asking about posture and back pain.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"desk posture and mobility\",\"categories\":[\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\",\"UX & UI design\"],\"tags\":[\"posture\",\"ergonomics\",\"mobility\",\"back pain\",\"standing desk\"],\"quote\":\"my first recommended steps for you would be: 1. Watch the \\\"Deskbound\\\" Google Talk by Kelly Starett\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"kh88fVP2FWQ\",\"title\":\"The US Has More 