ary_topic\":\"tying shoelaces\",\"categories\":[\"Home, DIY & practical skills\",\"Ideas & learning (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"shoelace knot\",\"square knot\",\"ted\",\"life hack\"],\"quote\":\"You are probably tying a granny knot instead of a square knot.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"zUyH3XhpLTo\",\"title\":\"How to Understand What Black Holes Look Like\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium explains what a black hole should look like, using J.P. Luminet's 1979 work and a cardboard 3D model to show how light bends around it. It was made just as the first black hole image was released.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended for correctly predicting the features of the real black hole image and clearly explaining the lensing physics behind it.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"black hole imaging\",\"categories\":[\"Cosmology & astrophysics\",\"Fundamental physics\"],\"tags\":[\"black holes\",\"gravitational lensing\",\"accretion disk\",\"event horizon telescope\",\"m87\"],\"quote\":\"Good video that correctly predicted the image and describes why it looks the way it does\",\"recommenders\":8,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"zi0rHwfiX1Q\",\"title\":\"John Hughes - Testing the Hard Stuff and Staying Sane\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"John Hughes, co-creator of QuickCheck, talks about property-based testing of hard systems. He shows production examples where it found very low-level bugs, including issues in the CAN bus standard.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the best introduction to property-based testing and cited for its examples of bugs found in the CAN standard.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"property-based testing\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"quickcheck\",\"property-based testing\",\"generative testing\",\"can bus\",\"john hughes\"],\"quote\":\"John Hughes (not the filmmaker) gives a great talk about it\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"zyVTxGpEO30\",\"title\":\"Practical Creativity\",\"channel\":\"GDC Festival of Gaming\",\"summary\":\"Raph Koster's 2014 GDC Next talk on what science says about creativity, with practical steps game designers and developers can use to be more creative. It frames creativity as applying existing patterns in new contexts.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended even to people outside game development as a useful, practical perspective on how creativity works.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"creativity for game designers\",\"categories\":[\"Game design\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"raph koster\",\"creativity\",\"game design\",\"gdc\",\"design process\"],\"quote\":\"I recommend his 2014 GDC talk, \\\"Practical Creativity\\\" [1], even to developers who are not interested in game development.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.5},{\"vid\":\"-I_jE0l7sYQ\",\"title\":\"Joe Armstrong - Keynote: The Forgotten Ideas in Computer Science - Code BEAM SF 2018\",\"channel\":\"Code Sync\",\"summary\":\"Erlang co-creator Joe Armstrong's Code BEAM SF 2018 keynote revisits good ideas from the history of computing that were dropped, and asks which should be revived. It also touches on Erlang and Elixir.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people recommend it as a reading list of historical CS ideas worth knowing, and as a favorite among Armstrong's talks, especially after his death.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of computing ideas\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Functional programming\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"joe armstrong\",\"erlang\",\"forgotten ideas\",\"programming history\",\"keynote\"],\"quote\":\"Ted has a knack for reframing things in ways that make the arbitrariness of certain historical decisions clear\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"-Ln-8QM8KhQ\",\"title\":\"How is this Website so fast!?\",\