dard example of government-built housing keeping rents down.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"social housing\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Labor, work & inequality\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"vienna\",\"affordable housing\",\"public housing\",\"rents\",\"urban policy\"],\"quote\":\"if you supply at least some public housing, it can have an outsized ability to keep rents down\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"449j7oKQVkc\",\"title\":\"Ron Pressler - Pull Push: Please stop polluting our imperative languages with pure concepts-Curry On\",\"channel\":\"Curry On!\",\"summary\":\"Ron Pressler's Curry On 2015 talk arguing that imperative languages should not be polluted with pure functional concepts like monads. He favors simple blocking code over non-blocking styles.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as the case for blocking code and virtual threads over monads and async/await.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"imperative vs functional programming\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Functional programming\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"monads\",\"async await\",\"virtual threads\",\"blocking code\",\"language design\"],\"quote\":\"one of my recent favorite videos generally on this topic has been from Curry On conference\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"4RSht_aV7AU\",\"title\":\"Keynote: Begin...End?\",\"channel\":\"David Beazley\",\"summary\":\"David Beazley's PyData Global 2021 keynote, an oral history of early scientific Python. He tells how Python ended up running on a Connection Machine supercomputer at Los Alamos.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in threads about Python's rise as a first-hand, entertaining account of why it won in scientific computing.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of scientific python\",\"categories\":[\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\",\"History of computing\",\"Data science & statistics\"],\"tags\":[\"python\",\"los alamos\",\"connection machine\",\"scientific computing\",\"history\"],\"quote\":\"I recommend one of the recent videos by Dave Beazly\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"4hZxo96x48A\",\"title\":\"Music Software & Interface Design: MuseScore\",\"channel\":\"Tantacrul\",\"summary\":\"Tantacrul gives a detailed UX and UI design review of the MuseScore 3 notation software and compares it with Sibelius. It examines visual appeal, navigation and notation quality.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as a funny, informative teardown of bad interface design, and for the story that MuseScore hired him as head of design afterward.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ui design critique\",\"categories\":[\"UX & UI design\",\"Music theory, synthesis & production\"],\"tags\":[\"musescore\",\"ux design\",\"music notation\",\"sibelius\",\"interface review\"],\"quote\":\"I find it very entertaining yet still informative\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"4oAB83Z1ydE\",\"title\":\"Think Different\",\"channel\":\"英倫米高 Michael in England\",\"summary\":\"Apple's classic 'Think Different' commercial, a one-minute spot celebrating creative misfits and innovators. This upload is from 2006.\",\"why_hn\":\"Shared as an emotionally powerful example of great advertising, especially after Steve Jobs died.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"apple advertising\",\"categories\":[\"Tech company stories & documentaries\",\"Product, marketing & sales\"],\"tags\":[\"think different\",\"apple\",\"advertising\",\"steve jobs\",\"branding\"],\"quote\":\"I must admit that after watching it just now I teared up a bit.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"5HbYScltf1c\",\"title\":\"Louis C.K. Hates Cell Phones | CONAN on TBS\",\"channel\":\"Team Coco\",\"summary\":\"Louis C.K. on Conan explains why he won't give his kids cell phones. He argues that phones keep people from sitting with sadness and being alone with their thoughts, and that kids lose empathy when they cannot see another person's reaction.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters bring it up in threads on phones, boredom, attention and children's screen use as a funny but resonant take.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"smartphones and attention\",\"categories\":[\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\",\"Futurism & technology and society\"],\"tags\":[\"smartphones\",\"boredom\",\"parenting\",\"stand-up\",\"loneliness\"],\"quote\":\"arrived at the same conclusion much more concisely nearly a decade ago\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"5Tek9h3a5wQ\",\"title\":\"James Baldwin vs William F Buckley: A legendary debate from 1965\",\"channel\":\"Aeon Video\",\"summary\":\"The 1965 Cambridge Union debate between James Baldwin and William F. Buckley on race, justice and history in America. Baldwin argues that the American dream came at the expense of Black Americans.\",\"why_hn\":\"Held up as a classic debate with a clear winner and Baldwin's powerful oratory, still relevant today.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"race and american history debate\",\"categories\":[\"Social issues & culture\",\"World history\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\"],\"tags\":[\"james baldwin\",\"william buckley\",\"debate\",\"civil rights\",\"rhetoric\"],\"quote\":\"he mesmerises the audience by telling them what it's like to grow up black in America not even knowing you're black\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"5zkC9IMQmYA\",\"title\":\"High Altitude Leadership | Chris Warner | Talks at Google\",\"channel\":\"Talks at Google\",\"summary\":\"Climber Chris Warner talks at Google about his book on high altitude leadership and the 2007 K2 expedition, where eighteen climbers summited and two died.\",\"why_hn\":\"Strongly recommended as a gripping hour on K2 and on leadership and summit fever in extreme conditions.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"mountaineering leadership\",\"categories\":[\"Management, leadership & organizations\",\"Sports & athletics\"],\"tags\":[\"k2\",\"leadership\",\"mountaineering\",\"risk\",\"everest\"],\"quote\":\"I highly, highly recommend listening to Chris Warner's talk on summitting K2\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"6AeWu1fZ7bY\",\"title\":\"Benno Rice: The Tragedy of systemd -- BSDCan 2018\",\"channel\":\"BSDCan\",\"summary\":\"Benno Rice's BSDCan 2018 talk on why systemd exists and what it got right and wrong. It traces the history of Unix init and why Linux distributions adopted it, while asking what the BSDs should learn.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a balanced, eloquent take on the systemd debate that even skeptics find persuasive.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"systemd and init systems\",\"categories\":[\"Operating systems & kernels\",\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\"],\"tags\":[\"systemd\",\"bsd\",\"init scripts\",\"unix history\",\"linux distributions\"],\"quote\":\"One of the better videos on this subject\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"6BhV-o77RqQ\",\"title\":\"Mosquito trap DIY 8,000 mosquito kill reduce ZIKA DENGUE MALARIA MaxxAir Fan CO2\",\"channel\":\"GREENPOWERSCIENCE\",\"summary\":\"A DIY demonstration of a fan-and-screen mosquito trap that uses carbonated water as a CO2 attractant. The creator reports catching about 8,000 mosquitoes in two nights.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point people to it as a cheap, simple, effective way to kill mosquitoes, and some report trying it successfully.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"mosquito control\",\"categories\":[\"Home, DIY & practical skills\",\"Animals & nature\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\"],\"tags\":[\"mosquito trap\",\"diy\",\"fan trap\",\"co2 attractant\",\"pest control\"],\"quote\":\"attach a screen (or net) to a high powered fan and catch/kill insane amount of mosquitoes.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"7CgXvIuZR40\",\"title\":\"TasBlock at AGDQ 2017: TASBot\",\"channel\":\"TASVideosChannel\",\"summary\":\"A recording of the TASBot block at Awesome Games Done Quick 2017, where a robot plays games via tool-assisted speedruns, including Super Mario 64 and Portal on an SNES via arbitrary code execution, and ending with a Twitch Plays segment. It showcases console exploits and creative hardware hacking.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters rate it a great GDQ watch and point to its feats, such as streaming video and Skype through controller ports, as inspiring hacks.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"tool-assisted speedruns\",\"categories\":[\"Speedrunning, glitches & game hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"tasbot\",\"gdq\",\"arbitrary code execution\",\"snes\",\"console hacking\"],\"quote\":\"TASBot which always makes a great GDQ watch\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"7HKbjYqqPPQ\",\"title\":\"2017 ACM PPoPP Keynote: It's Time for a New Old Language\",\"channel\":\"Guy Steele\",\"summary\":\"Guy Steele's PPoPP 2017 keynote surveying the history of computer science metanotation, including BNF, inference rules, and overline notation. He argues the field's notations are inconsistent and fragmented.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as an excellent talk on notation problems, useful when reading papers or designing a new metalanguage.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming language notation\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"Logic & formal methods\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"metanotation\",\"bnf\",\"inference rules\",\"guy steele\",\"pl theory\"],\"quote\":\"an excellent talk by Guy Steele that goes over issue of notation and balkanization\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"7OpM_zKGE4o\",\"title\":\"The Problem with Solar Energy in Africa\",\"channel\":\"Real Engineering\",\"summary\":\"Real Engineering looks at why Africa's abundant solar potential is hard to turn into usable power, especially for Europe. It covers the transmission, infrastructure and economic obstacles, and the pivot from concentrated solar to photovoltaics.\",\"why_hn\":\"Linked whenever commenters ask whether Sahara solar could power Europe, as a clear explanation of why it is impractical.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"solar energy transmission\",\"categories\":[\"Renewables, batteries & storage\",\"Power grid, heating & buildings\"],\"tags\":[\"solar power\",\"sahara\",\"power transmission\",\"renewables\",\"europe energy\"],\"quote\":\"A very good video from Real Engineering explains that no, not really\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"816VLQNdPMM\",\"title\":\"Why Adults Can't Develop Perfect Pitch\",\"channel\":\"Rick Beato\",\"summary\":\"Rick Beato argues that adults cannot develop true perfect pitch, which he ties to critical-period brain plasticity and language acquisition. He says it can only be developed in young children, and discusses the research behind this.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as the standard explanation of why perfect pitch is a childhood window, in threads about absolute pitch training.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"perfect pitch\",\"categories\":[\"Neuroscience & the brain\",\"Music theory, synthesis & production\"],\"tags\":[\"absolute pitch\",\"ear training\",\"music cognition\",\"critical period\",\"rick beato\"],\"quote\":\"This video convinced me that adults cannot develop or train perfect pitch.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"85ZCTuekjGA\",\"title\":\"Building a 6800 CPU on an FPGA with nMigen (part 1)\",\"channel\":\"Robert Baruch\",\"summary\":\"The first part of Robert Baruch's series on building a 6800 CPU on an FPGA with nMigen, a Python-based hardware description language. It introduces the HDL while implementing a simpler 6800 core.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends the series as a practical introduction to nMigen and formal verification for CPU design.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"fpga cpu design\",\"categories\":[\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Semiconductors & chip manufacturing\",\"Embedded systems & DIY electronics\"],\"tags\":[\"fpga\",\"nmigen\",\"6800\",\"hdl\",\"formal verification\"],\"quote\":\"I can only recommend these Youtube series, where nMigen is used to build a full 6800 CPU\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"86Krv3gE-c4\",\"title\":\"Giant Robot Project, Part 1\",\"channel\":\"Jaimie Stuff\",\"summary\":\"The first installment of a hobbyist's video diary documenting his build of a giant walking spider-like vehicle. The footage is casual and self-shot, following the early stages of the project.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as the start of a gripping, wonder-inducing build saga by a prolific maker of ambitious mechanical projects.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"diy giant robot building\",\"categories\":[\"3D printing, CNC & maker projects\",\"Robotics\",\"Mechanical & precision engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"giant robot\",\"walking vehicle\",\"maker\",\"mechanical engineering\",\"diy\"],\"quote\":\"I'm afraid I've wrecked what's left of the work day as I follow the saga.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"8ORLN_KwAgs\",\"title\":\"How the Quantum Eraser Rewrites the Past | Space Time | PBS Digital Studios\",\"channel\":\"PBS Space Time\",\"summary\":\"PBS Space Time explains the quantum eraser experiment, in which erasing which-path information appears to reverse causality in a double-slit setup. It discusses what this means for quantum mechanics.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a quick introduction to the quantum eraser and delayed choice weirdness, though one commenter notes later corrections to its framing.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"quantum eraser experiment\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum physics\"],\"tags\":[\"double slit\",\"delayed choice\",\"causality\",\"quantum mechanics\",\"pbs space time\"],\"quote\":\"a quick video from space time for those being first introduced\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"9-IWMbJXoLM\",\"title\":\"\\\"What UNIX Cost Us\\\" - Benno Rice (LCA 2020)\",\"channel\":\"linux.conf.au\",\"summary\":\"Benno Rice's linux.conf.au 2020 talk asks whether UNIX was the best design we could have had. He looks at what alternatives existed, what was gained and lost, and what could be re-learned, including the limits of treating everything as a file.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an insightful and entertaining critique of UNIX design choices such as file descriptors and 'everything is a file'.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"unix design history\",\"categories\":[\"Operating systems & kernels\",\"Linux & open-source ecosystems\"],\"tags\":[\"unix\",\"file descriptors\",\"operating systems\",\"freebsd\",\"everything is a file\"],\"quote\":\"Benno Rice is an insightful, energetic, witty, and just plain enjoyable tech speaker.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"90NsjKvz9Ns\",\"title\":\"Mark DiMarco: User Interface Algorithms [JSConf2014]\",\"channel\":\"JSConf\",\"summary\":\"A JSConf 2014 talk visually breaking down algorithms behind everyday UI patterns, such as dropdown menus that vanish when the mouse drifts and picking the nearest target to the cursor. It shows how these are implemented in JavaScript.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it whenever menu hover problems or nearest-to-pointer selection come up, and as proof algorithms matter in front-end work.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"user interface algorithms\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"UX & UI design\",\"Algorithms & data structures\"],\"tags\":[\"ui algorithms\",\"dropdown menus\",\"mouse interaction\",\"javascript\",\"front-end\"],\"quote\":\"Algorithms do play an important role as you can see in this great presentation\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"99dKzubvpKE\",\"title\":\"Refterm v2 - Resource usage, binary splat, glyph sizing, and more\",\"channel\":\"Molly Rocket\",\"summary\":\"Casey Muratori follows up his refterm proof-of-concept, a GPU-based terminal renderer, with a second part covering memory usage, glyph sizing and correct Unicode and VT handling. It is built to show that a terminal can be fast and correct without heroic effort.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as proof that a small, straightforward renderer can beat Windows Terminal on speed and memory, whenever software slowness comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"terminal rendering performance\",\"categories\":[\"Software performance & optimization\",\"Computer graphics & rendering\",\"Graphic design & typography\"],\"tags\":[\"refterm\",\"gpu rendering\",\"windows terminal\",\"unicode\",\"software performance\"],\"quote\":\"Casey Muratori produced a highly correct Unicode renderer with correct VT code support. Watch the video\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"9F9ahZQ7oP0\",\"title\":\"RetroAhoy: The Secret of Monkey Island\",\"channel\":\"Ahoy\",\"summary\":\"A roughly 75-minute video essay from Ahoy's RetroAhoy series on the making and history of The Secret of Monkey Island. It covers the small LucasArts team, the game's development and its sequel.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a well-made documentary-style history that gives context around the game and inspires replays.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"monkey island game history\",\"categories\":[\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Gaming culture & analysis\"],\"tags\":[\"monkey island\",\"lucasarts\",\"adventure games\",\"retro gaming\",\"game development history\"],\"quote\":\"Do yourself a favor and watch this wonderful video essay historique on The Monkey Island series from Ahoy\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"9LfmrkyP81M\",\"title\":\"RailsConf 2014 - Keynote: Writing Software by David Heinemeier Hansson\",\"channel\":\"Confreaks\",\"summary\":\"David Heinemeier Hansson's RailsConf 2014 keynote argues that programming is closer to writing than to science, with clear code produced by iterating through drafts. It prioritizes clarity and craft over technical superiority.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it in discussions of programming as a creative craft and of how to write clear software.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming as writing\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Python, Ruby & scripting languages\"],\"tags\":[\"dhh\",\"software craft\",\"ruby on rails\",\"code clarity\",\"railsconf\"],\"quote\":\"I really like this[1] talk from DHH on the subject\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"9W74aeuqsiU\",\"title\":\"PFAS: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO)\",\"channel\":\"LastWeekTonight\",\"summary\":\"John Oliver's Last Week Tonight segment on PFAS, the 'forever chemicals' linked to health problems. It covers their widespread use in products like nonstick coatings and repellent fabrics and the limits of regulation.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a well-researched, accessible overview of PFAS in PFAS-related threads.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"pfas chemicals\",\"categories\":[\"Environment, agriculture & food systems\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Chemistry\"],\"tags\":[\"pfas\",\"forever chemicals\",\"john oliver\",\"regulation\",\"environment\"],\"quote\":\"he does his homework\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"9crggox5rbc\",\"title\":\"Entanglement and Complexity: Gravity and Quantum Mechanics\",\"channel\":\"Stanford Institute for Theoretical Physics\",\"summary\":\"Leonard Susskind lectures at Stanford on how gravity and quantum information theory have merged into a new way of thinking about physics. He covers entanglement and complexity, with applications from black holes to strange metals and the foundations of quantum mechanics.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters recommend it as a clear, characteristically engaging Susskind lecture for people trying to grasp the entanglement and gravity connection.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"quantum gravity and entanglement\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum physics\",\"Cosmology & astrophysics\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"susskind\",\"black holes\",\"wormholes\",\"quantum information\",\"complexity\"],\"quote\":\"Mr.Susskind has a Stanford lecture on this topic detailing the physics in his own unique & awesome way\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"AGCmPLWZKd8\",\"title\":\"36C3 -  BahnMining - Pünktlichkeit ist eine Zier - english translation\",\"channel\":\"media.ccc.de\",\"summary\":\"David Kriesel's 36C3 talk, in an English translation, analyzes a year of scraped Deutsche Bahn data covering every stop of every long-distance train in Germany. It explores delays and how punctuality is actually measured.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN brings it up in any thread about German or European rail as a data-driven, entertaining analysis of why trains are late.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"german railway punctuality\",\"categories\":[\"Data science & statistics\",\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Hacker culture & free software\"],\"tags\":[\"deutsche bahn\",\"data analysis\",\"chaos communication congress\",\"trains\",\"scraping\"],\"quote\":\"There was this really great talk on the Chaos Communication Congress some time back\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"ARYP83yNAWk\",\"title\":\"De-fragmenting C++: Making Exceptions and RTTI More Affordable and Usable - Herb Sutter  CppCon 2019\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Herb Sutter's CppCon 2019 talk on the split in the C++ community over exceptions and RTTI. He proposes making exceptions cheaper and more usable, including a zero-overhead, Result-like design.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to whenever C++ exception overhead, bans on exceptions or error-handling alternatives come up.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"c++ exceptions\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Programming language design\"],\"tags\":[\"c++\",\"exceptions\",\"rtti\",\"herb sutter\",\"error handling\",\"zero-overhead\"],\"quote\":\"I can really recommend this talk\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"AZR0UKxNPh8\",\"title\":\"Energy From Thorium: A Nuclear Waste Burning Liquid Salt Thorium Reactor\",\"channel\":\"Google TechTalks\",\"summary\":\"Kirk Sorensen's Google Tech Talk on liquid-fluoride thorium reactors, a design that could burn nuclear waste and use thorium as fuel. It draws on Oak Ridge National Laboratory work from the 1950s and 60s.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN used it as the go-to introduction to LFTR and thorium reactor technology in energy threads.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"thorium molten salt reactors\",\"categories\":[\"Nuclear power & fusion\",\"Renewables, batteries & storage\"],\"tags\":[\"thorium\",\"lftr\",\"nuclear energy\",\"molten salt\",\"kirk sorensen\"],\"quote\":\"If you don't know about the LFTR, check out this talk by Kirk Sorensen\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"Ac7G7xOG2Ag\",\"title\":\"\\\"Turbo Encabulator\\\" the Original\",\"channel\":\"Dave Rondot\",\"summary\":\"The earliest filmed version of the Turbo Encabulator technical-jargon parody, shot in the late 1970s with narrator Bud Haggert. It deadpans a stream of meaningless engineering language.\",\"why_hn\":\"Used as the canonical reference for the jargon-parody trope, and as a joke whenever technobabble appears.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"technical jargon parody\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Mechanical & precision engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"turbo encabulator\",\"parody\",\"technobabble\",\"bud haggert\",\"engineering humor\"],\"quote\":\"Doug Zongker's presentation similarly instructs people how to make better presentations to peers by demonstrating what not to do\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"Av0PQDVTP4A\",\"title\":\"Literate Programming in the Large\",\"channel\":\"Next Day Video\",\"summary\":\"Timothy Daly, an original author of the Axiom computer algebra system, talks about turning a large Common Lisp codebase into a literate program. He argues code should be written as readable manuals, and the talk was given at Write the Docs 2013.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it in literate programming threads as a rare look at applying the idea to a huge, complex codebase, though some find it meandering.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"literate programming\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Functional programming\"],\"tags\":[\"literate programming\",\"axiom\",\"common lisp\",\"documentation\",\"legacy code\"],\"quote\":\"well worth watching: it talks about real, huge comolex systems, and how lp can help bring them back under control\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"B2482h_TNwg\",\"title\":\"The \$200M Machine that Prints Microchips:  The EUV Photolithography System\",\"channel\":\"Branch Education\",\"summary\":\"Branch Education's animated deep dive into ASML's EUV photolithography machine and how it prints nanometer-scale transistors onto chips. It covers the system's components and the process of chip manufacturing.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN sends people here as the most straightforward and detailed starting point for understanding EUV lithography.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"semiconductor lithography\",\"categories\":[\"Semiconductors & chip manufacturing\",\"Manufacturing & industrial processes\"],\"tags\":[\"euv\",\"asml\",\"chip manufacturing\",\"photolithography\",\"microchips\"],\"quote\":\"I highly recommend watching this video about lithography and the machine that makes it all possible\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"B8Y146v8HxE\",\"title\":\"Clockmaking - How To Make A Clock In The Home Machine Shop - Part 1 - Making The Clock Frames\",\"channel\":\"Clickspring\",\"summary\":\"The first episode of Clickspring's series building a John Wilding skeleton clock from raw stock in a home machine shop. It covers making the frames along with the tools needed for the job.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an absorbing masterpiece of craftsmanship, often raised in threads about watches and clockmaking.\",\"content_type\":\"tutorial\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"clockmaking and machining\",\"categories\":[\"Woodworking, metalworking & workshop skills\",\"Mechanical & precision engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"clickspring\",\"skeleton clock\",\"home machine shop\",\"machining\",\"horology\"],\"quote\":\"It's also a masterpiece, just in a different medium\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"BEG-ly9tQGk\",\"title\":\"Lars Andersen: A new level of archery\",\"channel\":\"larsandersen23\",\"summary\":\"Archer Lars Andersen demonstrates fast, accurate shooting techniques he says he revived from historical art and texts, arguing Hollywood archery is not historical.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an entertaining example of rediscovered lost skills, though some commenters note it is contested.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"historical archery\",\"categories\":[\"Sports & athletics\",\"Magic, puzzles & curiosities\"],\"tags\":[\"archery\",\"lars andersen\",\"forgotten skills\",\"speed shooting\",\"history\"],\"quote\":\"Lars Andersen taking archery to the next level by using forgotten skills he learned by looking at old art and various texts. Worth a watch!\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"ByNs9TG30E8\",\"title\":\"Om Next - David Nolen\",\"channel\":\"ClojureTV\",\"summary\":\"David Nolen presents Om Next, the redesign of the ClojureScript Om library, drawing on Relay, Netflix's JSONGraph and Datomic. He covers the tradeoffs in handling the client-server boundary over immutable data.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as the lead ClojureScript developer's own account of Om's evolution, one-way data flow and single-state design.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"clojurescript ui architecture\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Web development\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"om next\",\"clojurescript\",\"relay\",\"datomic\",\"immutable data\",\"react\"],\"quote\":\"David Nolen does a great job talking about the trade offs and the evolution of everything in this talk\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"CBYhVcO4WgI\",\"title\":\"Lecture 1 - How to Start a Startup (Sam Altman, Dustin Moskovitz)\",\"channel\":\"YC Root Access\",\"summary\":\"The opening lecture of the How to Start a Startup course at Stanford, with Sam Altman covering ideas and products and Dustin Moskovitz discussing why to start a startup.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend the series as timeless startup advice from strong speakers, and cite specific points from Altman and Moskovitz.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"starting a startup\",\"categories\":[\"Startups & entrepreneurship\",\"University lectures & courses\"],\"tags\":[\"y combinator\",\"sam altman\",\"dustin moskovitz\",\"startup ideas\",\"entrepreneurship\"],\"quote\":\"Amazing set of speakers and pretty timeless advice for starting a startup\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"D6EPuUdIC1E\",\"title\":\"Doctor Dissects the Wim Hof Method - Cold Hard Science Analysis\",\"channel\":\"Medlife Crisis\",\"summary\":\"A cardiologist runs a detailed, skeptical review of the Wim Hof Method, covering the research on cold exposure and breathing techniques. It also teaches how to read scientific statistics critically.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points people to it as a nuanced, medically informed take whenever Wim Hof breathing or cold plunges come up.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"wim hof method\",\"categories\":[\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\",\"Medicine & infectious disease\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"wim hof\",\"breathing\",\"cold exposure\",\"medical skepticism\",\"cardiology\"],\"quote\":\"Here is an amazing summary on the research of a sceptical doctor\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"DFFVSvAr7Wc\",\"title\":\"Geoffrey West on COMPLEXITY\",\"channel\":\"FQxI\",\"summary\":\"Physicist Geoffrey West speaks at an FQXi conference on time about complexity and scaling laws. He covers how metabolism, growth and lifespan scale across organisms and how the same mathematics applies to cities and companies.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as an accessible introduction to scaling laws and to why cities keep growing while companies and organisms eventually die.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"scaling laws and complexity\",\"categories\":[\"Fundamental physics\",\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Biology & biotechnology\"],\"tags\":[\"geoffrey west\",\"scaling laws\",\"cities\",\"metabolism\",\"complex systems\"],\"quote\":\"For a great, accessible intro talk on this topic, check out Geoffrey West's\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"DShDaJXK5qo\",\"title\":\"War - Part Two: Anybody's Son Will Do (1 of 6)\",\"channel\":\"Ingsoc75\",\"summary\":\"An episode of Gwynne Dyer's 1983 documentary series War, about how military basic training turns recruits into soldiers. It covers the psychological side of conditioning.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a classic documentary on military training and the psychology of going to war.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"military basic training\",\"categories\":[\"Military history & warfare\",\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Documentaries & storytelling\"],\"tags\":[\"war\",\"gwynne dyer\",\"basic training\",\"military psychology\",\"pbs\"],\"quote\":\"This documentary explains in great detail the nature of military basic training and its psychological component\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"DXvAlwMAxiA\",\"title\":\"How a transistor works\",\"channel\":\"Ben Eater\",\"summary\":\"Ben Eater gives a detailed look at how an NPN bipolar junction transistor works and what it does. It is an eleven-minute explanation aimed at building real understanding of the device.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters prefer it over other transistor explanations as an enlightening starting point that leads into building logic gates and a CPU.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"transistors\",\"categories\":[\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Semiconductors & chip manufacturing\"],\"tags\":[\"transistor\",\"bjt\",\"npn\",\"electronics\",\"ben eater\"],\"quote\":\"I found Ben Eater's videos on how semiconductors work and how transistors work interesting and enlightening.\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"DoeNbZlxfUM\",\"title\":\"DEF CON 18 - Moxie Marlinspike - Changing Threats To Privacy: From TIA to Google\",\"channel\":\"DEFCONConference\",\"summary\":\"Moxie Marlinspike's DEF CON 18 talk argues that privacy threats have shifted from centralized state databases like TIA to corporate data collection by companies like Google, despite wins in strong cryptography and decentralization.\",\"why_hn\":\"Strongly recommended as a formative talk on privacy, especially for its argument that voting with your feet fails as a privacy strategy.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"digital privacy\",\"categories\":[\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"Security & hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"moxie marlinspike\",\"surveillance\",\"defcon\",\"tracking\",\"privacy policy\"],\"quote\":\"It was formative in my understanding of privacy and security.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"E0W1ZZYIV8o\",\"title\":\"A reality check on renewables - David MacKay\",\"channel\":\"TED-Ed\",\"summary\":\"David MacKay's TEDxWarwick talk uses basic arithmetic to show how much land and energy renewables would need to power a country like the UK. It explains the limits of sustainable options and why they should still be pursued.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in energy debates for its back-of-envelope scale of the transition and the land and storage limits of wind and solar.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"renewable energy limits\",\"categories\":[\"Renewables, batteries & storage\",\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\"],\"tags\":[\"david mackay\",\"renewables\",\"energy density\",\"land use\",\"sustainable energy\"],\"quote\":\"I found this ted talk on the feasibility of various renewable energy sources quite eye opening\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"EUXnJraKM3k\",\"title\":\"Microsoft Re-Designs the iPod Packaging\",\"channel\":\"Brant Walsh\",\"summary\":\"A 2006 parody made by a Microsoft team that shows how the company would redesign iPod packaging, with a box piled with feature claims, logos and clutter in contrast to Apple's clean design.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters bring it up whenever Microsoft product naming, branding or marketing is confusing, pointing to it as a long-running self-aware critique.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"product packaging parody\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Product & industrial design\",\"Graphic design & typography\"],\"tags\":[\"microsoft\",\"ipod\",\"branding\",\"design parody\",\"marketing\"],\"quote\":\"brutal but hilarious commentary on Microsoft's inherent dysfunction when it comes to product naming and packaging\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"Ecy1FBdCRbQ\",\"title\":\"[1299] Unpickable Locks From Stuff Made Here\",\"channel\":\"LockPickingLawyer\",\"summary\":\"The Lock Picking Lawyer tests the supposedly unpickable lock designs built by the Stuff Made Here channel. He looks for bypasses and weaknesses in the novel anti-picking mechanisms.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it in lock-security threads as a good example of engineering meeting real-world exploitation, where flaws unrelated to the headline mechanism get found.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"lock security\",\"categories\":[\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"Security & hacking\",\"Magic, puzzles & curiosities\"],\"tags\":[\"lock picking\",\"unpickable lock\",\"bypass\",\"stuff made here\",\"mechanical security\"],\"quote\":\"The interaction of engineering and \\\"use\\\" by the Lock Picking Lawyer\",\"recommenders\":5,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"Eghl19elKk8\",\"title\":\"Timothy Snyder Speaks, ep. 14: Politics of Eternity, Politics of Inevitability\",\"channel\":\"Timothy Snyder\",\"summary\":\"Historian Timothy Snyder explains the politics of inevitability and the politics of eternity, two ways of thinking about time that frame his books On Tyranny and The Road to Unfreedom.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a short, clear introduction to these two concepts for making sense of current politics.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"politics of time\",\"categories\":[\"World history\",\"US politics & elections\"],\"tags\":[\"timothy snyder\",\"inevitability\",\"eternity\",\"history\",\"authoritarianism\"],\"quote\":\"You will love this video (12m)\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"FBaVwwuErmU\",\"title\":\"Look Around You: Season 1 Pilot - Calcium\",\"channel\":\"DocShadow660\",\"summary\":\"The pilot of the British comedy series Look Around You, a deadpan parody of 1970s and 80s school science programmes, in which the scientists examine calcium.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a surreal, perfectly observed parody of old educational films, and quote it in jokes.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"british parody comedy\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Stand-up & sketch comedy\"],\"tags\":[\"look around you\",\"parody\",\"educational films\",\"british tv\",\"deadpan\"],\"quote\":\"a lot of HN readers might love it. It's a surreal but perfectly observed parody\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"FTKP0Y9MVus\",\"title\":\"CD / Color\",\"channel\":\"Captain Disillusion\",\"summary\":\"Captain Disillusion's comedic, fast-paced explainer on how color works in cameras, displays and video. It covers the biology of color perception and what it means for screens.\",\"why_hn\":\"Commenters recommend it as a dense but entertaining primer on color theory and color in video systems.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"color theory in video\",\"categories\":[\"Animation & visual effects\",\"Audio & video engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"color perception\",\"video\",\"displays\",\"captain disillusion\",\"visual effects\"],\"quote\":\"I went looking for an understanding of how color works in video systems, and ran across this gem from Captain Disillusion\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"G-WO-z-QuWI\",\"title\":\"How To Terraform Venus (Quickly)\",\"channel\":\"Kurzgesagt – In a Nutshell\",\"summary\":\"Kurzgesagt lays out a speculative plan to terraform Venus, covering its hostile atmosphere and how it might be made Earth-like, with a sources page linked.\",\"why_hn\":\"Offered as a readable explainer whenever Venus terraforming or alternatives to Mars colonization come up.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"terraforming venus\",\"categories\":[\"Space exploration & spaceflight\",\"Cosmology & astrophysics\"],\"tags\":[\"venus\",\"terraforming\",\"space colonization\",\"kurzgesagt\",\"planetary science\"],\"quote\":\"There is a decent kurzgesagt video explaining what transforming a new planet would look like\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"G0ZZJXw4MTA\",\"title\":\"Leading Questions - Yes Prime Minister\",\"channel\":\"LimeXd\",\"summary\":\"A clip from Yes Prime Minister in which Sir Humphrey shows how leading questions can make a survey support or oppose national service. It is a short satirical scene.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters bring it up whenever a poll or survey result looks suspect, as the classic illustration of biased question framing.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"leading questions in surveys\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Film & TV clips\",\"Data science & statistics\"],\"tags\":[\"yes prime minister\",\"polling\",\"survey bias\",\"satire\",\"bbc\"],\"quote\":\"An excellent illustration of this\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"GtQdIYUtAHg\",\"title\":\"Bytebeat: Experimental music from very short C programs\",\"channel\":\"Ville-Matias Heikkilä (viznut)\",\"summary\":\"Viznut demonstrates bytebeat, experimental music generated by extremely short C programs. It is the first of a series of videos on the technique.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN calls it the video that started the one-line-code music trend and recommends exploring the demoscene author's channel.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"bytebeat generative music\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"Music theory, synthesis & production\",\"Creative coding & generative art\"],\"tags\":[\"bytebeat\",\"viznut\",\"demoscene\",\"algorithmic music\",\"c programming\"],\"quote\":\"The video that started it all\",\"recommenders\":4,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"H6ZFzEW7_Q4\",\"title\":\"Homeless man talks openly about being addicted to heroin. We have an opioid crisis in America.\",\"channel\":\"Invisible People\",\"summary\":\"A short street interview from Invisible People with Keith, a homeless man in Los Angeles who lost his housing-industry job in the crash and moved from painkillers to heroin because it was cheaper. He talks candidly about the grip of addiction.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it in threads on homelessness and drug policy as a short, honest firsthand account from an articulate addict.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"heroin addiction and homelessness\",\"categories\":[\"Social issues & culture\",\"Mental health & neurodiversity\"],\"tags\":[\"addiction\",\"heroin\",\"homelessness\",\"opioids\",\"firsthand account\"],\"quote\":\"Extremely powerful 5 minute interview with an obviously intelligent homeless person addicted to heroin: Terrifying\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"HMBl0ttu-Ow\",\"title\":\"Social Physics: How Good Ideas Spread | Sandy Pentland | Talks at Google\",\"channel\":\"Talks at Google\",\"summary\":\"Sandy Pentland of MIT presents his book on social physics, which uses big-data studies of how people interact to explain how ideas spread. He describes findings on collective intelligence and social influence.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it for the finding that idea exchange and connectivity drive productivity and wealth, which they apply to cities, offices and online communities.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"social physics\",\"categories\":[\"Psychology & human behavior\",\"Data science & statistics\",\"Ideas & learning (other)\"],\"tags\":[\"social physics\",\"idea flow\",\"collective intelligence\",\"cities\",\"big data\"],\"quote\":\"There's a great Authors at Google talk by Sandy Pentland\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"HPqGaIMVuLs\",\"title\":\"Surface-Stable Fractal Dithering Explained\",\"channel\":\"runevision\",\"summary\":\"Rune Skovbo Johansen explains Surface-Stable Fractal Dithering, a technique he invented and released as open source. It makes dithering stick to 3D surfaces and covers the experiments and shortcomings along the way, inspired by Return of the Obra Dinn.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the original explainer for a clever dithering technique and the go-to video in dithering threads.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"surface-stable dithering\",\"categories\":[\"Computer graphics & rendering\",\"Creative coding & generative art\",\"Game programming & engines\"],\"tags\":[\"dithering\",\"shaders\",\"computer graphics\",\"obra dinn\",\"fractals\"],\"quote\":\"a 4-days old video related to dithering which is really worth watching.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"HSxSgbNQi-g\",\"title\":\"Small Planes Over Big Oceans (ETOPS Explained)\",\"channel\":\"Wendover Productions\",\"summary\":\"A short Wendover Productions explainer on ETOPS, the rules that let twin-engine jets fly long routes over oceans. It covers the economics that favored twinjets like the 787 over