it to butyric acid and Hershey's history, and why familiarity makes Americans love it. He compares it with British Cadbury.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a fascinating explanation of how taste is cultural and why American chocolate tastes distinctive.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"food science\",\"categories\":[\"Cooking & food\",\"Chemistry\"],\"tags\":[\"hershey\",\"chocolate\",\"butyric acid\",\"cadbury\",\"taste\",\"adam ragusea\"],\"quote\":\"Adam Ragusea has an excellent video on this very topic\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"JRuNA2eK7w0\",\"title\":\"Is AI Safety a Pascal's Mugging?\",\"channel\":\"Robert Miles AI Safety \",\"summary\":\"Robert Miles examines whether the case for AI safety is a Pascal's mugging, a tiny probability of an enormous payoff. He argues it is not, because AI risk does not depend on vanishingly small probabilities.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a reasoned rebuttal to the claim that AI risk is just Pascal's wager.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"ai safety\",\"categories\":[\"AI safety, AGI & impact\",\"Probability & game theory\"],\"tags\":[\"pascal's mugging\",\"ai risk\",\"robert miles\",\"decision theory\",\"existential risk\"],\"quote\":\"the main point of the video is that kind of situation happens when the probability involved is vanishingly small\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":4.0},{\"vid\":\"Jd3-eiid-Uw\",\"title\":\"Head Tracking for Desktop VR Displays using the WiiRemote\",\"channel\":\"Johnny Lee\",\"summary\":\"Johnny Lee demonstrates head tracking with a Wii remote and a head-mounted pair of IR LEDs, so a screen acts like a window into a 3D scene as your head moves.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the classic reference for head-tracked parallax displays, cited whenever new 3D or holographic display tech appears.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"head-tracked 3d display\",\"categories\":[